// Generated by ./build-allspeak — do not edit.
// The editor and its two modules, as one script a page can load across origins.
window.AllSpeak_EditorParts = {"asedit.allspeak":"!!  Tabbed script editor with auto-save and external change detection.\n!!  Served by server.allspeak locally, or any web server with /list, /read, /write routes.\n!   asedit.allspeak\n\n    script ASEditor\n!! @hash 57eacdb1\n!! @verified 57eacdb1\n!!!\n!! Variable declarations\n!! The script uses a lot of variables; they are grouped here by function.\n!   -- DOM --\n    div Body\n    div TabBar\n    div TabBtn\n    div Overlay\n    div Scroller\n    div FileRow\n    div EditorArea       ! flat-mode pane (CodeMirror-wrapped textarea)\n    div BlocksArea       ! Blocks-mode pane (toolbar + split)\n    textarea ContentEditor\n    textarea BlocksCodePane\n    textarea BlocksDocPane\n    span StatusSpan\n    span TabLabel\n    span BlocksTitle\n    span BlocksBadge\n    button OpenBtn\n    button FindBtn\n    button BlocksBtn\n    button GraphBtn\n    button RecordBtn     ! record the open script and put the recording beside it\n    button LaunchBtn     ! open the page this script's `@app` names, in a window of its own\n    button BlocksPrev\n    button BlocksNext\n    button BlocksVerify\n    button BlocksVerifyAll\n    button CloseBtn\n    img PlusBtn\n    img TabClose\n    a FileName\n    pre UIPre\n\n!   -- State --\n    variable Lang\n    variable StrOpen\n    variable StrFind\n    variable StrFlowCall\n    variable StrFlowJump\n    variable StrFlowReturn\n    variable StrClose\n    variable StrSaved\n    variable StrSaveFailed\n    variable StrRecording\n    variable StrReloaded\n    variable StrExtChange\n    variable StrNoConnect\n    variable StrSaveAs\n    variable StrEmpty\n    variable StrUpLevel\n    variable StrRoot\n    variable StrUpdated\n    variable StrRestart\n    variable TabPath       ! array: full file path per tab (relative to project root)\n    variable TabName       ! array: display name (basename) per tab\n    variable TabSaved      ! array: last content saved to server per tab\n    ! Where the flat editor was standing, per tab: the caret's line and the viewport's scroll offset.\n    ! Two values and not one, because they are independent — scrolling does not move the insertion\n    ! point, and coming back must not carry the view to the caret. A video editor teaches you to\n    ! expect that; a text editor usually does not, and the jump is what catches you out.\n    variable TabCursor     ! array: the caret's line, per tab\n    variable TabView       ! array: the viewport's scroll offset, per tab\n    variable TabCount\n    variable ActiveTab\n    variable Content       ! scratch: current editor content\n    variable FileContent   ! scratch: content fetched from server\n    variable FileList      ! scratch: JSON array from /list\n    variable JsonLines     ! scratch: a JSON file's text split into lines\n    variable JsonText      ! scratch: text on its way to the JSON formatter\n    variable N\n    variable M\n    variable Item          ! scratch: single entry object from list\n    variable File          ! scratch: single filename\n    variable FileCount\n    variable TabItem\n    variable UIWebson\n    variable VersionResult\n    variable CurrentPath   ! current directory being browsed (relative, no leading /)\n    variable EntryName\n    variable EntryType\n\n!   -- Blocks mode (parser + view + edit) --\n    variable BlocksMode    ! 0 = flat, 1 = Blocks pane visible\n    div GraphArea        ! the Graph pane's whole area — the drawing, the grip and the sidebar — shown and hidden here\n    div GraphGrip        ! the draggable splitter between the drawing and the sidebar\n    div GraphSide        ! the sidebar's box, which is the module's; this file only sizes it\n    variable GripPicked    ! 1 while the drag in progress began on the Graph grip\n    variable GripWidthAtPick ! the sidebar's width when that drag began\n    variable GripMost      ! the widest the sidebar may get, which depends on the window\n    variable SideStored    ! the sidebar width kept from a previous session\n    variable GraphMode     ! 0 = not graphed, 1 = Graph pane visible\n    variable TracePath     ! the recording this tab expects, sent to the pane so a miss can name it\n    module VizModule       ! the Graph pane, a companion module fetched and run on demand\n    variable VizSrc         ! its source, as fetched — `run` compiles the text\n    variable VizLoaded      ! 1 once it is running, so the fetch happens once\n    module SideModule      ! the Graph sidebar, a second companion module beside the pane\n    variable SideSrc        ! its source, as fetched\n    variable SideLoaded     ! 1 once it is running\n    variable SideIn         ! the dict the pane reported, as it arrived\n    variable SideSel        ! the line the pane last reported, which is what the sidebar is about\n    variable SideVisit      ! which arrival on that line the clicked dot was\n    variable SideValues     ! what that arrival captured, passed on untouched\n    variable SideTotal      ! how many arrivals that line had in the whole recording\n    variable SideMsg        ! the dict sent to the sidebar: which kind of news, and what it is\n    variable SideFound      ! 1 if a doc block holds that line\n    variable SideTitle      ! the block's first prose line, for the sidebar's heading\n    variable SideProse      ! its prose\n    variable SideI          ! scratch: index over the sections looking for one\n    variable VizRun         ! the dict the pane is sent: the run, the script, the words, the path\n    variable RecordSource   ! the open buffer, handed to the plugin as the script to run\n    variable RecordTrace     ! what that run collected, as the trace document's text\n    variable RecordVerdict   ! what the recording amounts to — visits and windows, or why there is none\n    variable AppPage         ! the page this script's `@app` attribute names, if it has one\n    variable TabFlavour      ! this tab's script: `py` or `js`, as the plugin resolved it — the script's own\n                             ! marker, then the project's `#editor-runtime`, then `js`\n    variable RecordReply     ! what the server says after it ran a Python script and recorded the run\n    variable TraceText      ! the recording, as read — the `trace` field of that dict\n    variable SourceText     ! the script, as read — the `source` field of it\n    variable CurBlock      ! 0-based index into Sections arrays\n    variable BlockDirty    ! 1 if user edited the current block since last splice\n    variable SecCount      ! number of parsed sections\n    variable SecStart      ! array: 1-based opener line number\n    variable SecEnd        ! array: 1-based terminator line number\n    variable SecProse      ! array: prose text (one entry, joined by newline, !! prefix stripped)\n    variable SecCode       ! array: code text (joined by newline, verbatim)\n    variable SecHash       ! array: stored @hash, or empty\n    variable SecVerified   ! array: stored @verified, or empty\n    variable SecHashState  ! array: fresh / stale / no-baseline / no-code\n    variable SecVerifyState ! array: verified-fresh / verified-stale / unverified / verified-no-code\n    variable Found         ! 1 if the search term was found in section I\n    variable FoundPane     ! `code` or `doc` — pane that holds the match\n    variable SearchTerm    ! the text being searched for (survives RenderBlock/RenderToc, which reuse Tmp)\n\n!   -- Parser scratch (rebuilt every parse) --\n    variable Lines         ! source split on newline\n    variable LineCount\n    variable Line\n    variable Kind          ! `TERM`, `DOC`, `META`, `CODE`\n    variable LContent      ! body of a DOC/META line\n    variable MetaKey\n    variable MetaValue\n    variable Pos\n    variable Tmp\n    variable Tip              ! a sidebar row's tooltip, decided with its colour\n    variable InSection\n    variable SecHasCode\n    variable SecStartTmp\n    variable SecCodeTmp\n    variable SecProseTmp\n    variable SecHashTmp\n    variable SecVerifiedTmp\n    variable CurHash\n    variable HashState\n    variable VerifyState\n    variable I\n    variable Source        ! whole source text being parsed\n    variable Outside       ! array: text between sections (length SecCount + 1)\n    variable OutsideTmp    ! accumulator for the current outside chunk\n    variable ProseSrc      ! scratch: prose text being split into lines\n    variable PCount        ! scratch: count of prose lines\n    variable PJ            ! scratch: index over prose lines\n    variable PL            ! scratch: one prose line\n    variable BlkText       ! scratch: assembled section text\n    variable NewProse      ! scratch: edited prose pulled from BlocksDocPane\n    variable NewCode       ! scratch: edited code pulled from BlocksCodePane\n    variable OldProse      ! scratch: previously stored prose for this section\n    variable OldCode       ! scratch: previously stored code for this section\n\n!   -- Blocks: TOC + draggable divider --\n    div BlocksToc          ! left sidebar listing all blocks\n    div BlocksDivider      ! draggable splitter between code and doc panes\n    div TocRow             ! one row per block in the TOC\n    span TocLabel          ! the clickable label inside each TocRow\n    variable PickPos       ! pick coords at mousedown on divider\n    variable DragPos       ! drag coords during mousemove\n    variable PickX\n    variable DragX\n    variable CodeWidthAtPick\n    variable NewWidth\n    variable StoredWidth\n    variable FinalWidth\n    variable FirstLine     ! first prose line of a block (for TOC label)\n    variable From          ! first line of a block, for the review-signal test\n    variable To            ! and its last line\n    variable BlockAfter    ! index of the block after this one, which bounds it\n    variable FromCheck     ! the two bounds moved out by one, so both tests can be positive\n    variable ToCheck\n    variable FlagCount     ! lines the plugin says nothing enters, over all blocks\n    variable FlagLine      ! array: those lines\n    variable K             ! walk index over the records, and over the flagged lines\n    variable MarkRecord    ! one model record, as the walk reaches it\n    variable MarkCount     ! how many records the plugin returned\n    variable MarkLine      ! one flagged line, as the walk reaches it\n    variable MarkRest      ! scratch: the record after `line=`\n    variable MarkSpace     ! scratch: where its number ends\n    variable MarkDigits    ! scratch: the number itself\n    variable EditorPath    ! the current tab's path, handed to the plugin as its target\n    variable Model         ! array: what the plugin returned\n    variable NL\n    variable J\n!! @hash 649556b5\n!! @verified 90e6973b\n!!!\n!! The UI is described by a DOM element 'asedit-ui',\n!! a <pre> element that contains all the DOM elements that go to make up the editor.\n!! These are formatted using Webson, a JSON representation of any number of DOM elements,\n!! which is rendered into an existing element using the 'render' command\n!! 'body' without a capital, is the HTML <body> element\n!! 'Body', where the first letter is a capital, is a variable that is attached to the page body\n!! so that other elements - in this case those represented by the Webson -  can be added to it.\n!   -- Build UI --\n    attach Body to body\n    attach UIPre to `asedit-ui`\n    put the content of UIPre into UIWebson\n    render UIWebson in Body\n    attach TabBar to `se-tabbar`\n    attach ContentEditor to `se-textarea`\n    attach StatusSpan to `se-status`\n    attach OpenBtn to `se-open`\n    attach FindBtn to `se-findbtn`\n    attach PlusBtn to `se-plusbtn`\n    attach Overlay to `se-overlay`\n    attach Scroller to `se-scroller`\n    attach CloseBtn to `se-closebtn`\n    attach BlocksBtn to `se-blocksbtn`\n    attach GraphBtn to `se-graphbtn`\n    attach RecordBtn to `se-recordbtn`\n    attach LaunchBtn to `se-launchbtn`\n    attach GraphArea to `se-graph-area`\n    attach GraphGrip to `se-graph-grip`\n    attach GraphSide to `se-graph-side`\n    attach EditorArea to `se-editor-area`\n    attach BlocksArea to `se-blocks-area`\n    attach BlocksTitle to `se-blocks-title`\n    attach BlocksPrev to `se-blocks-prev`\n    attach BlocksNext to `se-blocks-next`\n    attach BlocksBadge to `se-blocks-badge`\n    attach BlocksVerify to `se-blocks-verify`\n    attach BlocksVerifyAll to `se-blocks-verify-all`\n    attach BlocksCodePane to `se-blocks-code`\n    attach BlocksDocPane to `se-blocks-doc`\n    attach BlocksToc to `se-blocks-toc`\n    attach BlocksDivider to `se-blocks-divider`\n!! @hash 414ea5b9\n!! @verified 414ea5b9\n!!!\n!! Some general initialisation\n!   -- CodeMirror --\n    codemirror attach to ContentEditor\n\n    put 0 into TabCount\n    put 0 into ActiveTab\n    put `` into CurrentPath\n!! @hash 3a601096\n!! @verified 3a601096\n!!!\n!! Language detection\n!! The command 'language xx' switches the system to the given language (fr, de, it etc.)\n!! and defaults to 'en'. All of the language-specific strings are then defined for each language.\n!   -- Detect language and set strings for each supported language --\n    div LangDiv\n    ! **Textareas, and the reason is in how `the text of` reads an element.** The branches switch on the\n    ! *symbol's* type: a `div` reads `innerHTML` with every newline removed, which turns a 2,700-line module\n    ! into one line — and its first line is a `!!` comment, which then comments out the lot. Measured\n    ! 2026-10-04: the pane compiled as `1 lines (0 tokens)` and nothing was ever drawn. A `pre` would keep the\n    ! newlines but hands back *escaped* markup, and the module has 25 `<` in it. A textarea's `value` is the\n    ! text and only the text.\n    textarea VizSrcDiv\n    textarea SideSrcDiv\n    put `en` into Lang\n    attach LangDiv to `editor-lang` or go to SetStrings\n    put the text of LangDiv into Lang\n    replace ` ` with `` in Lang\n    replace newline with `` in Lang\n    if Lang is empty put `en` into Lang\n\nSetStrings:\n    if Lang is `it`\n    begin\n        put `Apri` into StrOpen\n        put `Cerca` into StrFind\n        put `Chiudi` into StrClose\n        put `Salvato` into StrSaved\n        put `Salvataggio fallito` into StrSaveFailed\n        put `Registrazione dell'app` into StrRecording\n        put `Ricaricato` into StrReloaded\n        put `Modifica esterna rilevata` into StrExtChange\n        put `Impossibile connettersi al server` into StrNoConnect\n        put `Salva come (nome file senza .allspeak):` into StrSaveAs\n        put `(cartella vuota)` into StrEmpty\n        put `.. (livello superiore)` into StrUpLevel\n        put `/ (radice progetto)` into StrRoot\n        put `L'editor è stato aggiornato. Riavviare?` into StrUpdated\n        put `Riavvia il server manualmente.` into StrRestart\n        put `vaisub` into StrFlowCall\n        put `vai` into StrFlowJump\n        put `ritorna` into StrFlowReturn\n    end\n    else if Lang is `fr`\n    begin\n        put `Ouvrir` into StrOpen\n        put `Rechercher` into StrFind\n        put `Fermer` into StrClose\n        put `Enregistré` into StrSaved\n        put `Échec de l'enregistrement` into StrSaveFailed\n        put `Enregistrement de l'app` into StrRecording\n        put `Rechargé` into StrReloaded\n        put `Modification externe détectée` into StrExtChange\n        put `Impossible de se connecter au serveur` into StrNoConnect\n        put `Enregistrer sous (nom de fichier sans .allspeak) :` into StrSaveAs\n        put `(dossier vide)` into StrEmpty\n        put `.. (niveau supérieur)` into StrUpLevel\n        put `/ (racine du projet)` into StrRoot\n        put `L'éditeur a été mis à jour. Redémarrer ?` into StrUpdated\n        put `Veuillez redémarrer le serveur manuellement.` into StrRestart\n        put `vasous` into StrFlowCall\n        put `va` into StrFlowJump\n        put `retourne` into StrFlowReturn\n    end\n    else if Lang is `de`\n    begin\n        put `Öffnen` into StrOpen\n        put `Suchen` into StrFind\n        put `Schließen` into StrClose\n        put `Gespeichert` into StrSaved\n        put `Speichern fehlgeschlagen` into StrSaveFailed\n        put `Aufzeichnung der App` into StrRecording\n        put `Neu geladen` into StrReloaded\n        put `Externe Änderung erkannt` into StrExtChange\n        put `Verbindung zum Server nicht möglich` into StrNoConnect\n        put `Speichern unter (Dateiname ohne .allspeak):` into StrSaveAs\n        put `(leerer Ordner)` into StrEmpty\n        put `.. (Ebene höher)` into StrUpLevel\n        put `/ (Projektstamm)` into StrRoot\n        put `Der Editor wurde aktualisiert. Neu starten?` into StrUpdated\n        put `Bitte den Server manuell neu starten.` into StrRestart\n        put `gosub` into StrFlowCall\n        put `gehe` into StrFlowJump\n        put `retourniere` into StrFlowReturn\n    end\n    else\n    begin\n        put `Open` into StrOpen\n        put `Find` into StrFind\n        put `Close` into StrClose\n        put `Saved` into StrSaved\n        put `Save failed` into StrSaveFailed\n        put `Recording the app` into StrRecording\n        put `Reloaded` into StrReloaded\n        put `External change detected` into StrExtChange\n        put `Cannot connect to server` into StrNoConnect\n        put `Save as (filename without .allspeak):` into StrSaveAs\n        put `(empty directory)` into StrEmpty\n        put `.. (up one level)` into StrUpLevel\n        put `/ (project root)` into StrRoot\n        put `asedit has been updated. Restart to apply changes?` into StrUpdated\n        put `Please restart the server manually.` into StrRestart\n        put `gosub` into StrFlowCall\n        put `go` into StrFlowJump\n        put `return` into StrFlowReturn\n    end\n    set the content of OpenBtn to StrOpen\n    set the content of FindBtn to StrFind\n    set the content of CloseBtn to StrClose\n!! @hash 3f450398\n!! @verified 3f450398\n!!!\n!! The editor checks on startup and periodically for updates to itself.\n!! (explain)\n!! then \n!   -- Check for updates --\n    rest get VersionResult from `/version` or go VersionDone\n    if VersionResult is `updated`\n    begin\n        if confirm StrUpdated\n        begin\n            rest get VersionResult from `/restart` or begin\n                alert StrRestart\n                go VersionDone\n            end\n            wait 3 seconds\n            location the location\n\n!\t\t\t Is this alternative valid?\n!            rest get VersionResult from `/restart`\n!            on failure alert StrRestart\n!           \telse\n!            begin\n!            \twait 3 seconds\n!            \tlocation the location\n!            end\n        end\n    end\nVersionDone:\n!! @hash 96465c41\n!! @verified 96465c41\n!!!\n!! Set up click handlers for the various editor functions.\n!! Blocks-mode handlers (toggle, navigate, mark verified) live alongside\n!! the existing flat-mode handlers. The pane editor textareas have\n!! keyboard input handlers wired in EnterBlocks so we can stop watching\n!! them when the pane is hidden.\n!   -- Handlers --\n    on click OpenBtn go to ShowBrowser\n    on click PlusBtn go to NewFile\n    on click FindBtn go to DoFind\n    on click BlocksBtn go to ToggleBlocks\n    on click GraphBtn go to ToggleGraph\n    on click RecordBtn go to RecordRun\n    on click LaunchBtn go to LaunchApp\n    on click BlocksPrev go to PrevBlock\n    on click BlocksNext go to NextBlock\n    on click BlocksVerify go to MarkVerified\n    on click BlocksVerifyAll go to MarkAllVerified\n\n    on pick BlocksDivider\n    begin\n        ! Two splitters and one document-level drag, so the flag says which of them was taken hold of —\n        ! the drag and the drop then know which they are moving without having to ask the pointer.\n        put 0 into GripPicked\n        put the pick position into PickPos\n        put property `x` of PickPos into PickX\n        put the width of BlocksCodePane into CodeWidthAtPick\n    end\n    on pick GraphGrip\n    begin\n        put 1 into GripPicked\n        put the pick position into PickPos\n        put property `x` of PickPos into PickX\n        put the width of GraphSide into GripWidthAtPick\n    end\n    on drag\n    begin\n        put the drag position into DragPos\n        ! **The Graph grip, which is the one drag that belongs to Graph mode.** It moves the sidebar's\n        ! *left* edge, so a pointer travelling left makes the panel wider — the opposite sense to the\n        ! Blocks splitter, whose right edge the pointer carries. The clamps are a floor and a ceiling: at\n        ! the floor the prose has no width left to be read in, and at the ceiling the drawing is the thing\n        ! being squeezed, which is the pane's whole purpose. The ceiling is the panel's own width less a\n        ! floor for the picture, so a narrow window cannot let the sidebar eat the run.\n        if GripPicked is 1\n        begin\n            put property `x` of DragPos into DragX\n            take DragX from PickX giving NewWidth\n            ! **The width kept at the pick is added *to* the travel, and the destination is the travel.**\n            ! Written the other way round — `add NewWidth to GripWidthAtPick` — this compiles and runs, and\n            ! is wrong twice over: the sum lands in the width kept at the pick, so the travel is left as it\n            ! was and every drag clamps, and the starting width grows by the travel on *every* pointer move,\n            ! so a drag that went on long enough would accelerate. The two idioms differ by which operand is\n            ! the destination, and both look reasonable; the harness's grip checks exist for this line.\n            add GripWidthAtPick to NewWidth\n            put 700 into GripMost\n            put the width of GraphArea into Tmp\n            take 320 from Tmp\n            if Tmp is less than GripMost put Tmp into GripMost\n            if GripMost is less than 200 put 200 into GripMost\n            if NewWidth is less than 200 put 200 into NewWidth\n            if NewWidth is greater than GripMost put GripMost into NewWidth\n            set style `flex` of GraphSide to `0 0 ` cat NewWidth cat `px`\n            return\n        end\n        ! A drag in Graph mode belongs to the picture, and the pane's own handler is registered on the\n        ! document too — so this one has nothing to do while that pane is up, and says so by doing\n        ! nothing rather than by asking the pane whether it wants it.\n        if GraphMode is 0\n        begin\n            put property `x` of DragPos into DragX\n            put DragX into NewWidth\n            take PickX from NewWidth\n            add CodeWidthAtPick to NewWidth\n            if NewWidth is less than 100 put 100 into NewWidth\n            set style `flex` of BlocksCodePane to `0 0 ` cat NewWidth cat `px`\n        end\n    end\n    on drop\n    begin\n        ! **The grip answers first**, because it is the one that can be held while Graph mode is up — the\n        ! Blocks splitter's own guard below refuses to do anything there and would otherwise swallow this\n        ! drop, losing the width the reader just chose.\n        !\n        ! Nothing is redrawn on the way out, and that is deliberate rather than an omission: the pane\n        ! measures its own box on every gesture (`VizPixelScale` is called from the pick), so the picture\n        ! needs no telling that its box has changed, and a redraw here would re-fit the drawing under the\n        ! reader's hand for nothing.\n        if GripPicked is 1\n        begin\n            put 0 into GripPicked\n            put the width of GraphSide into FinalWidth\n            put FinalWidth into storage as `asedit-graph-side-width`\n            return\n        end\n        if GraphMode is 1 return\n        put the width of BlocksCodePane into FinalWidth\n        put FinalWidth into storage as `asedit-blocks-code-width`\n    end\n\n    put 0 into BlocksMode\n    put 0 into GraphMode\n    put 0 into BlockDirty\n!! @hash 3efa1530\n!! @verified 3efa1530\n!!!\n!! One `on message`, registered here rather than with the code it runs.\n!!\n!! **A handler is registered by *executing* the line, and this file stops in the block below.** Everything after `fork to AutoSave` is a subroutine, so a registration written beside its own handler at the end of the file would never run: the pane's report would arrive at a program with no handler and be dropped in silence. The rule that falls out of it is worth keeping — in this editor a handler goes where the flow reaches it, and only its *body* may live at the end.\n!!\n!! It is named here with its body for that reason: the handler is `SideMarkLine`, in the Graph sidebar section, and the two are one thing split by the shape of the file rather than by design.\non message go to SideMarkLine\n!! @hash f327f0fa\n!! @verified f327f0fa\n!!!\n!! While editor is running it periodically saves changes made by the user\n!! and updates the display to show updates made externally.\n!! This avoids the need for any Save mechanism.\n!   -- Start background loops --\n    fork to AutoSave\n    fork to PollFile\n    stop\n!! @hash f86feb98\n!! @verified f86feb98\n!!!\n!! Every half-second, changes to the content are saved.\n!!\n!! TabSaved is an array of tab contents. If its content differs from that currently held in the editor, the current content is saved to the file and TabSaved is updated.\n!   -- Auto-save loop --\nAutoSave:\n    wait 500 millis\n    if TabCount is 0\n    begin\n        fork to AutoSave\n        stop\n    end\n    codemirror get content of ContentEditor into Content\n    index TabSaved to ActiveTab\n    if Content is not TabSaved\n    begin\n        index TabPath to ActiveTab\n        if TabPath is empty\n        begin\n            if Content is empty\n            begin\n                fork to AutoSave\n                stop\n            end\n            put prompt StrSaveAs into File\n            if File is empty\n            begin\n                fork to AutoSave\n                stop\n            end\n            put File cat `.allspeak` into File\n            ! Save into the currently browsed directory\n            if CurrentPath is not empty\n                put CurrentPath cat `/` cat File into File\n            index TabPath to ActiveTab\n            put File into TabPath\n            index TabName to ActiveTab\n            put File into TabName\n            gosub to RebuildTabBar\n        end\n        rest post Content to `/write/` cat TabPath or go SaveFailed\n        index TabSaved to ActiveTab\n        put Content into TabSaved\n        set the content of StatusSpan to StrSaved\n        fork to ClearStatus\n    end\n    fork to AutoSave\n    stop\n\nSaveFailed:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to StrSaveFailed\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    fork to AutoSave\n    stop\n!! @hash 63538f67\n!! @verified 63538f67\n!!!\n!! Every 3 seconds, the current file is re-read into FileContent.\n!!\n!! If this differs from what is currently held in TabSaved for this tab, the editor content is updated and a message is displayed briefly.\n!   -- External change polling --\nPollFile:\n    wait 3 seconds\n    if TabCount is 0\n    begin\n        fork to PollFile\n        stop\n    end\n    ! A mode that owns the screen must not have its content reloaded underneath it. In Blocks mode the\n    ! in-memory Sections array is the source of truth and a silent reload would clobber the user's pane\n    ! edits; in Graph mode the poll replaced the editor's content every three seconds while the graph was\n    ! up, which is when the drawing was seen to be overwritten. Either way, skip until the pane is left.\n    if BlocksMode is 1 or GraphMode is 1 go to PollNext\n    index TabPath to ActiveTab\n    if TabPath is empty go to PollNext\n    rest get FileContent from `/read/` cat TabPath or go PollNext\n    ! Formatted as it is read, exactly as the loader formats it. Without this the poll compares a\n    ! one-line file on disk against the formatted text in the tab, sees a difference on every pass,\n    ! and reloads the file over the formatted text every three seconds — which is what \"it formats\n    ! it, then after a couple of seconds reverts to a one-liner\" turned out to be. Like has to be\n    ! compared with like for the poll to settle.\n    if TabPath ends with `.json`\n    begin\n        put FileContent into JsonText\n        gosub to FormatJson\n        put JsonText into FileContent\n    end\n    codemirror get content of ContentEditor into Content\n    index TabSaved to ActiveTab\n    if FileContent is not TabSaved\n    begin\n        if Content is TabSaved\n        begin\n            ! Editor is clean -- silently reload\n            put FileContent into TabSaved\n            codemirror set content of ContentEditor to FileContent\n            set the content of StatusSpan to StrReloaded\n            fork to ClearStatus\n        end\n        else\n        begin\n            set the content of StatusSpan to StrExtChange\n        end\n    end\nPollNext:\n    fork to PollFile\n    stop\n!! @hash bbd67e70\n!! @verified bbd67e70\n!!!\n!! File browser overlay behind the Open button: lists the current directory, navigates into folders and back to the project root, and hands the chosen file to the tab-management section to open.\n!   -- File browser with directory navigation --\nShowBrowser:\n    ! Opening a tab while Blocks mode is up leaves the panes out of sync with\n    ! the active tab's source; switch back to flat mode first.\n    if BlocksMode is 1 gosub to DoExitBlocks\n    rest get FileList from `/list/` cat CurrentPath or go BrowserError\n    put the json count of FileList into FileCount\n    set the elements of FileRow to FileCount\n    set the elements of FileName to FileCount\n    set the content of Scroller to ``\n\n    ! Show current path at top\n    create FileRow in Scroller\n    set the style of FileRow to `padding:4px 8px;font-size:0.85em;color:#666;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd`\n    if CurrentPath is empty\n        set the content of FileRow to StrRoot\n    else\n        set the content of FileRow to `/` cat CurrentPath\n\n    ! Show \"back\" row if inside a subdirectory\n    if CurrentPath is not empty\n    begin\n        create FileRow in Scroller\n        set the style of FileRow to `padding:4px 8px;cursor:pointer;background:#f0f0ff`\n        set the content of FileRow to StrUpLevel\n        on click FileRow go to GoUp\n    end\n\n    if FileCount is 0\n    begin\n        create FileRow in Scroller\n        set the style of FileRow to `padding:4px 8px;color:#999`\n        set the content of FileRow to StrEmpty\n    end\n\n    put 0 into N\n    while N is less than FileCount\n    begin\n        put element N of FileList into Item\n        put property `name` of Item into EntryName\n        put property `type` of Item into EntryType\n        index FileRow to N\n        create FileRow in Scroller\n        set the style of FileRow to `padding:3px 8px;cursor:pointer`\n        index FileName to N\n        create FileName in FileRow\n        if EntryType is `dir`\n        begin\n            set the style of FileName to `display:block;padding:3px 0;cursor:pointer;font-weight:bold`\n            set the content of FileName to `📁 ` cat EntryName\n        end\n        else\n        begin\n            set the style of FileName to `display:block;padding:3px 0;cursor:pointer`\n            set the content of FileName to EntryName\n        end\n        on click FileName go to SelectEntry\n        add 1 to N\n    end\n    on click CloseBtn go to CloseBrowser\n    set style `display` of Overlay to `block`\n    stop\n\nGoUp:\n    put the position of the last `/` in CurrentPath into N\n    if N is less than 0\n        put `` into CurrentPath\n    else\n        put left N of CurrentPath into CurrentPath\n    go to ShowBrowser\n\nBrowserError:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to StrNoConnect\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\n\n!   -- Entry selected in browser --\nSelectEntry:\n    put the index of FileName into N\n    put element N of FileList into Item\n    put property `name` of Item into EntryName\n    put property `type` of Item into EntryType\n    if EntryType is `dir`\n    begin\n        ! Navigate into directory\n        if CurrentPath is empty\n            put EntryName into CurrentPath\n        else\n            put CurrentPath cat `/` cat EntryName into CurrentPath\n        go to ShowBrowser\n    end\n    ! Build full relative path and open file\n    if CurrentPath is empty\n        put EntryName into TabItem\n    else\n        put CurrentPath cat `/` cat EntryName into TabItem\n    set style `display` of Overlay to `none`\n    put EntryName into File\n    go to OpenFile\n\nCloseBrowser:\n    set style `display` of Overlay to `none`\n    stop\n!! @hash ac5d5c01\n!! @verified ac5d5c01\n!!!\n!! This section is tab management; creating a new tab, opening a file, selecting a tab and closing a tab.\n!! Activating a tab leaves Blocks mode first. A tab is a different file, and Blocks mode belongs to the file you were reading — so switching tabs, and opening a file, both land in the flat editor rather than refusing to move until you leave the mode by hand.\n!! **A tab remembers where the flat editor was standing** — the caret's line and the viewport's scroll offset — and puts both back on return, whether the reader left by the tab bar, the Blocks button or the Graph one. Two values rather than one because they are independent: scrolling must not move the insertion point, and coming back must not carry the view to the caret, which is the jump a video editor teaches you to distrust. `codemirror` carries a command for each half and each half only — `cursor to line` does not scroll and `view to` does not move the caret — and the scroll one refreshes the editor on the way, which a CodeMirror hidden by a mode switch needs.\n!   Make room for one more tab: the count up by one and every per-tab array grown to match. **In one\n!   place, and that is the point of it.** There are two ways to open a tab — the + button and the file\n!   browser — and each used to grow the arrays itself, so an array added for one of them is a tab that\n!   fails in the other. That is not hypothetical: the caret and view records added for the tab\n!   restoration above went into `NewFile` only, and opening a *file* then died with \"Array index 1 is\n!   out of range for 'TabCursor'\" the moment a second tab existed with no slot to hold it.\n!   `CloseTab` shifts the value-carrying arrays back down when a tab goes — the element ones are rebuilt\n!   from the count instead — so it is the other place that has to know this list, and there are only two.\n!\n!   **The new slot is cleared, not assumed empty.** Growing an array does not shorten it, so a slot\n!   vacated when a tab was closed keeps whatever it held — and the two records below are only ever\n!   *read*, so a stale caret and scroll would be put back on a tab that has never been anywhere. The\n!   other arrays are written by their callers straight afterwards, which is why they never showed it.\n!   Empty is also what `TabRestore` wants: it asks whether a value is *numeric*, so a cleared slot is\n!   a tab with nowhere to restore to, which is the honest answer for one that has never been left.\nTabGrow:\n    add 1 to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabPath to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabName to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabSaved to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabCursor to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabView to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabBtn to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabLabel to TabCount\n    set the elements of TabClose to TabCount\n    ! `TabCount - 1`, because an index is counted from zero and the count is not: the slot just made is\n    ! the last one, not the next one. (`Tmp` rather than `N`, which the callers are holding the new\n    ! tab's index in.)\n    put TabCount into Tmp\n    subtract 1 from Tmp\n    index TabCursor to Tmp\n    put `` into TabCursor\n    index TabView to Tmp\n    put `` into TabView\n    return\n\n!   -- New empty tab --\nNewFile:\n    put TabCount into N\n    gosub to TabGrow\n    index TabPath to N\n    put `` into TabPath\n    index TabName to N\n    put `new` into TabName\n    index TabSaved to N\n    put `` into TabSaved\n    put N into ActiveTab\n    go to ActivateTab\n\n!   -- Open a file (TabItem=relative path, File=display name) --\nOpenFile:\n    ! Check if already open in a tab\n    put 0 into N\n    while N is less than TabCount\n    begin\n        index TabPath to N\n        if TabPath is TabItem\n        begin\n            put N into ActiveTab\n            go to ActivateTab\n        end\n        add 1 to N\n    end\n    ! Not open -- load from server and create a new tab\n    rest get Content from `/read/` cat TabItem or go BrowserError\n    if TabItem ends with `.json`\n    begin\n        put Content into JsonText\n        gosub to FormatJson\n        put JsonText into Content\n    end\n    gosub to TabGrow\n    index TabPath to N\n    put TabItem into TabPath\n    index TabName to N\n    put File into TabName\n    index TabSaved to N\n    put Content into TabSaved\n    put N into ActiveTab\n    go to ActivateTab\n\n!   -- A JSON file arrives minified as often as not, and one line of it is unreadable --\nFormatJson:\n    ! Only a file that is *one line* is reformatted. A file already laid out, at whatever indent its\n    ! author chose, is left exactly as it is — the complaint is that one line of JSON is unreadable,\n    ! not that somebody's formatting is wrong, and normalising it would rewrite their file the first\n    ! time they saved. `json split` divides on newline by default, so the test costs two statements\n    ! and needs no new vocabulary.\n    json split JsonText into JsonLines\n    if the json count of JsonLines is greater than 1 return\n    ! The formatted text is what the tab records as its own, so opening a file does not mark it\n    ! modified — and because the poll formats the file it reads in just the same way, the two agree\n    ! and the tab settles rather than reloading itself every three seconds. Malformed JSON is left\n    ! as it is: a file that will not parse is the one you most want to look at, so the error is\n    ! caught rather than reported. The runtime's own formatter does the work — `json format` is\n    ! JSON.stringify with a two-space indent, the same indent the Python runtime writes on save.\n    json format JsonText or go FormatJsonEnd\nFormatJsonEnd:\n    return\n\n!   -- Switch to an existing tab --\nSwitchTab:\n    put the index of TabLabel into ActiveTab\n    go to ActivateTab\n\n!   -- Close a tab --\nCloseTab:\n    put the index of TabClose into M\n    ! Shift elements above M down by one\n    put M into N\n    add 1 to N\n    while N is less than TabCount\n    begin\n        index TabPath to N\n        put TabPath into File\n        subtract 1 from N\n        index TabPath to N\n        put File into TabPath\n        add 1 to N\n        index TabName to N\n        put TabName into File\n        subtract 1 from N\n        index TabName to N\n        put File into TabName\n        add 1 to N\n        index TabSaved to N\n        put TabSaved into FileContent\n        subtract 1 from N\n        index TabSaved to N\n        put FileContent into TabSaved\n        add 1 to N\n        index TabCursor to N\n        put TabCursor into Tmp\n        subtract 1 from N\n        index TabCursor to N\n        put Tmp into TabCursor\n        add 1 to N\n        index TabView to N\n        put TabView into Tmp\n        subtract 1 from N\n        index TabView to N\n        put Tmp into TabView\n        add 1 to N\n        add 1 to N\n    end\n    subtract 1 from TabCount\n    if TabCount is 0\n    begin\n        put 0 into ActiveTab\n        codemirror set content of ContentEditor to ``\n        set the content of TabBar to ``\n        stop\n    end\n    ! Adjust ActiveTab if it was after the closed tab\n    if ActiveTab is greater than M subtract 1 from ActiveTab\n    ! Clamp ActiveTab to valid range\n    put TabCount into FileCount\n    subtract 1 from FileCount\n    if ActiveTab is greater than FileCount\n    begin\n        put FileCount into ActiveTab\n    end\n    go to ActivateTab\n\n!   Where the flat editor is standing, remembered per tab and put back on return. The caret and the\n!   view are kept apart on purpose: `codemirror cursor to line` does not scroll and\n!   `codemirror view to` does not move the caret, so a revisit restores both without one dragging\n!   the other. Restoring the caret *and then* the view is the order that matters — the view is the\n!   one that has to end up exact, and setting the caret cannot disturb it.\nTabRemember:\n    if TabCount is 0 return\n    index TabCursor to ActiveTab\n    codemirror get cursor of ContentEditor into TabCursor\n    index TabView to ActiveTab\n    codemirror view of ContentEditor into TabView\n    return\n\nTabRestore:\n    if TabCount is 0 return\n    ! `is numeric` and not `is not empty`: a tab that has never been left has an untouched slot, and\n    ! zero is a perfectly good caret line and scroll offset — so the test has to be about the kind of\n    ! value, not about whether there is one.\n    index TabCursor to ActiveTab\n    if TabCursor is numeric codemirror cursor to line TabCursor in ContentEditor\n    index TabView to ActiveTab\n    if TabView is numeric codemirror view to TabView in ContentEditor\n    return\n\nActivateTab:\n    gosub to DoExitPanes\n    gosub to TabRemember\n    index TabSaved to ActiveTab\n    codemirror set content of ContentEditor to TabSaved\n    gosub to TabRestore\n    gosub to RebuildTabBar\n    stop\n!! @hash e6bbb6c8\n!! @verified e6bbb6c8\n!!!\n!! Rebuilding the tab bar is done by assigning styles to each tab to indicate which one is current.\nRebuildTabBar:\n!   -- Rebuild tab bar --\n    set the content of TabBar to ``\n    put 0 into N\n    while N is less than TabCount\n    begin\n        index TabBtn to N\n        create TabBtn in TabBar\n        if N is ActiveTab\n        begin\n            set the style of TabBtn to `display:flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;padding:4px 10px;cursor:pointer;background:#555;color:white;border-bottom:2px solid lightgreen`\n        end\n        else\n        begin\n            set the style of TabBtn to `display:flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;padding:4px 10px;cursor:pointer;background:#3a3a3a;color:#aaa`\n        end\n        index TabLabel to N\n        create TabLabel in TabBtn\n        index TabName to N\n        set the text of TabLabel to TabName\n        on click TabLabel go to SwitchTab\n        index TabClose to N\n        create TabClose in TabBtn\n        set attribute `src` of TabClose to `https://allspeak.ai/icon/stop.png`\n        set the style of TabClose to `width:12px;height:12px;opacity:0.6;cursor:pointer`\n        on click TabClose go to CloseTab\n        add 1 to N\n    end\n    return\n!! @hash 1347598c\n!! @verified 1347598c\n!!!\n!! Here are some utility functions.\n!! DoFind searches in flat mode via CodeMirror's find dialog; in Blocks mode it jumps to the next block whose code or prose contains the text selected in either pane (wrapping around), highlighting the match so repeated presses walk through the hits.\n!   -- Find --\nDoFind:\n    if BlocksMode is 1 go to FindInBlocks\n    codemirror find in ContentEditor\n    stop\n\nFindInBlocks:\n    ! Blocks-mode find: walk the sections after the current one (wrapping)\n    ! for the term selected in either pane and jump to the first hit.\n    put the selected text into SearchTerm\n    if SearchTerm is empty\n    begin\n        set the content of StatusSpan to `Select text in a pane to search blocks`\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    put CurBlock into I\n    add 1 to I\n    while I is not CurBlock\n    begin\n        put I modulo SecCount into I\n        if I is CurBlock go to FindNotFound\n        gosub to BlockContains\n        if Found is 1 go to FindMatch\n        add 1 to I\n    end\nFindNotFound:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `No other block contains ` cat SearchTerm\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\nFindMatch:\n    put I into CurBlock\n    gosub to RenderBlock\n    gosub to SelectOccurrence\n    put CurBlock into I\n    add 1 to I\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Found in block ` cat I cat ` of ` cat SecCount\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\n\nBlockContains:\n    ! Search section I's code then prose for the term (SearchTerm); sets Found and FoundPane.\n    put 0 into Found\n    index SecCode to I\n    put the position of SearchTerm in SecCode into Pos\n    if Pos is not less than 0\n    begin\n        put 1 into Found\n        put `code` into FoundPane\n        return\n    end\n    index SecProse to I\n    put the position of SearchTerm in SecProse into Pos\n    if Pos is not less than 0\n    begin\n        put 1 into Found\n        put `doc` into FoundPane\n    end\n    return\n\nSelectOccurrence:\n    ! Highlight the first occurrence of the term (SearchTerm) in the pane that matched.\n    if FoundPane is `code`\n    begin\n        index SecCode to CurBlock\n        put the position of SearchTerm in SecCode into Pos\n        if Pos is not less than 0\n        begin\n            put Pos into N\n            put the length of SearchTerm into M\n            add N to M\n            set the selection of BlocksCodePane from N to M\n        end\n    end\n    else\n    begin\n        index SecProse to CurBlock\n        put the position of SearchTerm in SecProse into Pos\n        if Pos is not less than 0\n        begin\n            put Pos into N\n            put the length of SearchTerm into M\n            add N to M\n            set the selection of BlocksDocPane from N to M\n        end\n    end\n    return\n\n!   -- Utilities --\nClearStatus:\n    wait 3 seconds\n    set the content of StatusSpan to ``\n    stop\n!! @hash 74b13222\n!! @verified 74b13222\n!!!\n!! Blocks parser. Walks the current Source line-by-line and populates the per-section arrays (Start, End, Prose, Code, Hash, Verified, HashState, VerifyState) plus the Outside-content array used to preserve text between sections during rebuild.\n!!\n!! Same classifier logic as tools/asdoc-check.allspeak — the spec is one source of truth even though the code is duplicated here for the editor's in-process use.\nParseSource:\n    put `` into AppPage\n    ! The flavour is re-resolved on every parse, for the same reason `AppPage` is cleared: a marker that has\n    ! just been deleted, or a `@py` that has just been added, must not go on deciding.\n    put `` into TabFlavour\n    put 0 into SecCount\n    put 0 into InSection\n    put `` into Source\n    codemirror get content of ContentEditor into Source\n    put Source into Lines\n    split Lines\n    put the elements of Lines into LineCount\n    set the elements of Outside to 1\n    index Outside to 0\n    put `` into Outside\n    put `` into OutsideTmp\n    put 0 into N\n    while N is less than LineCount\n    begin\n        index Lines to N\n        put Lines into Line\n        gosub to Classify\n        if InSection is 0 gosub to OutsideDispatch\n        else gosub to InsideDispatch\n        add 1 to N\n    end\n    if InSection is 1\n    begin\n        ! Unclosed section — close synthetically so user can still see it.\n        gosub to CloseSection\n    end\n    ! Save trailing outside chunk; need to grow Outside one past the last\n    ! per-section index allocated by OpenSection.\n    put SecCount into Tmp\n    add 1 to Tmp\n    set the elements of Outside to Tmp\n    index Outside to SecCount\n    put OutsideTmp into Outside\n\n    !   -- Review signals --\n    ! What the parse cannot see for itself: blocks nothing enters. The viz plugin compiles\n    ! the same source and reports it. Nothing here raises an error — a buffer mid-edit\n    ! frequently will not compile, which is the editor's ordinary condition, and the plugin\n    ! simply reports fewer records. With no flags the TOC renders exactly as it did before.\n    put 0 into FlagCount\n    index TabPath to ActiveTab\n    put TabPath into EditorPath\n    model the script in EditorPath as Source giving Model\n    put the elements of Model into MarkCount\n    put 0 into K\n    while K is less than MarkCount\n    begin\n        index Model to K\n        put Model into MarkRecord\n        if MarkRecord starts with `anchor | reachable=no` gosub to NoteMarker\n        add 1 to K\n    end\n\n    ! A script the plugin cannot compile yields no anchors and so no flags — which used to\n    ! look exactly like a clean file. The plugin reports why in a `problem` record, so say it:\n    ! a reviewer's first question about an empty picture is whether there was anything to draw.\n    ! The record carries the runtime's own wording, so it is already in the reader's language.\n    put 0 into K\n    while K is less than MarkCount\n    begin\n        index Model to K\n        put Model into MarkRecord\n        if MarkRecord starts with `problem |` gosub to NoteProblem\n        add 1 to K\n    end\n\n    ! **The attributes the program carries — the real spelling.** The plugin reads them off the compiled\n    ! program, which is the only place they can be read correctly: the tokeniser is what knows an `@` inside a\n    ! literal from an attribute, so a reader scanning the buffer's text would be a second, worse implementation\n    ! of that rule. A file-level attribute like `@app parser.html` is a line of its own, and its record carries\n    ! the line it is on, so a tool that wants to point at it can.\n    put 0 into K\n    while K is less than MarkCount\n    begin\n        index Model to K\n        put Model into MarkRecord\n        if MarkRecord starts with `attr | ` gosub to NoteAttribute\n        if MarkRecord starts with `flavour | ` gosub to NoteFlavour\n        add 1 to K\n    end\n    return\n\n!   One attribute, as the model reports it: `attr | line=N | <text>`.\n!   The text is the language's to carry and a tool's to read — the runtime never splits a key from a value — so\n!   the split below is the tool's, on the record's own separator, which the text cannot contain.\nNoteAttribute:\n    split MarkRecord on ` | `\n    if the elements of MarkRecord is less than 3 return\n    index MarkRecord to 2\n    put MarkRecord into LContent\n    gosub to SplitMeta\n    return\n\n!   Which runtime this tab's script is for, and where that came from: `flavour | py | from=script`.\n!\n!   **Read from the model and not from the script's own attributes**, and that is the fault this replaced. An\n!   `attr` record is emitted only for a program the runtime *compiled* — and a script carrying `@py` is by\n!   definition one the JavaScript runtime cannot compile, because `dictionary` and `list` are Python's. So the\n!   marker was invisible to the editor exactly when it mattered: `parser.allspeak` with `@py` at the top and\n!   `dictionary` at line 43 modelled to `commands=0`, no `attr` records, `TabFlavour` fell through to the\n!   project's answer and Record refused a script it had been built to run. Reported by Graham, 2026-10-05.\n!   The plugin reports this from the *token stream*, so it is there whether the compile succeeded or not.\nNoteFlavour:\n    split MarkRecord on ` | `\n    if the elements of MarkRecord is less than 3 return\n    index MarkRecord to 1\n    put MarkRecord into TabFlavour\n    return\n\nOutsideDispatch:\n    if Kind is `DOC` gosub to OpenSection\n    else if Kind is `META` gosub to OpenSection\n    else\n    begin\n        if OutsideTmp is empty put Line into OutsideTmp\n        else put OutsideTmp cat newline cat Line into OutsideTmp\n    end\n    return\n\nInsideDispatch:\n    if Kind is `TERM` gosub to CloseSection\n    else if Kind is `META` gosub to RecordMeta\n    else if Kind is `DOC` gosub to AppendProse\n    else if Kind is `CODE` gosub to AppendCode\n    return\n\n!   Classify the current line as code/comment or documentation\nClassify:\n    put `CODE` into Kind\n    if Line is `!!!`\n    begin\n        put `TERM` into Kind\n        return\n    end\n    if Line is `!!`\n    begin\n        put `DOC` into Kind\n        put `` into LContent\n        return\n    end\n    if Line starts with `!! ` or Line starts with `!!` cat tab\n    begin\n        put `DOC` into Kind\n        put from 3 of Line into LContent\n        gosub to MaybeMeta\n        return\n    end\n    return\n\n!   Deal with @ (META)\nMaybeMeta:\n    if LContent starts with `@`\n    begin\n        put `META` into Kind\n        put from 1 of LContent into LContent\n        gosub to SplitMeta\n    end\n    return\n\n!   A key and the rest of it, from an attribute's text.\n!\n!   **The one reading of an attribute's text, shared by both places attributes arrive.** A `@`-line inside a\n!   `!!` doc block is the analyser's metadata and never reaches the compiler; an attribute in the script itself\n!   is carried into the program and comes back through the model. They are the same thing to a reader — a key,\n!   a space, and the rest — so they are split in one place rather than two, and a tool vocabulary read here\n!   reads the same either way.\n!\n!   The spelling `!! @app` is accepted and is not the real thing: the doc-block form says nothing to the\n!   compiler, so a page named there launches a page that the *program* does not know about. Prefer `@app`.\nSplitMeta:\n    put the position of ` ` in LContent into Pos\n    if Pos is less than 0\n    begin\n        put LContent into MetaKey\n        put `` into MetaValue\n    end\n    else\n    begin\n        put left Pos of LContent into MetaKey\n        add 1 to Pos\n        put from Pos of LContent into MetaValue\n    end\n    if MetaKey is `app` put MetaValue into AppPage\n    return\n\nOpenSection:\n    ! Finalize the outside chunk that ended with the previous line.\n    put SecCount into Tmp\n    add 1 to Tmp\n    set the elements of Outside to Tmp\n    index Outside to SecCount\n    put OutsideTmp into Outside\n    put `` into OutsideTmp\n    put N into SecStartTmp\n    add 1 to SecStartTmp\n    put 1 into InSection\n    put `` into SecProseTmp\n    put `` into SecCodeTmp\n    put `` into SecHashTmp\n    put `` into SecVerifiedTmp\n    put 0 into SecHasCode\n    if Kind is `META` gosub to RecordMeta\n    if Kind is `DOC` gosub to AppendProse\n    return\n\nRecordMeta:\n    if MetaKey is `hash` put MetaValue into SecHashTmp\n    if MetaKey is `verified` put MetaValue into SecVerifiedTmp\n    return\n\nAppendProse:\n    if SecProseTmp is empty put LContent into SecProseTmp\n    else put SecProseTmp cat newline cat LContent into SecProseTmp\n    return\n\nAppendCode:\n    if SecCodeTmp is empty put Line into SecCodeTmp\n    else put SecCodeTmp cat newline cat Line into SecCodeTmp\n    if Line is not `` put 1 into SecHasCode\n    return\n\nCloseSection:\n    ! Compute hash + states.\n    if SecHasCode is 0 gosub to ScoreNoCode\n    else gosub to ScoreWithCode\n    ! Grow all the per-section arrays by one and store this section.\n    put SecCount into I\n    add 1 to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecStart to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecEnd to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecProse to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecCode to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecHash to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecVerified to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecHashState to SecCount\n    set the elements of SecVerifyState to SecCount\n    index SecStart to I\n    put SecStartTmp into SecStart\n    index SecEnd to I\n    put N into SecEnd\n    add 1 to SecEnd\n    index SecProse to I\n    put SecProseTmp into SecProse\n    index SecCode to I\n    put SecCodeTmp into SecCode\n    index SecHash to I\n    put CurHash into SecHash    ! store current hash as the live one (not stale-stored)\n    index SecVerified to I\n    put SecVerifiedTmp into SecVerified\n    index SecHashState to I\n    put HashState into SecHashState\n    index SecVerifyState to I\n    put VerifyState into SecVerifyState\n    put 0 into InSection\n    put `` into OutsideTmp\n    return\n\nScoreNoCode:\n    put `` into CurHash\n    put `no-code` into HashState\n    if SecVerifiedTmp is empty put `unverified` into VerifyState\n    else put `verified-no-code` into VerifyState\n    return\n\nScoreWithCode:\n    put hash SecCodeTmp into Tmp\n    put left 8 of Tmp into CurHash\n    if SecHashTmp is empty put `no-baseline` into HashState\n    else if SecHashTmp is CurHash put `fresh` into HashState\n    else put `stale` into HashState\n    if SecVerifiedTmp is empty put `unverified` into VerifyState\n    else if SecVerifiedTmp is CurHash put `verified-fresh` into VerifyState\n    else put `verified-stale` into VerifyState\n    return\n!! @hash 5795434c\n!! @verified d37980ab\n!!!\n!! Blocks view.\n!! ToggleBlocks switches between flat and Blocks panes.\n!! EnterBlocks parses the current source, opens the block containing the flat editor's cursor line (so the pane lands where you were working), and scrolls the TOC to that block.\n!! ExitBlocks flushes pending edits, reveals the flat pane again, and scrolls it to the start of the block just viewed; the work is in DoExitBlocks so ShowBrowser can call it as a subroutine before opening a tab.\n!! RenderBlock paints the textareas and the toolbar badge for the current section.\n!! UpdateBadge derives badge text/colour from the section's hash and verify states.\nToggleBlocks:\n    if BlocksMode is 0 go to EnterBlocks\n    go to ExitBlocks\n\n!   Enter Blocks mode\n!   **At most one mode is on, and `DoExitPanes` is where that is said.** Every entry point turns the\n!   others off through it rather than each naming the ones it happens to know about — which is how\n!   this one came to miss the Graph pane: it was written before Graph existed, and nothing pointed at\n!   the assumption. The omission had two faces, and Graham found both by the one path that showed it,\n!   Graph then Blocks: the Graph button still read `Edit`, so two buttons offered to leave a pane\n!   neither of which was on, and `VizHost` was never hidden, so the graph sat under the Blocks pane.\nEnterBlocks:\n    gosub to DoExitPanes\n    gosub to ParseSource\n    if SecCount is 0\n    begin\n        set the content of StatusSpan to `No doc blocks in this file`\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    ! Open the block containing the flat editor's cursor: the last section\n    ! whose opener line is at or above the cursor's line, so a cursor in the\n    ! gap between two blocks lands on the block above. Falls back to block 0\n    ! when the cursor is above the first section.\n    codemirror get cursor of ContentEditor into Tmp\n    add 1 to Tmp\n    put 0 into CurBlock\n    put 0 into I\n    while I is less than SecCount\n    begin\n        index SecStart to I\n        if SecStart is not greater than Tmp put I into CurBlock\n        add 1 to I\n    end\n    put 1 into BlocksMode\n    ! The toggle button now leaves Blocks mode, so relabel it.\n    set the content of BlocksBtn to `Edit`\n    set style `display` of EditorArea to `none`\n    set style `display` of BlocksArea to `flex`\n    ! Restore divider position from previous session if persisted.\n    get StoredWidth from storage as `asedit-blocks-code-width`\n    if StoredWidth is `null` put 0 into StoredWidth\n    if StoredWidth is `undefined` put 0 into StoredWidth\n    if StoredWidth is empty put 0 into StoredWidth\n    if StoredWidth is greater than 100\n        set style `flex` of BlocksCodePane to `0 0 ` cat StoredWidth cat `px`\n    gosub to RenderToc\n    ! Reveal the current block's row in the TOC sidebar.\n    index TocRow to CurBlock\n    scroll TocRow into view\n    gosub to RenderBlock\n    stop\n\n!   Exit Blocks mode\nExitBlocks:\n    gosub to DoExitBlocks\n    stop\n\n!   Exit Blocks mode (callable as a subroutine -- e.g. from ShowBrowser)\nDoExitBlocks:\n    gosub to FlushBlock\n    ! Re-parse so SecStart reflects any line shifts from the flush rebuild.\n    gosub to ParseSource\n    put 0 into BlocksMode\n    ! Back in flat mode the toggle button re-enters Blocks mode.\n    set the content of BlocksBtn to `Blocks`\n    set style `display` of BlocksArea to `none`\n    set style `display` of EditorArea to `block`\n    ! Scroll the flat editor so the start of the block just viewed is visible.\n    if SecCount is greater than 0\n    begin\n        ! Guard against a stale CurBlock (e.g. tab switched while in Blocks mode).\n        if CurBlock is not less than SecCount\n        begin\n            put SecCount into Tmp\n            take 1 from Tmp\n            put Tmp into CurBlock\n        end\n        index SecStart to CurBlock\n        put SecStart into Tmp\n        take 1 from Tmp\n        codemirror scroll to line Tmp in ContentEditor\n    end\n    ! Remembered here rather than on the way *in*, because leaving is what moves the editor: the\n    ! scroll above is deliberate and puts the block's start at the top, and that is where the reader\n    ! then is. Every way out of Blocks passes through this subroutine — the button, a tab switch, and\n    ! the way into Graph — so one save here covers all of them, and the pair of values is consistent\n    ! whichever way the reader came back.\n    gosub to TabRemember\n    return\n\nNextBlock:\n    put SecCount into Tmp\n    take 2 from Tmp\n    if CurBlock is greater than Tmp stop\n    gosub to FlushBlock\n    add 1 to CurBlock\n    gosub to RenderBlock\n    stop\n\nPrevBlock:\n    if CurBlock is less than 1 stop\n    gosub to FlushBlock\n    take 1 from CurBlock\n    gosub to RenderBlock\n    stop\n\nRenderBlock:\n    index SecProse to CurBlock\n    set the content of BlocksDocPane to SecProse\n    index SecCode to CurBlock\n    set the content of BlocksCodePane to SecCode\n    gosub to UpdateBadge\n    gosub to RenderToc\n    return\n\nUpdateBadge:\n    put CurBlock into I\n    add 1 to I\n    set the content of BlocksTitle to `Block ` cat I cat ` of ` cat SecCount\n    index SecVerifyState to CurBlock\n    put SecVerifyState into VerifyState\n    index SecHashState to CurBlock\n    put SecHashState into HashState\n    if VerifyState is `verified-fresh`\n    begin\n        set the content of BlocksBadge to `verified · fresh`\n        set style `background` of BlocksBadge to `#2e7d32`\n    end\n    else if VerifyState is `verified-stale`\n    begin\n        set the content of BlocksBadge to `verified · stale`\n        set style `background` of BlocksBadge to `#f9a825`\n    end\n    else if HashState is `no-code`\n    begin\n        set the content of BlocksBadge to `no code`\n        set style `background` of BlocksBadge to `#666`\n    end\n    else\n    begin\n        set the content of BlocksBadge to `unverified`\n        set style `background` of BlocksBadge to `#666`\n    end\n    return\n!! @hash 4464102e\n!! @verified 4464102e\n!!!\n!! Blocks save.\n!! FlushBlock pushes the current pane edits back into the\n!! per-section arrays (recomputing the section's hash if its code changed)\n!! and rebuilds the full source by walking Outside + Section + Outside +\n!! ... etc. The new source is written to ContentEditor (CodeMirror), which\n!! the existing AutoSave loop persists to disk on its next iteration.\n!! No-op if neither pane changed.\n!!\nFlushBlock:\n    if BlocksMode is 0 return\n    put the text of BlocksDocPane into NewProse\n    put the text of BlocksCodePane into NewCode\n    index SecProse to CurBlock\n    put SecProse into OldProse\n    index SecCode to CurBlock\n    put SecCode into OldCode\n    if NewProse is OldProse and NewCode is OldCode return\n    index SecProse to CurBlock\n    put NewProse into SecProse\n    index SecCode to CurBlock\n    put NewCode into SecCode\n    ! Recompute hash and states for the edited block.\n    put NewCode into SecCodeTmp\n    put NewProse into SecProseTmp\n    put 0 into SecHasCode\n    if NewCode is not empty put 1 into SecHasCode\n    index SecVerified to CurBlock\n    put SecVerified into SecVerifiedTmp\n    index SecHash to CurBlock\n    put SecHash into SecHashTmp\n    if SecHasCode is 0 gosub to ScoreNoCode\n    else gosub to ScoreWithCode\n    index SecHash to CurBlock\n    put CurHash into SecHash\n    index SecHashState to CurBlock\n    put HashState into SecHashState\n    index SecVerifyState to CurBlock\n    put VerifyState into SecVerifyState\n    gosub to RebuildSource\n    codemirror set content of ContentEditor to Source\n    gosub to UpdateBadge\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Block flushed`\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    return\n\nRebuildSource:\n    index Outside to 0\n    put Outside into Source\n    put 0 into I\n    while I is less than SecCount\n    begin\n        gosub to BuildSectionText\n        put Source cat BlkText into Source\n        add 1 to I\n        index Outside to I\n        put Source cat newline cat Outside into Source\n    end\n    return\n\nBuildSectionText:\n    ! Reads I; writes BlkText with section I's reassembled lines.\n    put `` into BlkText\n    index SecProse to I\n    put SecProse into Tmp\n    if Tmp is not empty\n    begin\n        put Tmp into ProseSrc\n        split ProseSrc\n        put the elements of ProseSrc into PCount\n        put 0 into PJ\n        while PJ is less than PCount\n        begin\n            index ProseSrc to PJ\n            put ProseSrc into PL\n            if PL is empty put BlkText cat `!!` cat newline into BlkText\n            else put BlkText cat `!! ` cat PL cat newline into BlkText\n            add 1 to PJ\n        end\n    end\n    index SecCode to I\n    put SecCode into Tmp\n    if Tmp is not empty put BlkText cat Tmp cat newline into BlkText\n    index SecHash to I\n    put SecHash into Tmp\n    if Tmp is not empty put BlkText cat `!! @hash ` cat Tmp cat newline into BlkText\n    index SecVerified to I\n    put SecVerified into Tmp\n    if Tmp is not empty put BlkText cat `!! @verified ` cat Tmp cat newline into BlkText\n    put BlkText cat `!!!` into BlkText\n    return\n!! @hash d3dbcc0b\n!! @verified d3dbcc0b\n!!!\n!! Mark verified — writes the current code's hash into the section's\n!! \"@verified\" slot, then flushes so the file picks it up. The badge turns\n!! green on the next render.\n!! MarkAllVerified does the same for every code-bearing section in one pass.\n!! It is a bulk, self-attestation action, so it asks for confirmation first —\n!! a single misclick would otherwise wipe the file's granular verification record.\nMarkVerified:\n    if BlocksMode is 0 stop\n    gosub to FlushBlock      ! ensure SecHash reflects current code first\n    index SecHash to CurBlock\n    put SecHash into SecHashTmp\n    if SecHashTmp is empty stop    ! no-code section, nothing to verify\n    index SecVerified to CurBlock\n    put SecHashTmp into SecVerified\n    put `verified-fresh` into Tmp\n    index SecVerifyState to CurBlock\n    put Tmp into SecVerifyState\n    gosub to RebuildSource\n    codemirror set content of ContentEditor to Source\n    gosub to UpdateBadge\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Marked verified`\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\n\nMarkAllVerified:\n    if BlocksMode is 0 stop\n    gosub to FlushBlock      ! ensure the current block's hash is current too\n    ! Count the code-bearing sections; only those can carry a @verified mark.\n    put 0 into N\n    put 0 into I\n    while I is less than SecCount\n    begin\n        index SecHash to I\n        if SecHash is not empty add 1 to N\n        add 1 to I\n    end\n    if N is 0 stop\n    if N is 1 put `Mark 1 block as verified?` into Tmp\n    else put `Mark all ` cat N cat ` blocks as verified?` into Tmp\n    if confirm Tmp\n    begin\n        put 0 into I\n        while I is less than SecCount\n        begin\n            index SecHash to I\n            if SecHash is not empty\n            begin\n                index SecVerified to I\n                put SecHash into SecVerified\n                index SecVerifyState to I\n                put `verified-fresh` into SecVerifyState\n            end\n            add 1 to I\n        end\n        gosub to RebuildSource\n        codemirror set content of ContentEditor to Source\n        gosub to RenderBlock\n        if N is 1 put `Marked 1 block verified` into Tmp\n        else put `Marked ` cat N cat ` blocks verified` into Tmp\n        set the content of StatusSpan to Tmp\n        fork to ClearStatus\n    end\n    stop\n!! @hash b0f5bf9f\n!! @verified b0f5bf9f\n!!!\n!! Take the line out of `anchor | reachable=no | kind=label | line=NN | ...`, so a block\n!! can be told whether it is one nothing enters. The anchor record is the documented shape, which is why its line can be read positionally — a finding's line lives in prose and cannot.\nNoteMarker:\n    put the position of `line=` in MarkRecord into Pos\n    add 5 to Pos\n    put from Pos of MarkRecord into MarkRest\n    put the position of ` ` in MarkRest into MarkSpace\n    if MarkSpace is less than 0 put MarkRest into MarkDigits\n    else put left MarkSpace of MarkRest into MarkDigits\n    ! The line number is read out of a record, so it arrives as text. Comparing text with the\n    ! numeric block bounds does not coerce here, so the whole range test silently fails — the\n    ! same reason `add 0 to` is used for numbers read from strings elsewhere in this codebase.\n    add 0 to MarkDigits\n    ! Grow the array first, then write the new last element: sizing to the current count and\n    ! indexing it would ask for element 0 of an empty list on the first flag. This is the same\n    ! order the editor's own CloseSection uses, for the same reason.\n    put FlagCount into I\n    add 1 to FlagCount\n    set the elements of FlagLine to FlagCount\n    index FlagLine to I\n    put MarkDigits into FlagLine\n    return\n!! @hash e3dfc5f8\n!! @verified e3dfc5f8\n!!!\n!! Say, in the status line, why the analysis could not run. The plugin reports a script it could not compile as a `problem` record, and without this the result is indistinguishable from a file with nothing wrong in it.\nNoteProblem:\n    put the position of `| does not compile` in MarkRecord into Pos\n    if Pos is less than 0 put MarkRecord into MarkRest\n    else\n    begin\n        add 13 to Pos\n        put from Pos of MarkRecord into MarkRest\n    end\n    set the content of StatusSpan to MarkRest\n    return\n!! @hash 09c88156\n!! @verified 09c88156\n!!!\n!! Decide whether one block's line range holds a flagged line. The bounds are widened by one so that both tests can be positive: `is not less than` is a form the editor avoids elsewhere.\nBlockFlagged:\n    put 0 into Found\n    put From into FromCheck\n    subtract 1 from FromCheck\n    put To into ToCheck\n    add 1 to ToCheck\n    put 0 into K\n    while K is less than FlagCount\n    begin\n        index FlagLine to K\n        put FlagLine into MarkLine\n        if MarkLine is greater than FromCheck and MarkLine is less than ToCheck put 1 into Found\n        add 1 to K\n    end\n    return\n!! @hash b832c0aa\n!! @verified b832c0aa\n!!!\n!! Blocks TOC. Renders one row per parsed section in the BlocksToc\n!! sidebar, highlighting the current block. Each row's label is the\n!! first prose line of its section, truncated. Clicking a row flushes\n!! any pending edit and jumps to that block.\n!! Rows are shaded dark by verification state — green = verified, amber = stale, grey = unverified — so the blue current row stands out at a glance.\nRenderToc:\n    set the content of BlocksToc to ``\n    set the elements of TocRow to SecCount\n    set the elements of TocLabel to SecCount\n    put 0 into J\n    while J is less than SecCount\n    begin\n        index TocRow to J\n        create TocRow in BlocksToc\n        ! Colour and tooltip are decided together, so a row can never carry a colour without\n        ! saying what it means. The tooltips are the legend: a reviewer should be able to read\n        ! every state off the sidebar without being told them anywhere else.\n        put `padding:4px 10px;cursor:pointer` into Tmp\n        put `Not yet verified` into Tip\n        if J is CurBlock\n        begin\n            put Tmp cat `;background:#1e88e5;color:white` into Tmp\n            put `You are reading this block` into Tip\n        end\n        else\n        begin\n            index SecVerifyState to J\n            put SecVerifyState into VerifyState\n            if VerifyState is `verified-fresh`\n            begin\n                put Tmp cat `;background:#1b5e20` into Tmp\n                put `Verified: the prose and the code still agree` into Tip\n            end\n            else if VerifyState is `verified-stale`\n            begin\n                put Tmp cat `;background:#a26d18` into Tmp\n                put `Verified once, but the code has changed since — worth a look` into Tip\n            end\n            else put Tmp cat `;background:#3f3f3f` into Tmp\n        end\n        set the style of TocRow to Tmp\n        set attribute `title` of TocRow to Tip\n        index SecProse to J\n        put SecProse into ProseSrc\n        put the position of newline in ProseSrc into NL\n        if NL is less than 0 put ProseSrc into FirstLine\n        else put left NL of ProseSrc into FirstLine\n        put J into Tmp\n        add 1 to Tmp\n        put Tmp cat `. ` cat FirstLine into FirstLine\n\n        ! Does anything enter this block? The row's own colour already carries the\n        ! verification state, so the flag goes on the label and the two signals stay on\n        ! separate channels.\n        index SecStart to J\n        put SecStart into From\n        put J into BlockAfter\n        add 1 to BlockAfter\n        if BlockAfter is less than SecCount\n        begin\n            index SecStart to BlockAfter\n            put SecStart into To\n            subtract 1 from To\n        end\n        else put 1000000 into To\n        gosub to BlockFlagged\n\n\n        index TocLabel to J\n        create TocLabel in TocRow\n        set the content of TocLabel to FirstLine\n        ! A block nothing enters gets a solid background rather than only a struck label: it is\n        ! unmissable at a glance, and a reviewer should not have to read every row to find the one\n        ! that is unreachable. The trade is that such a row shows this instead of its verification\n        ! colour, which is the right way round — an orphaned block is the more urgent fact.\n        if Found is 1\n        begin\n            set the style of TocRow to `background:#b71c1c;color:white`\n            set attribute `title` of TocRow to `Orphaned: nothing enters this block, so none of its code can run`\n        end\n        if Found is 1 set the style of TocLabel to `text-decoration:line-through;opacity:0.55`\n        on click TocLabel go to JumpToBlock\n        add 1 to J\n    end\n    return\n\nJumpToBlock:\n    put the index of TocLabel into Tmp\n    if Tmp is CurBlock stop\n    gosub to FlushBlock\n    put Tmp into CurBlock\n    gosub to RenderBlock\n    stop\n!! @hash 56b2a0d3\n!! @verified 56b2a0d3\n!!!\n!! Graph mode: a third view — the run drawn, not the script.\n!!\n!! Blocks mode reads the script's *shape*; this reads what a *run* did. Those are different questions asked of the same file, which is why it is a third pane rather than another setting on either existing one: a code review asks what the code is made of, and a run review asks where the flow went.\n!!\n!! **The recording is not made here.** It is a file beside the script — `<script>.viz.json`, which the runtime writes with `--trace=` — so a run captured on remote hardware arrives with the project however the project travels, and reviewing a script never has to execute it. This pane reads that file and draws it, nothing more.\n!!\n!! **The drawing and its sidebar are two modules, each loaded on demand.** They are half of this editor by size between them and none of it is needed until somebody looks at a run, so one is `asedit-graph.allspeak` and the other `asedit-side.allspeak`: each fetched, compiled and run on the first click on Graph, and messaged from then on. The split is by *what each is about* rather than by size alone — the pane is the picture and owns the gestures, the sidebar is the reading aid and owns its tabs — so either can grow without the other's file growing with it, and the two are given **different elements** to attach to, which is what keeps the layout out of both of them. Two consequences are worth knowing. This file names no `svg` vocabulary any more — that left with the pane, and the pane is what needs the plugin, which the page still loads because the pane is compiled when it is fetched. And `various/plotview.allspeak` is gone: the module file *is* the view, so there is one copy of the drawing rather than a section kept verbatim beside the harness's own copy of the same code. The harness cuts the view out of the module at its header, so what it tests is still what ships.\n!!\n!! **What crosses is a dict each way, and only when a run is opened.** This section fetches the recording and the script — it is the one that knows which tab is open — and sends the pane their text, the three localised flow words, and the path the recording was expected at. The sidebar is sent the same run, because a doc block is prose about a *line* and the click is what names the line. **And one thing comes back**: a click on a mark is reported here as the line it names, and this file looks that line up in the doc-block model it already holds and shows the result in the sidebar. The editor is the hub in both directions rather than the pane knowing that the sidebar exists, which is what keeps either of them replaceable. The wait, and the message about a recording that is not there, go on the status line, which the pane attached for itself. The gestures never cross at all, and leaving is one more message each, because a module cannot see that it has been taken off the screen.\n!!\n!! **The plumbing is one sentence.** Markers decide what is recorded, and a script without them has nothing to show; rather than explain that in the pane, the message names the file it looked for, because that is the one thing a reader needs in order to produce one.\n!!\n!! **Leaving is automatic when a tab changes.** A mode owns the screen, so opening or switching to a file drops back to the flat editor rather than loading the new script into a pane nobody can see — which is what happened the first time this pane was used, and what Blocks has always done. The rule lives in `ActivateTab`, so every path that shows a file obeys it.\nToggleGraph:\n    if GraphMode is 0 go to EnterGraph\n    gosub to DoExitGraph\n    ! Back to the flat editor exactly where it was left — the caret *and* the view, which is what the\n    ! pane remembering its own state now means for this side too. It is the same pair a tab switch\n    ! restores, through the same two subroutines, so there is one answer to \"where was I\" rather than\n    ! one per way of leaving.\n    !\n    ! This is the only path out of Graph mode that restores: the other is a tab change, which is a\n    ! different file and starts wherever its own record says.\n    gosub to TabRestore\n    stop\n\n!   Entering shows the pane and hands this tab's run to it. Loading the pane first, because the\n!   first visit is the one that fetches and compiles it — the editor is already showing an empty\n!   pane by then, so the wait is visible as a pane rather than as a dead button.\n!   Leaving Blocks mode first is deliberate: the panes are alternatives, not layers.\nEnterGraph:\n    gosub to DoExitPanes\n    put 1 into GraphMode\n    set the content of GraphBtn to `Edit`\n    set style `display` of EditorArea to `none`\n    set style `display` of BlocksArea to `none`\n    set style `display` of GraphArea to `flex`\n    ! The sidebar's width, from the last session that had one. Restored here, before the pane is fetched,\n    ! so the drawing is fitted to the box it will actually be drawn in — a re-fit a moment later would\n    ! move the picture under the reader's eye for no reason it could see.\n    get SideStored from storage as `asedit-graph-side-width`\n    if SideStored is `null` put 0 into SideStored\n    if SideStored is `undefined` put 0 into SideStored\n    if SideStored is empty put 0 into SideStored\n    if SideStored is greater than 200 set style `flex` of GraphSide to `0 0 ` cat SideStored cat `px`\n    ! Where the flat editor was standing, so leaving puts it back there. Read before the editor is\n    ! hidden: a hidden editor still answers, but reading it while it is up is the honest order.\n    gosub to TabRemember\n    gosub to LoadModules\n    ! **The section model is built for this file before the sidebar can be asked about a line.** This file\n    ! parses its own buffer in Blocks mode and only there, so a reader who opens a run without ever having\n    ! opened Blocks would otherwise have no sections at all — or worse, the ones left behind by the last\n    ! file that *was* blocked. It is done here, before the fetch, because the fetch is what puts the pane\n    ! on screen: after it there is a window in which a mark could be clicked against a stale model.\n    gosub to ParseSource\n    gosub to FetchRunText\n    gosub to SendRunToViz\n    gosub to SendRunToSide\n    stop\n\n!   The two files the run is made of, read and defaulted before either is fetched.\n!\n!   **Both reads point their failure clause at the send, and that is what makes this work.** `rest get`\n!   compiles its failure clause — `or` and `on failure` alike — as a clause that ends the thread, so a\n!   clause that merely set a default would leave the pane waiting for a message that never came: the\n!   recording is optional, but the *send* is not. Defaulting both to empty first and jumping to the\n!   send on failure means the pane is told either way, and an empty recording is what makes it name the\n!   file it looked for.\n!\n!   The recording is read first because a recording that will not answer is the failure worth naming;\n!   the script is the picture's background, and it is a different file.\nFetchRunText:\n    put `` into TraceText\n    put `` into SourceText\n    index TabPath to ActiveTab\n    put TabPath cat `.viz.json` into TracePath\n    rest get TraceText from `/read/` cat TracePath or go VizSendTheRun\n    rest get SourceText from `/read/` cat TabPath or go VizSendTheRun\nVizSendTheRun:\n    return\n\n!   Both modules: each fetched, compiled and run once, and messaged from then on.\n!\n!   **`run` compiles the *text*, not a path**, which is why the fetch comes first — and it is a\n!   fetch of a file beside this page, so a project that has the editor has both as well.\n!   `release parent` inside each module is what makes its `run` return: by the time it does, that\n!   module is attached, its handlers are registered and it is listening.\nLoadModules:\n    if VizLoaded is 0 gosub to LoadVizModule\n    if SideLoaded is 0 gosub to LoadSideModule\n    return\n\nLoadVizModule:\n    ! **This directory first, the served copy second, and that order is the page's.** The editor is a file a\n    ! project can be *given* — a pack used to carry one — and a copy of a thing that moves is a copy that goes\n    ! stale: a project could run an old editor beside a current runtime and plugin, and only found out when the\n    ! editor refused to compile its own source. A pack carries none now, so this falls through to the released\n    ! one and cannot be stale; a working tree carries its own, and an edit to the pane is visible in it.\n    put `` into VizSrc\n    ! **The page's element first, then this directory.** A project cannot *fetch* the pane from the site — the\n    ! site answers `access-control-allow-origin: *` for `.js` and `.css` but not for `.allspeak` — so the page\n    ! hands it over in an element instead, the same channel the language comes through. A working tree has no\n    ! such element and reads the pane beside it, which is what makes an edit to the pane visible there.\n    attach VizSrcDiv to `editor-graph` or go VizModuleLocal\n    put the text of VizSrcDiv into VizSrc\n    if VizSrc is not empty go to VizModuleHere\nVizModuleLocal:\n    rest get VizSrc from `asedit-graph.allspeak?v=` cat now or go VizModuleHosted\n    ! **A `<` means the server sent a page, not a module.** A dev server that answers an unknown path with its\n    ! own page does it with `200` and a type guessed from the extension, so the body is the only thing that\n    ! says whether the file arrived — and a page is treated as a miss, so the next place gets its turn. Without\n    ! this, a mis-serving server takes the editor down with one syntax error per asset and names no cause.\n    if VizSrc starts with `<` put `` into VizSrc\n    if VizSrc is empty go to VizModuleHosted\nVizModuleHere:\n    if VizSrc is empty go to VizLoadFailed\n    run VizSrc as VizModule\n    put 1 into VizLoaded\n    return\n\nVizModuleHosted:\n    rest get VizSrc from `https://allspeak.ai/dist/asedit-graph.allspeak?v=` cat now or go VizLoadFailed\n    if VizSrc starts with `<` go to VizLoadFailed\n    go to VizModuleHere\n\n!   **The sidebar is not the pane, so it is loaded leniently.** Without it the picture still works —\n!   it is a reading aid beside a drawing — so a page that has one file and not the other says so on\n!   the status line and carries on, rather than dropping out of Graph mode over the half of the pane\n!   that is optional. The pane is the opposite case: with no pane there is nothing to look at, so a\n!   page without it leaves the mode entirely.\n!\n!   The default-then-read is `FetchRunText`'s pattern and for its reason: a failure clause ends the\n!   thread, so it has to point at the continuation rather than merely set a value. An empty source\n!   must not reach `run`, which would try to compile nothing.\nLoadSideModule:\n    ! The same two places as the pane, for the same reason and in the same order.\n    put `` into SideSrc\n    attach SideSrcDiv to `editor-side` or go SideModuleLocal\n    put the text of SideSrcDiv into SideSrc\n    if SideSrc is not empty go to SideModuleHere\nSideModuleLocal:\n    rest get SideSrc from `asedit-side.allspeak?v=` cat now or go SideModuleHosted\n    if SideSrc starts with `<` put `` into SideSrc\n    if SideSrc is empty go to SideModuleHosted\nSideModuleHere:\n    if SideSrc is empty go to SideLoadFailed\n    run SideSrc as SideModule\n    put 1 into SideLoaded\n    return\n\nSideModuleHosted:\n    rest get SideSrc from `https://allspeak.ai/dist/asedit-side.allspeak?v=` cat now or go SideLoadFailed\n    if SideSrc starts with `<` go to SideLoadFailed\n    go to SideModuleHere\n\nSideLoadFailed:\n    ! **Not cleared after three seconds, unlike every other message here.** A module that will not load is\n    ! a fault in the page rather than news about the work, and the reader needs it to still be there when\n    ! they look up from the pointer — which is exactly what went wrong the first time this path was hit:\n    ! the sidebar was not beside the editor, the line flashed and went, and the only symptom was that\n    ! nothing happened when a mark was clicked.\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `The Graph sidebar could not be loaded`\n    log `sidebar: could not fetch ` cat `asedit-side.allspeak` cat ` — is it beside the editor?`\n    return\n\n!   A page served without the pane beside it, which is the one way this can fail. Said on the status\n!   line and rectified in the same breath: the editor goes back to the flat view rather than leaving\n!   a pane up that nothing will ever draw in.\nVizLoadFailed:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `The Graph pane could not be loaded`\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    gosub to DoExitGraph\n    stop\n\n!   The run, as one dict — the whole of the interface in this direction. Built here rather than\n!   filled in place because the pane reads its fields by name, so a field added on one side is\n!   visible on the other.\n!\n!   **The `GraphMode` guard is not belt and braces.** Both fetches above hand control back to the event\n!   loop, so the reader can leave the pane — or switch to another file — while this is still waiting\n!   for the recording. Sending `open` then would leave the pane answering a pointer it no longer has,\n!   with the editor in flat mode and nothing left to send it the close.\nSendRunToViz:\n    if GraphMode is 0 return\n    put `{}` into VizRun\n    set property `open` of VizRun to 1\n    set property `trace` of VizRun to TraceText\n    set property `source` of VizRun to SourceText\n    set property `path` of VizRun to TracePath\n    set property `call` of VizRun to StrFlowCall\n    set property `jump` of VizRun to StrFlowJump\n    set property `return` of VizRun to StrFlowReturn\n    send VizRun to VizModule\n    return\n\n!   Leaving puts the flat editor back and relabels the button, so the pane's state is legible from\n!   the button alone. The pane is told it is off screen — the only thing about it this side can know\n!   and it cannot — because its gestures are registered on the pane *and* on the document, and a drag\n!   anywhere in the editor would otherwise be offered to a picture nobody is looking at.\nDoExitGraph:\n    put 0 into GraphMode\n    ! The grip's flag goes with the mode. It is cleared on every drop, so this is only belt and braces —\n    ! but a press that ended outside the window leaves it set, and the next drag in the flat editor would\n    ! otherwise be offered to a splitter nobody can see.\n    put 0 into GripPicked\n    set the content of GraphBtn to `Graph`\n    set style `display` of GraphArea to `none`\n    set style `display` of EditorArea to `block`\n    if VizLoaded is 1 gosub to TellVizClosed\n    if SideLoaded is 1 gosub to TellSideClosed\n    return\n\nTellVizClosed:\n    put `{}` into VizRun\n    set property `open` of VizRun to 0\n    send VizRun to VizModule\n    return\n\n!   Turn off every mode there is. The panes are alternatives, not layers, so each one's entry point\n!   has to turn the others off before turning itself on — and the list of \"the others\" belongs in one\n!   place, or the next pane added is missed by whichever entrant was written before it existed. That\n!   is exactly what happened to `EnterBlocks` and the Graph pane, and it showed as the Graph button\n!   still reading `Edit` (two buttons offering to leave a pane neither of which was on) with the\n!   graph left lying under the Blocks pane.\n!\n!   Called by `EnterBlocks`, `EnterGraph` and `ActivateTab`. A fourth pane means changing this and\n!   nothing else.\nDoExitPanes:\n    if BlocksMode is 1 gosub to DoExitBlocks\n    if GraphMode is 1 gosub to DoExitGraph\n    return\n!! @hash ecd231d6\n!! @verified ecd231d6\n!!!\n!! The sidebar: what the pane reports back, and what the sidebar is shown.\n!!\n!! **The pane cannot look a line up, and this file already can.** A doc block is prose about a *line*; the click names the line; and the model that knows which block holds it is the one Blocks mode is built from. So the click travels here rather than the model travelling there — which is also why the editor, and not the pane, knows that a sidebar exists at all.\n!!\n!! **`on message` is this file's first inbound message.** Everything else here is driven by the reader clicking one of the editor's own buttons; this is the one path that arrives from another program, and it arrives on the pane's pick. **The registration itself is not here** — a handler runs only if its line is executed, this file stops before this block, and the registration is in its own block above, where the flow reaches it.\n!!\n!! **The line is looked up rather than the block searched for.** `SecStart` and `SecEnd` already bound every section, so \"which block holds line N\" is a walk over them. A line in *no* block is an answer too, and the sidebar is told so rather than being left showing the last block it had. **And the model is built on the way in**, by the same `ParseSource` Blocks mode uses, because it used to be built *only* there: a reader who opened a run without ever opening Blocks had no sections at all — or, worse, the ones left behind by the last file that was blocked, which is the kind of stale answer that reads as a working panel.\nSideMarkLine:\n    if GraphMode is 0 stop\n    ! **The report arrives as a dict, so its line is read as a property and never converted.** Two things\n    ! are being avoided here, and both were live faults in the first cut: `put the message into SideSel`\n    ! leaves a dict in a variable the look-up does arithmetic on, and `add 0 to` it throws rather than\n    ! coercing; and the other tempting spelling, `the value of the message`, is `parseInt` of its argument\n    ! — which on a dict is *0 for every click*, so the panel faithfully reported that line 0 is in no doc\n    ! block. The one honest spelling is the property, because the property is what the pane set.\n    put the message into SideIn\n    put property `line` of SideIn into SideSel\n    ! The mark's own figures, which are the pane's to count and this file's only to pass on. They are read\n    ! here rather than in `SendBlockToSide` because they belong to the *click* and not to the block: a line\n    ! in no doc block still has a visit number, and the status bar shows it either way.\n    put property `visit` of SideIn into SideVisit\n    put property `total` of SideIn into SideTotal\n    ! Captured values, if the script asked for any. An absent property reads as nothing at all, so a press on\n    ! a mark with no `@show` beside it leaves this empty and the sidebar prints only the line and the visit.\n    put property `values` of SideIn into SideValues\n    gosub to SideFindBlock\n    gosub to SendBlockToSide\n    stop\n\nSideFindBlock:\n    ! A line lies in at most one block, so the walk stops at the first it finds. The bounds are the\n    ! block's own two documented lines — its opener and its terminator — and the code the click landed\n    ! on is between them, which is why both tests are inclusive.\n    !\n    ! The heading is the block's first prose line, numbered as the Blocks view numbers it, because that\n    ! number is how a reader gets from the sidebar back to the same block in the other pane.\n    put 0 into SideFound\n    put `` into SideTitle\n    put `` into SideProse\n    put 0 into SideI\n    while SideI is less than SecCount\n    begin\n        index SecStart to SideI\n        put SecStart into From\n        index SecEnd to SideI\n        put SecEnd into To\n        if SideSel is not less than From and SideSel is not greater than To\n        begin\n            put 1 into SideFound\n            index SecProse to SideI\n            put SecProse into SideProse\n            put the position of newline in SideProse into NL\n            if NL is less than 0 put SideProse into SideTitle\n            else put left NL of SideProse into SideTitle\n            put SideI into Tmp\n            add 1 to Tmp\n            put Tmp cat `. ` cat SideTitle into SideTitle\n        end\n        add 1 to SideI\n    end\n    return\n\nSendBlockToSide:\n    if SideLoaded is 0\n    begin\n        log `sidebar: not loaded, so the block for line ` cat SideSel cat ` has nowhere to go`\n        return\n    end\n    log `sidebar: line ` cat SideSel cat ` visit ` cat SideVisit cat ` of ` cat SideTotal cat ` found=` cat SideFound cat ` title=` cat SideTitle\n    put `{}` into SideMsg\n    set property `kind` of SideMsg to `block`\n    set property `line` of SideMsg to SideSel\n    set property `visit` of SideMsg to SideVisit\n    set property `total` of SideMsg to SideTotal\n    if SideValues is not empty set property `values` of SideMsg to SideValues\n    set property `found` of SideMsg to SideFound\n    set property `title` of SideMsg to SideTitle\n    set property `prose` of SideMsg to SideProse\n    send SideMsg to SideModule\n    return\n\n!   The run itself, sent beside the pane's own copy of it, because the sidebar is told about a run\n!   whether or not a mark is ever clicked — and a new run has to clear whatever block the last one\n!   was showing, which is why this carries nothing but the script's name.\nSendRunToSide:\n    if SideLoaded is 0 return\n    put `{}` into SideMsg\n    set property `kind` of SideMsg to `run`\n    set property `open` of SideMsg to 1\n    set property `path` of SideMsg to TracePath\n    send SideMsg to SideModule\n    return\n\nTellSideClosed:\n    put `{}` into SideMsg\n    set property `kind` of SideMsg to `run`\n    set property `open` of SideMsg to 0\n    send SideMsg to SideModule\n    return\n!! @hash 0c4a0949\n!! @verified 0c4a0949\n!!!\n!! Recording a run, from the button.\n!!\n!! **This is the trigger, and the pane's empty state is its argument.** A pane that says *\"no recording beside this script\"* names a file and leaves the reader to produce one at a console; this makes it instead — the buffer is already here, and so is the plugin, which the page loads for the pane's sake.\n!!\n!! **The recording is written beside the script, not handed straight to the pane**, and that is the pane's own decision rather than a convenience: `<script>.viz.json` is a file, so a run captured here travels with the project and can be read again without running anything. It is written through the `/write/` route a tab already uses, so nothing needs a new channel.\n!!\n!! **A run that waits is recorded as far as its first wait, and says so.** `wait` hands the rest of the program to a timer, so a capture made inside a call cannot follow it; the flag the command answers with is what the status line reports, rather than a slice being passed off as a whole run. Following one through is a different shape — the caller being *told* when the run ends — and it is recorded in TODO.md.\n!!\n!! **And a script that names an app is opened rather than run.** `@app parser.html` means the script is driven by its own page, so running it here could only ever fail — its markers are reached by a *person* clicking, not by the script starting. Record opens that page, arms it from outside and saves the recording when the window closes; the block below that is the script path, unchanged. The two acts are one button because the script already says which is right.\n!!\n!! **The status line says what the recording *is*, not just that one was made.** A document holding nothing but its header is still a valid document, so a button reading only the trace reports a run that died on its first statement as a success — and the two ways a recording comes back empty are worth telling apart, because one is a script this runtime cannot compile and the other is a script that stopped. So the plugin answers with a sentence — `12 visits in 1 window`, or `could not run: I don't understand 'dictionary' at line 46` — and it is shown as it stands.\n!!\n!! **And a guard is armed, with the recorder's own defaults.** A script handed to the editor by a tool is not to be trusted with the editor's responsiveness: a busy loop and a never-returning wait both end here, and the recording says which.\nRecordRun:\n    if TabCount is 0 stop\n    index TabPath to ActiveTab\n    put TabPath into EditorPath\n    ! A buffer that has never been saved has nowhere to put a recording, and the pane's message names the file it\n    ! would look for — so say the same thing here rather than record beside a name that is not there.\n    if EditorPath is empty\n    begin\n        set the content of StatusSpan to `Save this script first: a recording goes beside it`\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    codemirror get content of ContentEditor into RecordSource\n    put EditorPath cat `.viz.json` into TracePath\n    ! **One button, two acts, and the script says which is right.** A script that names an app runs *as* an app —\n    ! driven by its own page, clicked by a person — so it cannot be run here: the editor opens it instead and arms\n    ! it from outside, and the recording is written when that window closes. Anything else is a script, and Record\n    ! runs it here as it always has. The parse is what knows the difference, and it is the same parse the Blocks\n    ! view uses, so `@app parser.html` means the same thing on both sides of the editor.\n    gosub to ParseSource\n    ! **The flavour is the plugin's answer, not a second reading of the same facts.** The `flavour |` record\n    ! comes from the plugin's own resolution — the script's marker, then the project's `#editor-runtime`, then\n    ! `js` — so the editor and the thing that actually records cannot disagree. This file used to read\n    ! `#editor-runtime` itself and fall back to it when the tab said nothing: two implementations of one rule,\n    ! and the reason the marker's case was invisible here is that the *attributes* it used to be read from do\n    ! not exist on a script this runtime cannot compile. See `NoteFlavour`.\n    ! **A script for the Python runtime is run by the project's server, and not here.** The editor is a page, so\n    ! it has the JavaScript runtime and nothing else — which is why Record refused a `@py` script before this.\n    ! The dev server has Python: `/record/<script>` runs it exactly as `allspeak <script>` would, puts what it\n    ! printed in `<script>-stdout.txt` beside it (a script's output is unpredictable and would drown the console\n    ! the server is also writing to) and the recording in `<script>.viz.json`, which is the file the pane\n    ! already looks for. The server composes the sentence, so the file's name and the verdict are named once.\n    if TabFlavour is `py`\n    begin\n        rest get RecordReply from `/record/` cat EditorPath or begin\n            set the content of StatusSpan to `Could not run ` cat EditorPath cat ` — the server did not answer`\n            fork to ClearStatus\n            stop\n        end\n        set the content of StatusSpan to RecordReply\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    if AppPage is not empty\n    begin\n        record the app at AppPage to TracePath\n        ! **Short, true, and out of the way in three seconds.** The message names no path: the pane does, when\n        ! it reads the recording, and the toolbar is not the place for a filename. It used to say \"Recording\n        ! ... — close its window and the recording is saved to ...\", which was both a promise the editor cannot\n        ! keep (it printed whether or not a window opened) and the longest thing in the toolbar — Graham's two\n        ! objections, in one line. A failure is the plugin's to report, in the console, where it names which of\n        ! the two things went wrong.\n        set the content of StatusSpan to StrRecording\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n        stop\n    end\n    record the script in EditorPath as RecordSource giving RecordTrace reporting RecordVerdict\n    ! An empty trace is how a run that never got started comes back — a script this runtime cannot compile, most\n    ! often — and the verdict is the reason, in the runtime's own words. It names the word and the line, which is\n    ! what tells a reader that this is not the runtime the script was written for.\n    if RecordTrace is empty\n    begin\n        set the content of StatusSpan to `Could not record this script — ` cat RecordVerdict\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    rest post RecordTrace to `/write/` cat TracePath or go RecordFailed\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Recorded ` cat TracePath cat ` — ` cat RecordVerdict\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    ! The pane, if it is up, is drawing whatever recording was there before this one.\n    if GraphMode is 1 gosub to SendRunToViz\n    stop\n\nRecordFailed:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Could not record this script`\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\n!! @hash 08c20866\n!! @verified df567fa6\n!!!\n!! Opening the project's app, from the editor.\n!!\n!! **The app is a page, and a page the editor can already reach: its own.** `@app parser.html` names it relative to where the editor is served from — the project root — so no path has to be assembled, no server route is added, and the page resolves against the editor's own location. It opens in a window of its own, which is the point: the app runs exactly as it does when you open it by hand, with its own layout, its own DOM and its own `rest get`s, unconstrained by the editor's.\n!!\n!! **Nothing about the app changes, and that is deliberate.** `parser.html` fetches its script and runs it; the editor does not run it, poke it, or wrap it. That is what keeps this half simple, and it is also what makes the *next* half — recording a launched app — a separate problem: the recorder has to be armed before the app's own `viz start` line runs, which means the app page carrying a bootstrap or the editor injecting one before its scripts run. Launching needs neither.\n!!\n!! **A script that names no app says so** rather than the button doing nothing, because \"nothing happened\" is the one report a reader cannot act on. The message names the spelling, says it goes on a line of its own, and says it is not a comment — the three things a reader needs and the three the first version got wrong: it pointed at a doc block, which is where the *analyser's* `@hash` and `@verified` live and where an attribute never reaches the compiler.\nLaunchApp:\n    if TabCount is 0 stop\n    ! The parse is what reads the attributes, and the buffer may have changed since the last one — so read it now\n    ! rather than trusting `AppPage` to be current. It is the same walk the Blocks view uses, and it clears\n    ! `AppPage` first, so an attribute that has just been deleted cannot go on launching a page.\n    gosub to ParseSource\n    if AppPage is empty\n    begin\n        ! **The message says where, and that is the whole of its job.** It used to say \"to a doc block\", which is\n        ! where an attribute must *not* go: a `@`-line inside a `!!` block is the analyser's metadata and never\n        ! reaches the compiler, so a reader who followed that advice would have written something the program\n        ! cannot see. Graham asked which it was, which is the message failing at the one moment it exists for.\n        ! **The example is a real filename, not `<page>`, and that is not a style choice.** `set the content of`\n        ! writes `innerHTML` (`Browser.js`), so a placeholder in angle brackets is parsed as an HTML tag and\n        ! never rendered: the reader saw `add '@app ' on a line of its own` — the spelling this sentence exists\n        ! to give them, gone. Measured 2026-10-05, from Graham's own status line, and the check below could not\n        ! see it because the harness's stub stores the string it is given rather than parsing it.\n        set the content of StatusSpan to `No app named: add '@app mypage.html' on a line of its own — a script attribute, not a comment`\n        fork to ClearStatus\n        stop\n    end\n    location new AppPage\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `Opened ` cat AppPage\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    stop\n!! @hash def5bda2\n!! @verified 0faead49\n!!!\n","asedit.json":"{\n  \"$OpenBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-open\",\n    \"#content\": \"Open\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\"\n  },\n  \"$TabBar\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-tabbar\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"gap\": \"2px\",\n    \"overflow-x\": \"auto\",\n    \"min-width\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$PlusBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"img\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-plusbtn\",\n    \"@src\": \"https://allspeak.ai/icon/plus.png\",\n    \"width\": \"20px\",\n    \"height\": \"20px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$TabGroup\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-tabgroup\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"flex\": \"1\",\n    \"align-items\": \"center\",\n    \"min-width\": \"0\",\n    \"#\": [\"$TabBar\", \"$PlusBtn\"]\n  },\n  \"$BlocksBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocksbtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Blocks\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$GraphBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-graphbtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Graph\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$RecordBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-recordbtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Record\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$LaunchBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-launchbtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Launch\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$FindBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-findbtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Find\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 12px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$StatusSpan\": {\n    \"#element\": \"span\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-status\",\n    \"color\": \"lightgreen\",\n    \"font-size\": \"0.85em\",\n    \"padding\": \"0 8px\",\n    \"white-space\": \"nowrap\",\n    \"min-width\": \"6em\",\n    \"text-align\": \"right\"\n  },\n  \"$Logo\": {\n    \"#element\": \"img\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-logo\",\n    \"@src\": \"https://allspeak.ai/icon/duckthink.png\",\n    \"width\": \"28px\",\n    \"height\": \"28px\",\n    \"border-radius\": \"6px\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$TopBar\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-topbar\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"align-items\": \"center\",\n    \"background\": \"#2d2d2d\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px\",\n    \"gap\": \"4px\",\n    \"#\": [\"$Logo\", \"$OpenBtn\", \"$TabGroup\", \"$StatusSpan\", \"$BlocksBtn\", \"$GraphBtn\", \"$RecordBtn\", \"$LaunchBtn\", \"$FindBtn\"]\n  },\n  \"$ContentEditor\": {\n    \"#element\": \"textarea\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-textarea\",\n    \"width\": \"100%\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\"\n  },\n  \"$ContentDiv\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"width\": \"100%\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"#\": [\"$ContentEditor\"]\n  },\n  \"$EditorArea\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-editor-area\",\n    \"height\": \"calc(100vh - 40px)\",\n    \"#\": [\"$ContentDiv\"]\n  },\n  \"$BlocksTitle\": {\n    \"#element\": \"span\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-title\",\n    \"flex\": \"1\",\n    \"color\": \"#ddd\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksPrev\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-prev\",\n    \"#content\": \"▲\",\n    \"padding\": \"2px 10px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksNext\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-next\",\n    \"#content\": \"▼\",\n    \"padding\": \"2px 10px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksBadge\": {\n    \"#element\": \"span\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-badge\",\n    \"padding\": \"2px 8px\",\n    \"border-radius\": \"3px\",\n    \"font-size\": \"0.85em\",\n    \"background\": \"#555\",\n    \"color\": \"white\",\n    \"min-width\": \"10em\",\n    \"text-align\": \"center\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksVerifyBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-verify\",\n    \"#content\": \"Mark verified\",\n    \"padding\": \"2px 10px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksVerifyAllBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-verify-all\",\n    \"#content\": \"Mark all verified\",\n    \"padding\": \"2px 10px\",\n    \"cursor\": \"pointer\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksToolbar\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-toolbar\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"align-items\": \"center\",\n    \"gap\": \"8px\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 8px\",\n    \"background\": \"#3a3a3a\",\n    \"color\": \"#ddd\",\n    \"font-size\": \"0.9em\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\",\n    \"#\": [\"$BlocksTitle\", \"$BlocksPrev\", \"$BlocksNext\", \"$BlocksBadge\", \"$BlocksVerifyBtn\", \"$BlocksVerifyAllBtn\"]\n  },\n  \"$BlocksToc\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-toc\",\n    \"width\": \"200px\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"overflow-y\": \"auto\",\n    \"background\": \"#262626\",\n    \"color\": \"#ccc\",\n    \"font-family\": \"system-ui, sans-serif\",\n    \"font-size\": \"12px\",\n    \"padding\": \"4px 0\",\n    \"border-right\": \"1px solid #444\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\",\n    \"box-sizing\": \"border-box\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksCodePane\": {\n    \"#element\": \"textarea\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-code\",\n    \"flex\": \"0 0 60%\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"font-family\": \"monospace\",\n    \"font-size\": \"13px\",\n    \"border\": \"none\",\n    \"padding\": \"8px\",\n    \"box-sizing\": \"border-box\",\n    \"resize\": \"none\",\n    \"outline\": \"none\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksDivider\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-divider\",\n    \"width\": \"6px\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"cursor\": \"col-resize\",\n    \"background\": \"#bbb\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksDocPane\": {\n    \"#element\": \"textarea\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-doc\",\n    \"flex\": \"1 1 0\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"font-family\": \"Georgia, serif\",\n    \"font-size\": \"14px\",\n    \"border\": \"none\",\n    \"padding\": \"8px\",\n    \"box-sizing\": \"border-box\",\n    \"resize\": \"none\",\n    \"outline\": \"none\",\n    \"background\": \"#f8f8f5\"\n  },\n  \"$BlocksSplit\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-split\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"flex\": \"1\",\n    \"overflow\": \"hidden\",\n    \"#\": [\"$BlocksToc\", \"$BlocksCodePane\", \"$BlocksDivider\", \"$BlocksDocPane\"]\n  },\n  \"$GraphHost\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-graph-host\",\n    \"flex\": \"1\",\n    \"min-width\": \"0\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\"\n  },\n  \"$GraphSide\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-graph-side\",\n    \"width\": \"340px\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"box-sizing\": \"border-box\",\n    \"background\": \"#f8f8f5\",\n    \"color\": \"#222\",\n    \"overflow\": \"hidden\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"flex-direction\": \"column\"\n  },\n  \"$GraphGrip\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-graph-grip\",\n    \"width\": \"6px\",\n    \"flex-shrink\": \"0\",\n    \"height\": \"100%\",\n    \"cursor\": \"col-resize\",\n    \"background\": \"#bbb\"\n  },\n  \"$GraphArea\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-graph-area\",\n    \"display\": \"none\",\n    \"flex-direction\": \"row\",\n    \"height\": \"calc(100vh - 40px)\",\n    \"#\": [\"$GraphHost\", \"$GraphGrip\", \"$GraphSide\"]\n  },\n  \"$BlocksArea\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-blocks-area\",\n    \"display\": \"none\",\n    \"flex-direction\": \"column\",\n    \"height\": \"calc(100vh - 40px)\",\n    \"#\": [\"$BlocksToolbar\", \"$BlocksSplit\"]\n  },\n  \"$Scroller\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-scroller\",\n    \"flex\": \"1\",\n    \"overflow-y\": \"auto\",\n    \"margin-bottom\": \"0.5em\",\n    \"min-height\": \"100px\"\n  },\n  \"$CloseBtn\": {\n    \"#element\": \"button\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-closebtn\",\n    \"#content\": \"Close\"\n  },\n  \"$FileListing\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"position\": \"absolute\",\n    \"width\": \"50%\",\n    \"max-height\": \"70%\",\n    \"background\": \"white\",\n    \"padding\": \"1em\",\n    \"top\": \"50%\",\n    \"left\": \"50%\",\n    \"transform\": \"translateX(-50%) translateY(-50%)\",\n    \"display\": \"flex\",\n    \"flex-direction\": \"column\",\n    \"border-radius\": \"4px\",\n    \"#\": [\"$Scroller\", \"$CloseBtn\"]\n  },\n  \"$Overlay\": {\n    \"#element\": \"div\",\n    \"@id\": \"se-overlay\",\n    \"display\": \"none\",\n    \"position\": \"fixed\",\n    \"top\": \"0\",\n    \"left\": \"0\",\n    \"width\": \"100vw\",\n    \"height\": \"100vh\",\n    \"background\": \"rgba(0,0,0,0.5)\",\n    \"z-index\": \"100\",\n    \"#\": [\"$FileListing\"]\n  },\n  \"#\": [\"$TopBar\", \"$EditorArea\", \"$BlocksArea\", \"$GraphArea\", \"$Overlay\"]\n}\n","asedit-graph.allspeak":"!! asedit-graph.allspeak — the editor's Graph pane, as a companion module.\n!! **The vocabulary the picture is built from is the script's own, where the script offers one.** An attribute is a note a script writes about itself and the runtime ignores — text for a tool — so the recorder reads two keys and the pane shows what they collect: `@viz start` / `@viz stop` name the window in the script's own words, and `@show Total, Row` says which values are worth watching at that arrival, which reach the sidebar beside the line and visit of the mark they belong to. Nothing else is read, so a note written for a person or for another tool passes both by.\n!!\n!!\n!! The pane draws a run: a recording sitting beside a script, read as a sequence, as an arrival count per line, and as the flow between the marks. That is half of the editor by size, and none of it is needed until somebody looks at a run — so it is a module, fetched, compiled and run on the first click on **Graph**, and messaged from then on. The `svg` vocabulary is here rather than in the editor, and this is where the plugin is needed: the pane is compiled when it is fetched, which is why the page loads the plugin whether or not a run is ever opened.\n!!\n!! What it owns. Everything inside the pane, and the pane itself. The drawing and its state, the four gestures (the wheel scrolls the lines, shift-wheel zooms the lines, control-wheel the steps, a drag pans), the label names down the right-hand edge, the source drawn behind the heat, the key, the caption, the estimate shown before a long draw and the calibration file that estimate is learned from, and the status line for as long as it has something to say on it.\n!!\n!! What crosses the boundary, and what does not. One dict in: the run's text, the script's text, the three localised flow words, and the path the recording was expected at — the last only so that \"no recording beside this script\" can name the file it looked for. The gestures do not cross: pan and zoom are registered here, on the pane, because a message per drag event would put a round trip inside every pointer move. Nor does the pane's visibility, which is the editor's mode system to run — the editor says whether this pane is on screen, and the module's own `VizShown` is what its handlers consult, because that is the one thing it cannot see for itself. **One small dict goes the other way**, and only on a press: which line the mark under the pointer names, and — because a row of dots otherwise says the same thing however far along it a reader points — **which arrival that dot is on its line, and how many that line had**. The count is the one the dot's own colour is drawn from, so a reader comparing a pale dot with a red one in the same row is reading the number the report gives them. What a line *means* is the editor's model and the sidebar's to show, so the pane reports and decides nothing about it.\n!!\n!! Acquisition stays with the editor. The recording and the script are fetched there, because the editor is the one that knows which tab is open; this module fetches only what is its own, the calibration file, and never executes anything in order to draw a run.\n!   -- the module, and the pane it owns --\n\n    script ASEditorGraph\n!! @hash a078a83d\n!! @verified a078a83d\n!!!\n!! What the module holds from the editor's page, and the state the boundary is kept in.\n!!\n!! The pane and the status line are attached **by id** — `asedit.json` names both, and the editor does the same `attach` for its own elements — so the module has handles on the page without the editor declaring or creating a single element for it. Everything else here is either the run as it arrived, a number learned by drawing, or the three localised flow words the editor's string table holds.\n!   -- the page, by id --\n    div VizHost\n    span StatusSpan\n!   -- the run, and what was learned about drawing it --\n    variable VizTrace\n    ! Declared up here rather than beside the drawing's own variables: `VizDrawRun` asks for a draw and\n    ! reads this straight afterwards, and the compiler is single-pass, so a use may not come before the\n    ! declaration that introduces it. (This is the second time in this file that has bitten.)\n    variable VizPending\n    variable VizSource         ! the script, drawn behind the heat\n    variable TracePath         ! where the recording was looked for, so a miss can name it\n    variable TraceSize         ! bytes of the recording, which is what predicts the drawing time\n    variable DrawStarted       ! `now` when the drawing began\n    variable DrawMillis        ! how long it took\n    ! **The readout is an `svgtext` in the drawing, declared here rather than with the other shapes below.**\n    ! It was a `div` on the host and it landed at the top left of the *window*, over the editor's toolbar —\n    ! a `div` positioned absolutely goes to the nearest positioned ancestor, and the host is not one. In the\n    ! drawing it cannot escape, and the foot is where a reader looks for what the pane is saying. It is\n    ! declared here because the code that writes it is up in the module's plumbing, above the shape block,\n    ! and the compiler is single-pass: a name has to be declared above the line that uses it.\n    svgtext VizBusy\n    variable VizWarned         ! 1 when the warning on the editor's status line was ours to clear\n    variable VizBusyText       ! what the corner says while a drawing is under way, and after it\n    variable VizBusySecs       ! the drawing's own seconds, whole part\n    variable VizBusyTenths     ! ...and its tenths: the language has no fractions, and a corner that rounded\n                               ! a drawing of seven hundred milliseconds to `0 s` would be worse than none\n    variable VizEstimate       ! what the last drawing's rate says this one will cost, in seconds; 0 = no rate\n                               ! yet. Declared here with the host's own arithmetic rather than with the view's\n                               ! symbols because `VizPredictJudged` writes it and the compiler is single-pass:\n                               ! a declaration below that line is not there when the line runs.\n    variable VizMillisPerKb    ! learned: milliseconds per kilobyte of recording, on this machine\n    variable VizCalib\n    variable VizCalibParsed\n    variable VizPredicted\n    variable VizCalibText\n!   -- the flow words, as the editor's string table has them --\n    variable StrFlowCall\n    variable StrFlowJump\n    variable StrFlowReturn\n!   -- the boundary's own state --\n    variable VizRun            ! the dict the editor sent, as it arrived\n    variable VizShown          ! 1 while the editor has this pane on screen\n    ! What a drag is holding. Declared with the gestures rather than with the rest of the view's state\n    ! because the handlers below are the first thing in the file to read it, and a variable has to be\n    ! declared before the code that uses it.\n    variable VizSlide          ! 0 the picture, 1 the lines bar, 2 the steps bar\n    ! **A flag for the clip's own state, declared and set here because the plumbing can do that.** The\n    ! clip's own names are declared far below — after the module has already stopped — so the *first* draw\n    ! is the earliest place they can be given values, and this flag is how it knows it is the first.\n    ! Setting it here is legal exactly because its declaration is here too; after the plumbing's `stop`,\n    ! nothing is reachable at load.\n    variable VizClipInit\n    put 0 into VizClipInit\n!! @hash 1f8c29b2\n!! @verified a04b40d6\n!!!\n!! The pane is the module's from the moment it loads, and the gestures come with it.\n!!\n!! A handler belongs to the program that wants it, so these are registered here rather than wired from the editor: the window the gestures move is this module's own state, and the pointer events arrive many times a second — a message per event would put a round trip inside every move. `on drag` and `on drop` are document-level because a drag that begins on the pane continues wherever the pointer goes; the editor registers its own pair for the Blocks divider, and each ignores the mode it is not in.\n!!\n!! The pane's visibility is the one thing a module cannot see for itself, which is what `VizShown` is for: the editor turns it on and off with a message, and every handler below consults it.\n!   -- the pane, and the line that says what is happening --\n    attach VizHost to `se-graph-host`\n    attach StatusSpan to `se-status`\n!   -- the gestures --\n    on pick VizHost\n    begin\n        if VizShown is 0 return\n        gosub to VizGrab\n    end\n    on wheel VizHost\n    begin\n        if VizShown is 0 return\n        gosub to VizWheel\n    end\n    on drag\n    begin\n        if VizShown is 1 gosub to VizDrag\n    end\n    on drop\n    begin\n        if VizShown is 1 gosub to VizRelease\n    end\n\n!! @hash 06bab40f\n!! @verified 10f33ffc\n!!!\n!! The boundary: one message in, and the module parks until the next one.\n!!\n!! `release parent` is what lets the editor's `run` return — the module is loaded, attached and listening before the editor sends its first run, and from then on the two only ever meet at a message.\n    on message go to VizOpen\n    release parent\n    stop\n!! @hash 47612d91\n!! @verified 47612d91\n!!!\n!! Drawing a run that has arrived, and saying what it cost.\n!!\n!! What a recording will cost is said **before** the wait rather than discovered during it: a draw of a big run takes tens of seconds, and a pane that sits empty for that long reads as a fault, while a line that says what is happening costs nothing. The two constants are the whole of the judgement, and nothing is said on a first run — there is no measurement yet, and a guess would be worse than silence. What it actually cost is remembered at the end, overwritten rather than averaged: a machine's speed is not a thing to smooth, and the last measurement is the only evidence there is.\n!!\n!! The status line is the module's own during the wait, so the three-second clear is its own copy of the editor's: this one clears this message and nothing else.\nVizDrawRun:\n    ! **A recording that is not there is a state to draw, not a reason to stop drawing.** This used to jump\n    ! away to `GraphNone`, which named the missing file on the status line and returned — leaving the pane\n    ! showing whichever recording it drew last. Two tabs a click apart then showed one recording, which is the\n    ! confusion a reader cannot see through: the picture looks like an answer and belongs to another file.\n    ! Taking the notice as a *subroutine* keeps the naming and lets the draw carry on, so the empty state is\n    ! drawn — the script plain, no arrivals — and `GraphNone` is still the one place that says why.\n    if VizTrace is empty gosub to GraphNone\n    put the length of VizTrace into TraceSize\n    gosub to VizPredict\n    ! Said, and given a frame to be painted in, *before* the clock starts — so the twenty milliseconds the\n    ! browser takes for that frame is neither charged to the drawing nor counted into the rate the pane\n    ! learns from it.\n    gosub to VizBusyNotice\n    gosub to VizRequest\n    ! A request that had to wait is drawn after this entry has gone, so it is not timed here: timing it\n    ! would record the turn-round of a queue as the cost of a draw, and the estimate that learning\n    ! feeds is only worth having if it is a draw's own time.\n    if VizPending is 1 return\n    gosub to VizBusyDone\n    gosub to VizRemember\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    return\n\nClearStatus:\n    wait 3 seconds\n    set the content of StatusSpan to ``\n    stop\n\n!! @hash 06d18b21\n!! @verified 5b1f9f95\n!!!\n!! The module's one message: take the run, or stand down.\n!!\n!! `open` 0 is the editor saying the pane is no longer on screen, which is the module's cue to leave the pointer alone; 1 carries the run itself. The flow words arrive with it because they are the editor's string table's, localised for the file's language, and the path arrives so that a recording that is not there can be named rather than merely missed.\n!!\n!! This is the only place the two programs meet, and the dict is the whole of the interface — so a field added here is the whole of the change on both sides.\nVizOpen:\n    put the message into VizRun\n    if property `open` of VizRun is 0\n        begin\n        put 0 into VizShown\n        stop\n        end\n    put property `trace` of VizRun into VizTrace\n    put property `source` of VizRun into VizSource\n    put property `path` of VizRun into TracePath\n    put property `call` of VizRun into StrFlowCall\n    put property `jump` of VizRun into StrFlowJump\n    put property `return` of VizRun into StrFlowReturn\n    put 1 into VizShown\n    gosub to VizDrawRun\n    stop\n!   Nothing to draw, and the message names the file that was missing rather than what a marker is.\n!   A `return` rather than a `stop`: the entry called it with `gosub`, and the pane is left on screen\n!   with the status line saying why it is empty.\nGraphNone:\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `No recording beside this script: ` cat TracePath\n    fork to ClearStatus\n    return\n\nVizPredict:\n    if TraceSize is 0 return\n    put 0 into VizMillisPerKb\n    rest get VizCalib from `/read/.viz-calibration.json` or go VizPredictJudged\n    put json of VizCalib into VizCalibParsed\n    put property `millis_per_kb` of VizCalibParsed into VizMillisPerKb\nVizPredictJudged:\n    put 0 into VizEstimate\n    if VizMillisPerKb is 0 return\n    ! Multiply before dividing: the rate is per kilobyte and the recording is counted in bytes.\n    multiply TraceSize by VizMillisPerKb giving VizPredicted\n    divide VizPredicted by 1024\n    if VizPredicted is less than 5000 return\n    divide VizPredicted by 1000\n    ! Kept for the corner as well as used in the sentence below: this is the ETA a reader is shown while the\n    ! picture is being built, and it is a measurement of the last drawing rather than a promise about this one.\n    put VizPredicted into VizEstimate\n    ! Say where the number comes from. It is a measurement of the last drawing, not a promise, and\n    ! a stale one is self-explanatory when the reader knows that — which matters, because a single\n    ! slow run sets the rate and the next run corrects it.\n    !\n    ! **And the advice points at the *view*, not at the script.** It used to send the reader off to narrow\n    ! `viz start`/`viz stop` — which means editing and re-running the script, and answers a different\n    ! question. What they want is less of *this* recording on screen, and that is the clip bar. Graham's\n    ! wording, 2026-10-06.\n    !\n    ! **A line on the editor's status bar, not a dialog box, and that is a fix rather than a preference.**\n    ! It used to be `alert`, which the language hands straight to the browser (`Browser.js`, `Alert`) — so\n    ! the box was the browser's own, its button could be neither greyed nor dismissed on cue, and Graham\n    ! had to click it twice: *\"either the box should disappear or the button should grey out after being\n    ! clicked, it's not apparent that it took the click\"*. Nothing of that is ours to fix in a native\n    ! dialog. The status bar is already the pane's to write to (it names a missing recording there), it is\n    ! visible before the first drawing has built anything, and it needs no acknowledgement — and the\n    ! readout below the picture says `Working - about N seconds` from the moment the drawing starts, so\n    ! the number is not lost with the box.\n    set the content of StatusSpan to `This recording is ` cat TraceSize cat ` bytes - about ` cat VizPredicted cat ` seconds to draw. Drag the clip bar's markers above the picture to narrow the range viewed.`\n    put 1 into VizWarned\n    return\n\n!   And remember what it actually cost, so the next prediction is a measurement rather than a guess.\n!   Overwritten rather than averaged: a machine's speed is not a thing to smooth, and the last\n!   measurement is the only evidence there is.\n!   What the readout says while a drawing is under way, and the `wait` that lets it be read at all.\n!\n!   **It says `Working` in words, and it is coloured to be noticed.** Every drawing that rebuilds the\n!   picture — a marker released, a wheel notch, a script re-read — takes seconds on a recording worth\n!   clipping at all, and a pane that simply goes quiet for that long reads as a pane that has lost the\n!   reader's click. The word comes first because that is the part a reader needs; the estimate follows\n!   because it is the part that says how long to wait.\n!\n!   **Two numbers, and only the second is a measurement.** The estimate is the last drawing's own rate\n!   applied to this recording's size — stale on a machine that has changed — and it is said in seconds\n!   because that is the unit somebody waiting thinks in. With no rate yet the readout says only that a\n!   drawing is happening, which is still the one thing the reader cannot otherwise tell.\n!\n!   **The `wait` is not a delay: it is the whole of how the message is seen.** This script runs on the\n!   browser's own thread, so a line set and then painted over by seconds of drawing would never be read —\n!   `wait` hands the thread back for a frame first. Twenty milliseconds against a drawing that costs\n!   hundreds, and the clock is started after it so the wait is not charged to the rate the pane learns.\nVizBusyNotice:\n    put `Working` into VizBusyText\n    if VizEstimate is greater than 0 put `Working - about ` cat VizEstimate cat ` seconds` into VizBusyText\n    set the text of VizBusy to VizBusyText\n    wait 20 millis\n    return\n\n!   And what it says when the drawing is done, which is the number the reader wanted in the first place.\n!\n!   **In seconds with a tenth, and assembled rather than divided**, because this is where the language's\n!   integer arithmetic shows: `0.7` is not reachable by dividing 700 by 1000, and `0 s` for a drawing of\n!   seven hundred milliseconds would be a worse answer than no corner at all. Whole part and tenth are taken\n!   separately and joined; the tenth is floored, so the number never claims more time than was spent.\nVizBusyDone:\n    ! Only a message of ours: the status bar is the editor's, and a drawing that had nothing to warn about\n    ! must not wipe whatever the editor is saying there.\n    if VizWarned is 1\n    begin\n        put 0 into VizWarned\n        set the content of StatusSpan to ``\n    end\n    if DrawMillis is less than 1\n    begin\n        set the text of VizBusy to ``\n        return\n    end\n    divide DrawMillis by 1000 giving VizBusySecs\n    multiply VizBusySecs by 1000 giving VizBusyTenths\n    take VizBusyTenths from DrawMillis giving VizBusyTenths\n    divide VizBusyTenths by 100 giving VizBusyTenths\n    put `drew in ` cat VizBusySecs into VizBusyText\n    put VizBusyText cat `.` cat VizBusyTenths into VizBusyText\n    set the text of VizBusy to VizBusyText cat ` s`\n    return\n\nVizRemember:\n    if TraceSize is 0 or DrawMillis is less than 1 return\n    multiply DrawMillis by 1024 giving VizPredicted\n    divide VizPredicted by TraceSize giving VizMillisPerKb\n    put `{` cat `\"millis_per_kb\": ` cat VizMillisPerKb cat `}` into VizCalibText\n    rest post VizCalibText to `/write/.viz-calibration.json` or return\n    return\n!! @hash 2aa45248\n!! @verified 3fce0448\n!!!\n!! The view itself: everything from here down is the drawing.\n!!\n!! The third view's drawing, grown from the front. Plot-only on purpose: the editor already renders the script beside it, so this draws what the editor cannot — the sequence, the arrivals, and the flow between them.\n!!\n!! **What it draws.** The script as a picture with the run's arrivals marked on it, the flow drawn between them, a window over the whole that the gestures move, and the instruments that say what the window is showing. It is a *section* rather than a program — the editor gives it a panel and a recording and calls `VizOpen` — and everything it needs is declared below.\n! ---- the view, below here, is what a host runs ----\n!! @hash a34baa58\n!! @verified a34baa58\n!!!\n!! The view's own symbols, all prefixed `Viz…`.\n!! Names are the scarce resource in a flat table of declared symbols — which is not a figure of speech: an earlier version collided with the host's own `Y2` and the compiler refused it as a duplicate. Everything the section declares is therefore `Viz…`, so it can sit inside an editor that has its own drawings, elements and variables.\n!! The panel is this module's own — `VizHost`, attached by id to the editor's page — and the run arrives in `VizTrace` with the script beside it, fetched by the editor and handed over as a dict. The view fetches nothing but its own calibration file, and never executes anything in order to draw a run.\n    variable VizReady        ! set once the elements exist, so a redraw only redraws\n    variable VizParsed\n    variable VizEvents\n    variable VizEvent\n    variable VizArgs\n    variable VizCategory\n    variable VizIndex\n    variable VizLine\n    variable VizSteps\n    variable VizMinLine        ! the picture's first line: 1, because the picture is the whole file\n    variable VizMaxLine        ! and its last: the file's own line count\n    variable VizMaxSteps\n    variable VizSpanLines\n    variable VizDotSize\n    variable VizBreathe\n    variable VizCount\n    variable VizSeen        ! how many times each line has been reached so far, in this draw\n    variable VizPictureNew  ! 1 when this draw's recording or source is not the last one drawn, so the parts\n                             ! of the picture that depend on neither are built here and not on every gesture\n    variable VizRunCounts   ! how often each line is reached in the whole recording\n    variable VizMaxCount    ! the busiest line's total, which the ramp is scaled against\n    variable VizBand\n    variable VizHeatD\n    variable VizHeatE\n    variable VizHeatF\n    variable VizHeatG\n    variable VizKeyX\n    variable VizKeyTextX\n    variable VizKeyPitch\n    variable VizColourCall\n    variable VizColourJump\n    variable VizColourReturn\n    variable VizMargin\n    variable VizPlotW\n    variable VizPlotH\n    variable VizPlotRight\n    variable VizPlotBottom\n    variable VizRowHalf      ! half of VizPlotH: the half row between a line's own y and its row's top\n    variable VizRowY         ! where one row's baseline goes inside the picture: each row names its own\n    ! The flow, one path per kind of movement, because the colour *is* the kind. `branch` — the\n    ! compiler's own `if`/`while`/`wait` jumps — is deliberately not among them: scaffolding is not a\n    ! program jump, and the trace keeps it a separate kind precisely so a reader can be spared it.\n    ! The prototype drew it in grey behind a \"compiler jumps\" toggle; wanted here, that is a fourth\n    ! path and a button.\n    variable VizFlowCallD\n    variable VizFlowJumpD\n    variable VizFlowReturnD\n    ! The arrow head's three corners, computed per transfer — the head is what tells a reader which\n    ! end of a vertical segment is the destination, which at low zoom is otherwise a coin toss.\n    variable VizHeadL\n    variable VizHeadR\n    variable VizHeadY\n    ! One faint rule across the frame for every line the run touched, so that a mark or a transfer's\n    ! end can be placed on a line at low zoom, where the text is a texture and a mark is a dot.\n    variable VizRulesD\n    ! The lines the recording *names* — an arrival, or an end of a transfer — as a padded string, and\n    ! one line of it at a time for the membership test. See `VizRuleAdd`: the language has no set.\n    variable VizRuledLines\n    variable VizRuledLine\n    variable VizRuleLine\n    variable VizRuleEnd\n    variable VizRuleY\n    variable VizRuleX         ! where the rule begins: one em past the end of the line it names\n    variable VizRuleWidth\n    variable VizKind           ! the transfer's kind, while choosing its path\n    variable VizSegment        ! one transfer's path data, segment and head together\n    variable VizX\n    variable VizY\n    variable VizY2\n    variable VizTickStep\n    variable VizXStep\n    variable VizTickValue\n    variable VizTickPos\n    variable VizOutside\n    ! The window on the run that the picture shows, in the trace's own units — steps across, lines\n    ! down. A *window*, not a pair of scale factors: a factor would have to be relative to a fit that\n    ! changes with the recording, where an absolute one sits pinned at one end — the prototype's own\n    ! lesson, the same control reading 51.6 px a step for one window and 0.7 for another. It is\n    ! fitted to the whole recording when a run is opened, so the first picture is the one this pane\n    ! has always drawn; a gesture then moves the window, and nothing else about the drawing changes.\n    variable VizViewX0\n    variable VizViewXW\n    variable VizViewY0\n    variable VizViewYH\n    variable VizViewTemp\n    variable VizViewFit\n    variable VizFitXW\n    variable VizFitYH\n    ! **The clip is a *list of kept step ranges*, not one range.** That is what lets a reader keep either\n    ! the window between the markers *or* the two parts outside it: keeping the inside intersects every\n    ! range with the window, and keeping the outside subtracts it, which splits a range in two. The list\n    ! can only ever cover less — a cut narrows, and nothing but `reset` widens — which is the rule the\n    ! whole control is built on.\n    !\n    ! **A pair of holders rather than a json value, and that is a cost decision.** This list is walked\n    ! once per record, and a json access costs a parse of the whole array (~15ms on a 435KB trace), while\n    ! two array reads and two integer compares are the cheapest thing in this file. `VizKeepN` is how many\n    ! ranges are live; the spare room past it is there because keeping the outside can split one range\n    ! into two, and a list that is rebuilt needs somewhere to be built.\n    variable VizKeepFrom      ! array: each kept range's first step\n    variable VizKeepTo        ! array: each kept range's last step\n    variable VizKeepN         ! how many of them are live\n    variable VizKeepFrom2     ! array: the list being built by a cut, before it replaces the live one\n    variable VizKeepTo2\n    variable VizKeepN2\n    variable VizKeepI         ! scratch: the index over the ranges\n    variable VizKeepA         ! scratch: one range's start, while it is being rewritten\n    variable VizKeepB\n    variable VizKeepKept      ! 1 while a record is inside one of the ranges\n    variable VizKeepTail      ! what a cut keeps: 0 the whole recording, 1 the window, 2 the two parts outside it\n    variable VizKeepSet       ! and what the last cut kept, which is what the notice names\n    variable VizRangeFrom     ! scratch: one range, on its way into the list being built\n    variable VizRangeTo\n    variable VizKeepCount     ! records kept, which is the notice's first figure\n    variable VizKeepCap       ! how many ranges the holders hold: past it a splitting cut keeps less\n    variable VizAllTo         ! the recording's own last step, remembered while the list is empty\n    variable VizSetTag        ! bumped by every cut, so the two places that decide a picture see it\n    variable VizLastSetTag\n    ! **And a history deep enough to undo.** Every cut saves the list it replaced as one string —\n    ! `0-20`, or `0-4;12-20` — so an undo is a split and a draw rather than a re-filter of the recording,\n    ! which is what makes it instant. Strings rather than saved sets: the list is a handful of numbers,\n    ! while a set would be a copy of the recording. `VizHistN` is how many are on it, and it is the\n    ! redo's depth as well, which is why `VizHistAt` is kept beside it rather than being the top.\n    variable VizHist           ! array: the kept list before each cut, as text\n    variable VizHistN\n    variable VizHistAt\n    variable VizHistI          ! scratch over it\n    variable VizHistText       ! scratch: the list as text, and the piece being read back\n    variable VizHistPiece\n    variable VizHistParts     ! scratch: the list split on `;` by the loader\n    variable VizHistPair      ! scratch: one range split on `-` by it\n    ! **The run the picture shows need not start at step 0.** Unclipped it does, which is why these\n    ! read as they always did; clipped, the picture's steps are the clip's own range, so the first\n    ! step is the clip's start and the width is its span. Two names rather than a subtraction at\n    ! every site: the fit, the clamp, the bars and the handle drag all have to agree, and they agree\n    ! by reading the same two numbers. `VizSpanSteps` is set in pass one, where `VizMaxSteps` is\n    ! known; `VizMinSteps` here, where the filter is.\n    variable VizMinSteps\n    variable VizSpanSteps\n    ! The filter's own scratch: the records it kept, and how many of the recording's anchor and\n    ! transfer records it kept and saw — the two figures the notice on the status line carries.\n    variable VizKept\n    variable VizKeptKey     ! the clip the kept set was built for, so only a change rebuilds it\n    variable VizParsedFrom  ! the recording the parse came from, so it is read once and not per draw\n    variable VizClipKept\n    variable VizClipTotal\n    variable VizClipIs        ! 1 while the filter is looking at a record it might keep\n    variable VizStatusText\n    variable VizStatusEnd\n    variable VizStatusZoom\n    variable VizDrawing\n    ! The source behind the heat. It is a *picture* rather than text — one `<image>` for the whole\n    ! run — because an element is a declared symbol, so there cannot be one `svgtext` per line; the\n    ! picture is built once per run and only its geometry moves after that.\n    variable VizSourceLines      ! the script, split into lines by `json split`\n    variable VizSourceCount\n    variable VizSourceLine\n    variable VizSourceIndex\n    variable VizSourceAt\n    ! The recording this window was fitted to. The *same* recording is the same run, and a visit back\n    ! to the pane should find the zoom and the pan where they were left; a different one means the\n    ! window means nothing, and is fitted afresh. This is what replaced the host calling `VizReset` on\n    ! every entry — which was the same thing as throwing the view away each time, and is not what a\n    ! return visit is for.\n    variable VizLastTrace\n    variable VizLastSource    ! and the script the picture was drawn from, for the same reason\n    variable VizNewRun        ! 1 when this draw's recording is not the last one drawn\n    variable VizGlyphBody         ! the picture's rows, escaped and encoded once per run\n    variable VizGlyphWidth        ! the document's natural size, in the units the text is drawn at\n    variable VizGlyphHeight\n    variable VizGlyphEm           ! the em of the text, which is the gap a rule clears before it begins\n    variable VizGlyphRows         ! a window may not go below this: past it the text is at its own size\n    variable VizGlyphMax\n    ! The redaction's own scratch: the path data for the bars, one line's width in document units, and\n    ! where that line's row begins. Built in the same pass as the text it covers, for the reason the text\n    ! is built there at all.\n    variable VizRedactD\n    variable VizRedactW\n    variable VizRedactY\n    ! And the scale that pins the bars' width while the zoom moves: the document's own shrinkage, inverted,\n    ! as thousandths and then as a decimal an SVG `transform` can read.\n    variable VizRedactScale\n    variable VizRedactFactor\n    variable VizRedactText\n    variable VizRedactLength\n    variable VizWindowY\n    variable VizWindowW\n    variable VizWindowH\n    ! The diagnostic's own scratch: the document it builds, the line it is on, where that line lands, and\n    ! the two measurements the copy of the text needs — its size and how far its baseline sits below the\n    ! middle of a row. See the create block for why it exists at all.\n    variable VizLabelBody\n    variable VizLabelLine\n    variable VizLabelEnd\n    variable VizLabelY\n    variable VizLabelSize\n    variable VizLabelDrop\n    variable VizLabelSpace     ! the width a label's name and its one-em gap take, in frame units\n    ! And what `VizLabelCheck` answers: whether the line in `VizSourceLine` is a label, and how long its\n    ! name is — the line without the colon.\n    variable VizIsLabel\n    variable VizLabelLen\n    ! Which axes a zoom notch applies to. Shift is the lines and control is the steps — the modifiers\n    ! decide, so one handler serves both.\n    variable VizZoomAcross\n    variable VizZoomDown\n    ! And the pair they do not: both modifiers together *scroll* the steps, which until now had no way\n    ! of being moved but a drag. It is not a third kind of zoom but a second axis for the scroll, so\n    ! the flag sits with the scroll and `VizWheel` is where the pair is turned into it.\n    variable VizScrollAcross\n    ! The gestures' own scratch. `VizGrabFromX/Y` is where the window stood when the drag began, so\n    ! a pan is measured from the grab rather than accumulated step by step — an accumulated one\n    ! drifts, because the runtime's division truncates and every step loses its remainder.\n    variable VizGrabPos\n    variable VizGrabbed\n    variable VizGrabX\n    variable VizGrabY\n    variable VizGrabFromX\n    variable VizGrabFromY\n    variable VizDragPos\n    variable VizDragX\n    variable VizDragY\n    variable VizDx\n    variable VizDy\n    ! What a press on a *mark* needs: which line the nearest one names, how near it was, and the\n    ! scratch the walk over the recording uses. `VizMark` is the dict that carries the answer back to\n    ! the editor — the one thing that leaves this module, and only when a press names a line.\n    variable VizHit\n    variable VizHitDist\n    variable VizHitReach\n    variable VizHitI\n    variable VizHitX\n    variable VizHitY\n    variable VizHitDx\n    variable VizHitDy\n    variable VizHitNear\n    variable VizHitTmp\n    ! What the report says about the mark itself: how many arrivals this dot is on its line, and how many\n    ! that line had in the whole recording. `VizHitSeen` is the table the walk fills as it goes.\n    variable VizHitSeen\n    variable VizHitVisit\n    variable VizHitValues      ! what that arrival captured, if the script asked for anything\n    variable VizHitText        ! the report as one line, under the row the status line is on\n    variable VizHitTotal\n    variable VizHitCount\n    variable VizMark\n    variable VizPixelsW\n    variable VizPixelsH\n    variable VizPixelsK\n    variable VizPaneH\n    variable VizWheelAmount\n    variable VizZoomXW\n    variable VizZoomYH\n    variable VizMove\n    ! ---- the scrollbars ----\n    ! A bar is a window drawn as a proportion, so the arithmetic that turns one into the other is the\n    ! same for both axes and is written once, in `VizBarFit`, reading these five.\n    variable VizBarLen\n    variable VizBarExtent\n    variable VizBarSpan\n    variable VizBarStart\n    variable VizBarOrigin\n    variable VizSlideSize\n    variable VizSlideTravel\n    variable VizSlidePos\n    variable VizSlideRoom\n    ! Where each handle was drawn, kept because the pointer arrives between draws: the hit test has to\n    ! agree with what is on screen, and the drawn box is the only thing that does.\n    variable VizHandleVPos\n    variable VizHandleVSize\n    variable VizHandleHPos\n    variable VizHandleHSize\n    ! ---- the clip bar ----\n    ! **Two markers on the steps axis that set the clip range without a zoom** — because the zoom is\n    ! the expensive gesture on a big recording, and marking a range costs one drawing where reaching\n    ! it by wheel and pan takes five or six. The bar is the *recording's* own steps, not the picture's:\n    ! `VizRunFrom`/`VizRunTo` are the bar's axis: the **working set's** own step extent, measured in pass\n    ! one over whatever the set is — the whole recording, or what a clip has left of it. That is what\n    ! brings the markers home to the ends after a cut instead of leaving them standing where they were\n    ! chosen on a bar that still spanned the recording.\n    !\n    ! `VizSelFrom`/`VizSelTo` are the **selection**: the window the two markers make, in the bar's own\n    ! steps. The markers are the reader's and not the clip's — a drag moves them and commits nothing, and\n    ! only the `keep` control turns them into a clip — so they need a home that is not the applied range.\n    !\n    ! `VizClipInX`/`VizClipOutX` are where the two markers were *drawn*, which is what the hit test has\n    ! to agree with (the same reasoning the scrollbar handles' boxes are kept for). `VizClipInXW`/`VizClipOutXW`\n    ! are the same two steps mapped through the **window**, which is where the guides and the washes are\n    ! placed: they say where the selection falls in the *picture*, and the picture's own mapping is right\n    ! at any zoom rather than only at the fit.\n    variable VizRunFrom\n    variable VizRunTo\n    variable VizSelFrom\n    variable VizSelTo\n    variable VizSelKey      ! the applied range the selection was put home for\n    variable VizClipInX\n    variable VizClipOutX\n    variable VizClipInXW    ! the selection's ends in the picture's own x, for the guides and the washes\n    variable VizClipOutXW\n    variable VizClipWash    ! a wash's width, needing its own name: `take A from B` assigns to B\n    variable VizClipPos     ! one step's x, or one x's step, while the mapping runs\n    variable VizClipSpan    ! the axis's width in steps, which the mapping divides by\n    variable VizClipStep\n    variable VizClipOther   ! the other marker's step, while one is being dragged past it\n    variable VizClipMin     ! the least a range may be, which is never more than the run is\n    variable VizGrabMarker  ! where the grabbed marker stood when the press named it\n    ! **The controls above the bar, and their boxes as numbers.** A pane element cannot take a click, so a\n    ! control here is a *place*: the box is drawn from these and a press is matched against the same\n    ! numbers, which is the only way the two can be kept from disagreeing. The withdrawn attempt at this\n    ! hard-coded a position into the harness, so when the press did not take there was no way to tell a\n    ! missed hit test from a phase that never ran — hence variables, drawn and hit-tested alike.\n    !\n    ! `><` — take the window *out* of the run — is deliberately not here yet: it needs the set to be a set\n    ! rather than a range, which is the next piece of this work. A control that did nothing would be worse\n    ! than its absence.\n    variable VizCtlY        ! the strip the controls sit in, above the bar\n    variable VizCtlH\n    variable VizKeepX       ! `←--- ---→` — make the window between the markers the run\n    variable VizKeepW\n    variable VizOuterX      ! `---→ ←---` — make the two parts *outside* the window the run\n    variable VizOuterW\n    variable VizResetX      ! `reset` — the whole recording back, above the bar's end\n    variable VizResetW\n    variable VizPtrX\n    variable VizPtrY\n    variable VizPtrA\n    ! The window as it was before the gesture now running, so `VizRedraw` can tell whether anything\n    ! changed. See `VizNote` for why it is kept rather than re-derived.\n    variable VizMoved\n    variable VizWasX0\n    variable VizWasXW\n    variable VizWasY0\n    variable VizWasYH\n\n    svg VizCanvas\n    rect VizFrame\n    ! The source, drawn behind everything else: declared and created first, so the marks sit on top of\n    ! it. It is shown through `VizPane`, a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and `VizGlyphs` is the\n    ! picture itself. See the note where they are created for why it takes two elements to do it.\n    ! **`VizRedact` is the text's own shape** — one bar per line, behind the glyphs — and it lives in the\n    ! same pane for the same reason: see where it is created.\n    svg VizPane\n    path VizRedact\n    svg VizPlot\n    svgimage VizGlyphs\n    ! The diagnostic's own layer: the label lines of the script, right-aligned at the frame's edge, so\n    ! that the picture's text and the drawing's mapping can be compared side by side. Temporary.\n    svgimage VizLabelText\n    path VizRules         ! a faint rule across the frame for every line the run touched\n    path VizHeat0           ! the marks, coloured by how much work the line carried\n    path VizHeat1\n    path VizHeat2\n    path VizHeat3\n    path VizFlowCall      ! the flow, one path per kind of movement: the colour is the kind\n    path VizFlowJump\n    path VizFlowReturn\n    ! Scratch values are named by role rather than pushed on a stack, which would be the neater\n    ! idiom in Python: the view has to run in the JS runtime, where there is no `stack` type and\n    ! `push`/`pop` mean the argument and call stacks instead. See language-pack-issues.md #16.\n    !\n    ! The y labels are a pool, not a set built to fit: an element is a declared symbol, so the view\n    ! cannot make a label per tick any more than it can make a mark per arrival. Four are declared and\n    ! assigned; the count is the axis's resolution, which is all a tick count is.\n    svgtext VizYLabel0\n    svgtext VizYLabel1\n    svgtext VizYLabel2\n    svgtext VizYLabel3\n    svgtext VizXLabel0\n    svgtext VizXLabel1\n    svgtext VizXLabel2\n    svgtext VizXLabel3\n    ! **The flow key: the three colours the *lines* are drawn in.** A dash rather than a swatch,\n    ! because a transfer is drawn as a line and the shape should say so; the word beside it is the\n    ! language's own keyword for that kind of transfer (see `SetStrings`), so a reader meets the word\n    ! they would write rather than a name invented for the picture. `branch` has no entry because it\n    ! is not drawn.\n    !\n    ! It shares its row with the status line, on the left. The heat key that used to sit above it is\n    ! gone — see the ramp in the mark loop for why no range of counts can name those colours — and\n    ! the caption that used to sit opposite is gone too: it printed the run's line range and its step\n    ! count, both of which the status line already carries, so the row it occupied is now the status's.\n    rect VizFlowKeyMark0\n    rect VizFlowKeyMark1\n    rect VizFlowKeyMark2\n    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel0\n    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel1\n    svgtext VizFlowKeyLabel2\n    ! The status line, along the bottom: what the window is showing — the two zooms as percentages of\n    ! the fitted picture, and the span in the trace's own units. It exists to be *quoted*, which is why\n    ! it carries the numbers raw rather than saying them in words: a reader copying a line into a bug\n    ! report needs it to be exact, and the picture needs it to be short.\n    svgtext VizStatus\n    ! The mark's own report, on the line below the status line. It lives here rather than in the panel\n    ! beside the picture because of where a reader's eye already is: they are pointing at a dot, and on a\n    ! big screen the foot of the sidebar is the other side of the room. Graham, 2026-10-05.\n    svgtext VizHitStatus\n    ! **The scrollbars, and why the picture needs them.** Past a hundred per cent a reader can see\n    ! *where* they are — the axis says which lines are on screen — but not *how much* of the run is on\n    ! screen, and those are different questions. A scrollbar answers the second one at a glance and in\n    ! the vocabulary everybody already has: the handle's length is the visible proportion, its position\n    ! is the window's place in the run, and at the fit it fills the bar and cannot be moved because\n    ! there is nowhere to move it to. Two of them, because there really are two axes here — the lines\n    ! down the right margin, the steps along the foot.\n    !\n    ! They live in the margins, outside the plot, with the axis and the keys: they are instruments, and\n    ! a flow line crossing an instrument is a flow line crossing the thing you read the picture with.\n    rect VizBarV\n    rect VizHandleV\n    rect VizBarH\n    rect VizHandleH\n    ! **The clip bar, above the picture rather than below it.** It is the steps axis's own instrument,\n    ! so it belongs where the steps are — and above is where it can do the most good, because its two\n    ! markers carry *guides* down through the whole plot: a reader dragging one sees the exact x it is\n    ! at, over the marks it is choosing between, which is what a range chosen against a chart needs.\n    !\n    ! **And the mask**: the two areas outside the range are washed over while the range is being\n    ! chosen, so the reader sees what is about to be cut away and not merely where the edges are. It is\n    ! painted over the picture, which is the one thing the margins otherwise never do — deliberately,\n    ! because the thing being shown *is* the part of the picture that will go.\n    !\n    ! All of them are moved by attribute writes, which is what lets a drag show the reader where they\n    ! are without redrawing anything (see `VizClipMove`).\n    rect VizBarClip\n    rect VizClipBand\n    rect VizClipMarkIn\n    rect VizClipMarkOut\n    rect VizClipGuideIn\n    rect VizClipGuideOut\n    rect VizClipMaskIn\n    rect VizClipMaskOut\n    ! The notice line, on the bar's own row above the plot: the clip is stated here rather than on the\n    ! status line, where it ran into the flow key's words. A pane literal, so English like the rest of\n    ! the pane's own strings.\n    svgtext VizClipNotice\n    ! **And the controls, which are boxes with their words drawn in them.** They cannot be buttons — a\n    ! pane element takes no click — so each is a `rect` whose box the hit test matches arithmetically and\n    ! an `svgtext` drawn centred inside it, which is the reader's half of the same place.\n    ! **Three controls: the two cuts and the reset.** The arrow legends are the design's glyphs —\n    ! `←--- ---→` keeps the window between the markers, the arrows facing away from it, and `---→ ←---`\n    ! keeps the two parts *outside* it, the arrows facing each other across the gap they remove. A tooltip\n    ! for each is owed and not built yet; until then the notice says what the last cut kept, which is the\n    ! one place a reader can check which button did what.\n    rect VizKeepBox\n    svgtext VizKeepText\n    rect VizOuterBox\n    svgtext VizOuterText\n    rect VizResetBox\n    svgtext VizResetText\n    ! And the readout's own style is set where it is created, in the built-once block below.\nDraw:\n    ! **The drawing keeps its own clock, at its own two ends.** Nothing else can measure it honestly: a\n    ! gesture asks for a draw directly (the editor's own entry point is only the *first* draw of a run), and\n    ! the drawing hands the thread back as it breathes, so a clock kept by any caller would time the frame it\n    ! was given and not the work. `DrawMillis` is what the corner prints and what `VizRemember` learns the\n    ! rate from, so the two can never disagree about what a drawing cost.\n    put now into DrawStarted\n    ! **A draw is a long job, so it takes a ticket rather than a lock.** It says it is running here\n    ! and clears that at its end; anything that wants a draw in the meantime asks `VizRequest`, which\n    ! *remembers* the request instead of losing it. What must never happen is two draws in flight at\n    ! once — they build the picture in the same variables, so two would each write the other's halves —\n    ! but the gestures are the last thing that should wait, because a wheel notch that is dropped is a\n    ! notch the reader has to repeat.\n    put 1 into VizDrawing\n    ! **The arrivals are replaced, never left over, and the reading happens *inside* the guard that has a\n    ! recording to read.** Two faults met here and looked like one. The clearing must come first, because\n    ! `put json of` raises on an empty value and the raise ends the draw; and the parse must not be consulted\n    ! when there was nothing to parse, because `VizParsed` is scratch that keeps the *last* recording's document\n    ! — so a guard reading it re-set the previous tab's arrivals and the pane went on showing a recording that\n    ! belongs to another file. Both were found by asserting the empty state rather than by reading this.\n    put json of `[]` into VizEvents\n    if VizTrace is not empty\n    begin\n        ! **The recording is parsed once, not once per draw.** Measured on a 435KB trace: one\n        ! `element N of` costs about 15ms, because the runtime parses the whole array *and*\n        ! stringifies the element it returns (`Core.js`, the `element` and `item` cases) — so every\n        ! loop here is quadratic in the number of records, and re-parsing per draw paid that again\n        ! for nothing. Keyed on the recording's own text, which is what \"the same run\" means.\n        if VizTrace is not VizParsedFrom\n        begin\n            put json of VizTrace into VizParsed\n            put VizTrace into VizParsedFrom\n            ! A new recording is new objects, so the kept set belongs to the old one.\n            put `` into VizKeptKey\n        end\n        put property `traceEvents` of VizParsed into VizEvents\n    end\n    if VizTrace is empty put `` into VizParsedFrom\n    ! **A clip is a filter over the records, and it is applied here — before anything else reads\n    ! them.** Pass one measures the events, the marks are placed from them, the rules are the lines\n    ! they name and the hit test walks them, so replacing `VizEvents` with the kept ones is the whole\n    ! of it: the extent, the rule set, the busiest line over the ramp and the `visit N of T` the\n    ! sidebar reads all follow from the same set. That is what makes the notice on the status line a\n    ! fact rather than a decoration — the picture really is of the part of the run that is left.\n    !\n    ! The tests below are the re-fit key, and it is `VizSetTag` rather than the list itself: a cut bumps it,\n    ! so a cut is *visible* to the two places that decide a picture — without that it would change nothing,\n    ! because it does not touch the recording's text or the script, which is what those places compare.\n    ! `VizMinSteps` is the run's first step in the picture — 0, or the first step the reader kept — and it\n    ! is what the fit, the clamp and the bars read instead of a hard 0.\n    ! **A cut is a set of ranges over *this* recording, so a new recording drops it.** That also keeps the\n    ! bar's axis honest: the axis is the working set's own step extent, taken from pass one, which measures\n    ! the *set* once a cut is on — so a list carried over would be an axis against a run it never saw.\n    ! **The clip's state, the first time a draw happens and only then.** None of it can be declared-and-set\n    ! in the plumbing, because its names are declared below the plumbing's `stop` — where nothing runs at\n    ! load — and none of it can be read before it has been given a value, since reading an unset variable is\n    ! a runtime error rather than a zero. So it is born at the top of the first draw, behind a flag that the\n    ! plumbing *can* set. `VizClipInit` is declared and set up there.\n    if VizClipInit is 0\n    begin\n        put 0 into VizKeepN\n        put 0 into VizSetTag\n        put -1 into VizLastSetTag\n        put -1 into VizSelKey\n        put 0 into VizHistN\n        put 0 into VizHistAt\n        put -1 into VizKeptKey\n        put 1 into VizClipInit\n    end\n    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace\n    begin\n        gosub to VizCutWhole\n        ! A new recording takes the history with it: those ranges are steps in a run that is no longer on\n        ! screen, so an undo into them would cut the picture to a set nobody asked for.\n        put 0 into VizHistN\n        put 0 into VizHistAt\n    end\n    put 0 into VizMinSteps\n    ! **And the kept set is built when the *list* changes, not on every draw.** This is the whole of why\n    ! the clip did not pay at first: rebuilding it per draw cost more than the records it left out, so a\n    ! clipped gesture was slower than an unclipped one (measured on a 435KB trace: 41s a draw against 16s).\n    ! Built once, every later draw walks only the kept records — and since a walk is quadratic in what it\n    ! walks, that is where the saving is. The *draw* builds it rather than the cut, so that a host asking\n    ! for a draw — or a test — never has to know how the set is made.\n    if VizSetTag is not VizKeptKey gosub to VizKeepRun\n    ! **And the kept set goes back on *every* draw, not only when the filter runs.** The load block above\n    ! has just put the *whole recording's* events into `VizEvents` — that is what its last line does, on\n    ! every draw — so a draw that skipped the filter (every draw after the cut, the cache key being\n    ! unchanged) went on walking the full recording. That is the fault Graham found: the clip was applied\n    ! correctly and then thrown away, so a narrowed picture scrolled as slowly as the whole one. Measured\n    ! with a probe on his recording: after a clip to 96 of 533 records the *next* draw walked 533 again.\n    put VizKept into VizEvents\n!! @hash 323a2668\n!! @verified 1ae42b87\n!!!\n!! Geometry is in viewBox units, and the run is shown through a window.\n!!\n!! Nothing is measured in pixels: the frame is fixed at 1000x740 and the browser fits it to whatever panel it is given, which is what lets the whole script run against a stub DOM in node. What the window decides is *which part of the run* fills that frame. It starts fitted to the whole recording, so the first picture is the one this pane has always drawn; then the wheel scrolls it, shift and control zoom it on their own axes, the two together scroll it across, and a drag pans it. Graham's scheme, from Kdenlive and Audacity, with control added for the second axis.\n!! The picture never changes; the window over it does. `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and its `viewBox` is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw, and the picture itself is drawn once when the run is opened. That is what keeps the text the shape it was written in: a window whose aspect followed the zoom instead is what stretched the letters as you zoomed in. The pane's size being the frame is also the clipping, which is the one thing a picture cannot do for itself — an `<image>` either scales its content to its own box or is clipped by the outer viewport.\n    ! ---- built once, on the first draw ----\n    ! The panel is the module's own (`VizHost`, attached by id to the editor's page) and the run\n    ! arrives in `VizTrace`, fetched by the editor and handed over as a dict. Building the elements\n    ! once is what makes a redraw a redraw — the marks are attributes, so a second call replaces them\n    ! instead of adding a second picture.\n    if VizReady is 1 go to VizBuilt\n\n    ! The drawing's frame, in viewBox units. These belong to the host's panel in the end — they are\n    ! the one place the view still assumes a size — but a constant keeps the maths in whole numbers,\n    ! which this runtime wants anyway: its arithmetic is integer-first and division truncates.\n    !\n    ! **The plot is the margin band on every side, and the band below it carries the instruments.**\n    ! The canvas is 1000 by 740 and the plot is 880 by 580 inside a 60-unit margin. The foot holds the\n    ! steps scrollbar, then the step labels, then one row carrying the status line on the left and the\n    ! flow key on the right; the right-hand margin holds the y labels and the lines scrollbar. That\n    ! leaves the canvas taller than its contents, which is deliberate for now: a sidebar is coming that\n    ! will change the panel's shape, and the height is better chosen then, with the width it will have.\n    put 60 into VizMargin\n    put 880 into VizPlotW\n    put 580 into VizPlotH\n    put VizMargin into VizPlotRight\n    add VizPlotW to VizPlotRight\n    put VizMargin into VizPlotBottom\n    add VizPlotH to VizPlotBottom\n    ! Half the frame's height, which is half a row of text once `VizPlaceY` divides by the window: the\n    ! glyphs of a row are centred in it, so a line's own y is that much below the top of its row. Kept as\n    ! a value rather than done in the mapping so the division stays a division by the window alone.\n    divide VizPlotH by 2 giving VizRowHalf\n    ! The rows a window may not go below. The text is drawn at eighteen units a line, so a window of\n    ! this many rows is one where a source line is already as tall as it is drawn — there is no more\n    ! detail to be had past it, and zooming further only blows the picture up. Derived rather than\n    ! chosen, so it follows the frame if the frame ever comes from the host.\n    divide VizPlotH by 18 giving VizGlyphRows\n\n    create VizCanvas in VizHost\n    set attribute `viewBox` of VizCanvas to `0 0 1000 740`\n    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizCanvas to `xMidYMid meet`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizCanvas to 15\n    set attribute `fill` of VizCanvas to `#8b93a1`\n\n    create VizFrame in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizFrame to 60\n    set attribute `y` of VizFrame to 60\n    set attribute `width` of VizFrame to 880\n    set attribute `height` of VizFrame to 580\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFrame to `none`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizFrame to `#3a4048`\n\n    ! The source, behind the frame's contents, shown through a window. `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>`\n    ! the size of the frame — and it is there because it is the one thing a picture cannot do for\n    ! itself. An `<image>` either scales its content to its own box, which is what stretched the text\n    ! as the zoom changed, or is clipped by the outer viewport, which is the whole canvas. A picture\n    ! that is *moved* rather than resized therefore needs a container that clips, and a nested viewport\n    ! does that by default: it is `overflow: hidden`, and it has no viewBox of its own until one is\n    ! given. That viewBox is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw.\n    !\n    ! The style is not decoration. With none, the plugin defaults to `width:100%;height:100%`, and CSS\n    ! beats the width and height attributes, so the pane would fill the canvas whatever it was told.\n    create VizPane in VizCanvas style `overflow:hidden`\n    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizPane to `none`\n    set attribute `x` of VizPane to VizMargin\n    set attribute `y` of VizPane to VizMargin\n    set attribute `width` of VizPane to VizPlotW\n    set attribute `height` of VizPane to VizPlotH\n    ! **The redaction: the text's own shape, drawn behind it.** One bar per line, as wide as that line's\n    ! text and as tall as the whole eighteen-unit row — so it covers the glyphs *and* the leading above and\n    ! below them, which is what makes the block of a line read as a block rather than as a line.\n    !\n    ! **It is in the pane, and that is the whole of how it zooms.** The pane's `viewBox` is the *document*,\n    ! eighteen units to a row and eight to a column, so a bar drawn in those units is scaled by the browser\n    ! as the glyphs beside it are — which is what makes their *height* follow the row at every zoom.\n    !\n    ! **The fill is the rules' colour, a tenth darker, and both halves of that are Graham's.** He asked for the horizontal lines' colour first — *\"I'd prefer the text to stay black and to try the horizontal line colour for the redaction\"* — and then, having looked at it, for ten per cent off: *\"the redaction bars can be a bit darker, as the text is easily readable and the trace information is still the loudest thing in the picture.\"* The rules are `#eee` where they are created; this is `#d6d6d6`, which is that times 0.9 a channel. The measurements, so the next nudge starts from numbers rather than from taste: against the page 15.48 becomes 12.36; the text on it goes from 5.16 to **4.12** and is still plainly readable; a rule crossing a bar goes from 1.00 — identical, and so invisible — to 1.25, which is a shade clear but only a shade. The text itself is left exactly as it was, dark on light, which is the reading a redacted page has.\n    create VizRedact in VizPane\n    set attribute `fill` of VizRedact to `#d6d6d6`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizRedact to `none`\n\n    ! The picture, one document unit to one unit — the size the text is actually written at — and\n    ! placed by the pane's viewBox rather than by its own rectangle. Its width and height are set by\n    ! `VizSourceMeasure`, because they depend on the run.\n    create VizGlyphs in VizPane\n    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizGlyphs to `none`\n    set attribute `x` of VizGlyphs to 0\n    set attribute `y` of VizGlyphs to 0\n\n    ! **The plot, and it is the mask.** A nested `<svg>` over the frame — 60,60 to 940,640 in canvas units,\n    ! its `viewBox` the same, so one unit inside is one unit outside and *no* coordinate below has to\n    ! change — whose whole purpose is to clip. Everything that belongs to the plot is created *in* it: the\n    ! source picture's overlay, the rules, the marks and the flow. A transfer whose other end is off-screen\n    ! is now drawn at its true position and cut off by this viewport, which is the only way a picture of a\n    ! sliced run can say \"control came from above and left that way\". The axis, the key, the caption and the\n    ! status line stay outside it, because they live in the margin and a line crossing them would be a line\n    ! crossing the reader's instruments.\n    !\n    ! This is the mechanism the picture already used — see `VizPane` — rather than a `clipPath`, which the\n    ! plugin has no element for and which would take a second element anyway. A nested viewport clips by\n    ! default; the style is here for the same reason it is there: with no style of its own the plugin\n    ! defaults the element to `width:100%;height:100%`, and CSS beats the attributes.\n    create VizPlot in VizCanvas style `overflow:hidden`\n    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizPlot to `none`\n    set attribute `x` of VizPlot to VizMargin\n    set attribute `y` of VizPlot to VizMargin\n    set attribute `width` of VizPlot to VizPlotW\n    set attribute `height` of VizPlot to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `viewBox` of VizPlot to VizMargin cat ` ` cat VizMargin cat ` ` cat VizPlotW cat ` ` cat VizPlotH\n\n    ! **The diagnostic: the name of every label at the right-hand end of its own line.** A second\n    ! picture, built per draw, holding nothing but the label lines of the script — each copied verbatim\n    ! and right-aligned at the frame's right-hand edge, in a font size the source's own size scaled to\n    ! the frame. It is *temporary*, and it exists to settle one question that reading the code cannot:\n    ! whether the picture's text and the drawing agree about where a line is.\n    !\n    ! The reason it works is that both copies are the *same string* in the *same font* at the *same\n    ! size*, drawn through two independent mappings — the source's own document (inside the pane, whose\n    ! `viewBox` is the window) and the canvas's units (this image, one unit to one canvas unit). If the\n    ! two mappings agree, each name sits on its own source line; if they are half a row apart, the eye\n    ! sees the name floating above or below the text it copies. Graham's idea, and the right instrument:\n    ! a comparison of identical things rather than a judgement of small offsets.\n    !\n    ! It is one `<image>` rather than a pool of text elements because the number of labels in view is\n    ! the file's business, not the pane's: at the widest zoom every label in the script is on screen.\n    !\n    ! **Created after the plot, so it sits above the marks and the flow** — it is the reading aid, and\n    ! nothing should be drawn over it. It is also outside the plot's clipping, for the same reason the\n    ! instruments are: it is drawn inside the frame by construction, so there is nothing to clip, and\n    ! keeping it out means no zoom can ever cut a name out of it.\n    create VizLabelText in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `preserveAspectRatio` of VizLabelText to `none`\n    set attribute `x` of VizLabelText to 0\n    set attribute `y` of VizLabelText to 0\n    set attribute `width` of VizLabelText to 1000\n    set attribute `height` of VizLabelText to 740\n\n    ! A rule per line the run touched, faint and across the whole frame. It is drawn *over* the source\n    ! so a line can be picked out of the texture, and *under* everything else so it never competes\n    ! with a mark: at low zoom a mark is a dot in mid-space, and the rule is what says which line the\n    ! dot belongs to. Kept faint deliberately — it is a grid, not a reading.\n    create VizRules in VizPlot\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizRules to `#eee`\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizRules to 1\n    set attribute `fill` of VizRules to `none`\n\n    create VizYLabel0 in VizCanvas\n    create VizYLabel1 in VizCanvas\n    create VizYLabel2 in VizCanvas\n    create VizYLabel3 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel0 to `end`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel1 to `end`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel2 to `end`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizYLabel3 to `end`\n\n    create VizXLabel0 in VizCanvas\n    create VizXLabel1 in VizCanvas\n    create VizXLabel2 in VizCanvas\n    create VizXLabel3 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel0 to `middle`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel1 to `middle`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel2 to `middle`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizXLabel3 to `middle`\n\n    ! The caption sits under the plot and names the window this run covered. A recording is a\n    ! file beside the script, so a run that failed to record leaves the *previous* one in place\n    ! and the picture looks exactly as convincing as a fresh one — the line numbers are what\n    ! let a reader tell, and they are only useful if they are on the picture.\n    create VizStatus in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizStatus to `start`\n    create VizHitStatus in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizHitStatus to `start`\n\n    ! ---- the scrollbars ----\n    ! Created last, so they are drawn over everything: nothing overlaps them, and last is the safe\n    ! default. Both of the *troughs* are fixed — a bar is always the whole of its axis — so their\n    ! geometry is set once here and only the handles move. The handles are pills rather than rectangles\n    ! because a scrollbar handle reads as a pill; `rx` of half the thickness is what makes one.\n    !\n    ! The colours are the canvas's own greys: the trough a shade darker than the panel, the handle\n    ! lighter again, so the pair reads as a groove with something in it rather than as two rectangles.\n    ! The cursors are the whole of what makes a bar *feel* like a bar — `ns-resize` down the side and\n    ! `ew-resize` along the foot — and they are the reason the style clause is here rather than plain\n    ! attributes.\n    create VizBarV in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizBarV to 944\n    set attribute `y` of VizBarV to VizMargin\n    set attribute `width` of VizBarV to 10\n    set attribute `height` of VizBarV to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `rx` of VizBarV to 5\n    set attribute `fill` of VizBarV to `#7a8290`\n    create VizHandleV in VizCanvas style `cursor:ns-resize`\n    set attribute `x` of VizHandleV to 944\n    set attribute `width` of VizHandleV to 10\n    set attribute `rx` of VizHandleV to 5\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHandleV to `#c9ced8`\n    create VizBarH in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizBarH to VizMargin\n    set attribute `y` of VizBarH to 646\n    set attribute `width` of VizBarH to VizPlotW\n    set attribute `height` of VizBarH to 10\n    set attribute `rx` of VizBarH to 5\n    set attribute `fill` of VizBarH to `#7a8290`\n    create VizHandleH in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`\n    set attribute `y` of VizHandleH to 646\n    set attribute `height` of VizHandleH to 10\n    set attribute `rx` of VizHandleH to 5\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHandleH to `#c9ced8`\n    ! **The clip bar, above the picture.** It sits in the pane's top margin, which held nothing: the plot\n    ! begins at 60 and the step numbers are down at the foot, so this is the one free band, and above is\n    ! where the bar does the most good — its markers carry guides down the whole plot, so the reader\n    ! dragging one sees the exact x it is at, over the very marks they are choosing between.\n    !\n    ! The rail is two units of a pale grey, the band is the range, and the markers are twenty units tall\n    ! so there is something to grab; the cursor style says so, as it does on the scrollbar handles.\n    create VizBarClip in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizBarClip to VizMargin\n    set attribute `y` of VizBarClip to 40\n    set attribute `width` of VizBarClip to VizPlotW\n    set attribute `height` of VizBarClip to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizBarClip to `#d8d4cb`\n    create VizClipBand in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `y` of VizClipBand to 34\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipBand to 12\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipBand to `#c9d2e2`\n    create VizClipMarkIn in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`\n    set attribute `y` of VizClipMarkIn to 31\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipMarkIn to 4\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipMarkIn to 20\n    set attribute `rx` of VizClipMarkIn to 1\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMarkIn to `#5c6b7f`\n    create VizClipMarkOut in VizCanvas style `cursor:ew-resize`\n    set attribute `y` of VizClipMarkOut to 31\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipMarkOut to 4\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipMarkOut to 20\n    set attribute `rx` of VizClipMarkOut to 1\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMarkOut to `#5c6b7f`\n    ! The two guides, from the markers down through the plot: one unit wide, so they read as a plumb line\n    ! rather than as another rule. Their height is the plot's, and their y is where the bar's band ends.\n    create VizClipGuideIn in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipGuideIn to 1\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipGuideIn to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipGuideIn to `#5c6b7f`\n    create VizClipGuideOut in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipGuideOut to 1\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipGuideOut to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipGuideOut to `#5c6b7f`\n    ! The notice and the working readout: the first on the bar's own row, the second on the strip below\n    ! the plot that the bar used to occupy.\n    create VizClipNotice in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizClipNotice to `start`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizClipNotice to 13\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipNotice to `#5c6b7f`\n    move VizClipNotice to VizMargin 24\n    create VizBusy in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizBusy to `start`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizBusy to 15\n    set attribute `font-weight` of VizBusy to 600\n    set attribute `fill` of VizBusy to `#b2431f`\n    move VizBusy to VizMargin 690\n    ! The bar's axis starts at the pane's own zero — the fit starts there, so a marker at the far left is\n    ! step zero and lines up with the picture's left edge — and pass one replaces both ends with the\n    ! working set's own extent.\n    put 0 into VizRunFrom\n    put 0 into VizRunTo\n    ! And where the two markers stand before the first draw has put them anywhere, so a press in the\n    ! strip meets two numbers rather than two unset names — the same guard the scrollbar handles get by\n    ! being drawn before anything can press them.\n    put 0 into VizSelFrom\n    put 0 into VizSelTo\n    put VizMargin into VizClipInX\n    put VizPlotRight into VizClipOutX\n    put VizMargin into VizClipInXW\n    put VizPlotRight into VizClipOutXW\n    ! **The three controls above the bar, drawn from the numbers the hit test uses.** They sit in their own\n    ! strip above the rail and against its right end, laid out from `VizPlotRight` rather than from\n    ! constants so they follow the frame if it ever stops being 1000 wide. Their legends are the arrow\n    ! pairs of the design, drawn *in* them; the words that explain each one belong to a tooltip, which is\n    ! owed and not built yet — until then the notice names what the last cut kept.\n    put 8 into VizCtlY\n    put 18 into VizCtlH\n    put 84 into VizResetW\n    put 84 into VizOuterW\n    put 84 into VizKeepW\n    take VizResetW from VizPlotRight giving VizResetX\n    take VizOuterW from VizResetX giving VizOuterX\n    take 8 from VizOuterX\n    take VizKeepW from VizOuterX giving VizKeepX\n    take 8 from VizKeepX\n    create VizKeepBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `x` of VizKeepBox to VizKeepX\n    set attribute `y` of VizKeepBox to VizCtlY\n    set attribute `width` of VizKeepBox to VizKeepW\n    set attribute `height` of VizKeepBox to VizCtlH\n    set attribute `rx` of VizKeepBox to 3\n    set attribute `fill` of VizKeepBox to `#e6e2d8`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizKeepBox to `#9aa4b1`\n    create VizKeepText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizKeepText to `middle`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizKeepText to 11\n    set attribute `fill` of VizKeepText to `#40506b`\n    set the text of VizKeepText to `←--- ---→`\n    put VizKeepW into VizMove\n    divide VizMove by 2\n    add VizKeepX to VizMove\n    move VizKeepText to VizMove 21\n    create VizOuterBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `x` of VizOuterBox to VizOuterX\n    set attribute `y` of VizOuterBox to VizCtlY\n    set attribute `width` of VizOuterBox to VizOuterW\n    set attribute `height` of VizOuterBox to VizCtlH\n    set attribute `rx` of VizOuterBox to 3\n    set attribute `fill` of VizOuterBox to `#e6e2d8`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizOuterBox to `#9aa4b1`\n    create VizOuterText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizOuterText to `middle`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizOuterText to 11\n    set attribute `fill` of VizOuterText to `#40506b`\n    set the text of VizOuterText to `---→ ←---`\n    put VizOuterW into VizMove\n    divide VizMove by 2\n    add VizOuterX to VizMove\n    move VizOuterText to VizMove 21\n    create VizResetBox in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `x` of VizResetBox to VizResetX\n    set attribute `y` of VizResetBox to VizCtlY\n    set attribute `width` of VizResetBox to VizResetW\n    set attribute `height` of VizResetBox to VizCtlH\n    set attribute `rx` of VizResetBox to 3\n    set attribute `fill` of VizResetBox to `#e6e2d8`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizResetBox to `#9aa4b1`\n    create VizResetText in VizCanvas style `cursor:pointer`\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizResetText to `middle`\n    set attribute `font-size` of VizResetText to 11\n    set attribute `fill` of VizResetText to `#40506b`\n    set the text of VizResetText to `reset`\n    put VizResetW into VizMove\n    divide VizMove by 2\n    add VizResetX to VizMove\n    ! The baseline: `VizCtlY` plus thirteen, which sets eleven-unit glyphs inside an eighteen-unit box.\n    move VizResetText to VizMove 21\n\n    ! **The kept list's holders, and the history's.** `VizKeepCap` is how many ranges a list may hold; the\n    ! spare pair is the same size because a cut to the outside builds a whole new list before it replaces\n    ! the live one. The history is 32 deep, which is more cuts than anybody makes on one recording, and\n    ! each entry is a few bytes of text.\n    put 16 into VizKeepCap\n    set the elements of VizKeepFrom to VizKeepCap\n    set the elements of VizKeepTo to VizKeepCap\n    set the elements of VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepCap\n    set the elements of VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepCap\n    set the elements of VizHist to 32\n\n    ! A mark is a *stroked* hairline with a round cap, not a filled one: `M x y h0.01` alone fills to\n    ! nothing at all, and stroked without the cap it is a 0.01-long rectangle — so the cap belongs to\n    ! the mark, not to a scheme. Its width is set with the geometry on every draw, where the span is\n    ! known; see the note at that site.\n    !\n    ! The heat ramp's four bands, coolest first, and the only scheme. The marks were once drawn a\n    ! second time by a plain single-colour path; the fold removed the duplication, and with it the\n    ! template that the mark-size and line-cap mistakes were both copied from.\n    ! **The ramp, and it is a spectrum between blue and red.** It used to run blue, yellow, orange,\n    ! red, which is not a progression a reader can read as one — the eye has no way to say whether\n    ! yellow is between blue and red or off to the side of them. These four are on the line the eye\n    ! does interpolate: blue, violet, magenta, red. The *coldest* is what every mark starts as, which\n    ! is what makes a picture of a run show the program warming up; see the shading in the mark loop.\n    create VizHeat0 in VizPlot\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat0 to `none`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat0 to `#3b7dd8`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat0 to `round`\n    create VizHeat1 in VizPlot\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat1 to `none`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat1 to `#8f5fd8`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat1 to `round`\n    create VizHeat2 in VizPlot\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat2 to `none`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat2 to `#d2467f`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat2 to `round`\n    create VizHeat3 in VizPlot\n    set attribute `fill` of VizHeat3 to `none`\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizHeat3 to `#e5484d`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat3 to `round`\n\n    ! The flow, a path per kind, in the prototype's own colours. A transfer says where control went,\n    ! and *how* it went is the interesting part: a `gosub` and a `return` are the same gesture drawn\n    ! the same way until the colour tells them apart. `branch` is not drawn at all — see the note on\n    ! the declarations for why the scaffolding is left out rather than greyed.\n    ! One place for each, because the key draws them too: a legend that named a colour other than the\n    ! one on the wires would be worse than no legend, and two copies is how that happens.\n    put `#e0a44a` into VizColourCall\n    put `#43c6a8` into VizColourJump\n    put `#c07ad0` into VizColourReturn\n    create VizFlowCall in VizPlot\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowCall to VizColourCall\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowCall to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowCall to `none`\n    create VizFlowJump in VizPlot\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowJump to VizColourJump\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowJump to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowJump to `none`\n    ! **The masks last, and that is the whole of their reason: SVG paints in document order, so an element\n    ! created before the marks would sit *under* them and hide nothing.** One covers the recording before\n    ! the range and one what follows it, so a reader choosing a range sees the part that is about to go.\n    ! Their x and width are set per draw and per drag; only their shape is fixed here.\n    ! **The wash has to be given as a *string*, not as the number 0.62** — the language has no fractions and\n    ! `set attribute ... to 0.62` is a compile error (\"not an integer\"), where a backtick value is just the\n    ! text of the attribute and never goes near the arithmetic.\n    create VizClipMaskIn in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `y` of VizClipMaskIn to VizMargin\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipMaskIn to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMaskIn to `#f2efe9`\n    set attribute `fill-opacity` of VizClipMaskIn to `0.62`\n    create VizClipMaskOut in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `y` of VizClipMaskOut to VizMargin\n    set attribute `height` of VizClipMaskOut to VizPlotH\n    set attribute `fill` of VizClipMaskOut to `#f2efe9`\n    set attribute `fill-opacity` of VizClipMaskOut to `0.62`\n    create VizFlowReturn in VizPlot\n    set attribute `stroke` of VizFlowReturn to VizColourReturn\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizFlowReturn to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowReturn to `none`\n\n    ! The key, laid out once — only the ranges it names change with the run. It sits on the caption's\n    ! line, in the right half of the frame so a long caption cannot run into it, and its swatches are\n    ! the ramp's own colours, in the right-hand half of the foot: which run this is and what the\n    ! colours mean, side by side.\n    divide VizPlotW by 2 giving VizKeyX\n    add VizMargin to VizKeyX\n    divide VizPlotW by 8 giving VizKeyPitch\n\n    ! **The flow key, which is now the whole key.** It says *how* control reached a line, which the\n    ! marks' colours cannot say at all. The heat key that shared this row is gone with the counts it\n    ! named: the ramp means \"how hot, by when\", and no range of visit counts can stand for that.\n    divide VizPlotW by 2 giving VizKeyX\n    add VizMargin to VizKeyX\n    create VizFlowKeyMark0 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to VizKeyX\n    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 700\n    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 12\n    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark0 to VizColourCall\n    create VizFlowKeyLabel0 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel0 to `start`\n    add 18 to VizKeyX\n    move VizFlowKeyLabel0 to VizKeyX 711\n    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel0 to StrFlowCall\n    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX\n    take 18 from VizKeyX\n    create VizFlowKeyMark1 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to VizKeyX\n    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 700\n    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 12\n    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark1 to VizColourJump\n    create VizFlowKeyLabel1 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel1 to `start`\n    add 18 to VizKeyX\n    move VizFlowKeyLabel1 to VizKeyX 711\n    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel1 to StrFlowJump\n    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX\n    take 18 from VizKeyX\n    create VizFlowKeyMark2 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `x` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to VizKeyX\n    set attribute `y` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 700\n    set attribute `width` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 12\n    set attribute `height` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to 2\n    set attribute `fill` of VizFlowKeyMark2 to VizColourReturn\n    create VizFlowKeyLabel2 in VizCanvas\n    set attribute `text-anchor` of VizFlowKeyLabel2 to `start`\n    add 18 to VizKeyX\n    move VizFlowKeyLabel2 to VizKeyX 711\n    set the text of VizFlowKeyLabel2 to StrFlowReturn\n    add VizKeyPitch to VizKeyX\n    take 18 from VizKeyX\n\n    put 1 into VizReady\n\nVizBuilt:\n!! @hash 5158a082\n!! @verified 20bf4472\n!!!\n!! What a draw does, in order.\n!!\n!! Pass one measures the run — the lines it touched, the steps it took, the busiest of them — and everything below is drawn from those measurements: the rules from pass one's set of named lines, the marks and the transfers from the same walk at the scales it settled. Nothing below re-reads the recording to decide anything pass one could have decided, which is what keeps a redraw cheap and the picture self-consistent.\n    ! ---- drawn every time ----\n    ! **The drawing is swapped, not cleared and redrawn.** Everything a draw produces is built in the\n    ! variables below — the four mark strings, the flow string, every label's text and the picture's\n    ! window — and *nothing* is written to the DOM until the end, in one run of statements that cannot\n    ! be interrupted. So the screen always shows one whole picture or another, never a half-built one,\n    ! and there is no buffer to keep: the buffer is the variables and the swap is the last block.\n    !\n    ! **Why nothing is written here any more, and it is worth knowing.** This block used to blank the\n    ! marks and every label before the passes began, on the reasoning that a redraw interrupted by a\n    ! yield would otherwise show the last run's marks under the new run's axis. It did not, because the\n    ! axis and the marks are written in the same uninterrupted run — and it made things worse, because\n    ! the passes *do* yield (every hundred marks, so a long draw cannot freeze the browser) and a blank\n    ! is exactly what the browser then painted: the flash on a slow machine. A redraw now leaves the\n    ! previous picture up until the next one is complete, which is what a double buffer would do.\n    !\n    ! What remains here is the resetting of the accumulators, which are grown by `cat` and would\n    ! otherwise double up. Nothing else may live here: no value may depend on what the passes below\n    ! measure, because on a redraw it would silently carry the *previous* draw's number. The size of a\n    ! mark is where that cost two rounds to spot — its own note says how.\n    put `` into VizHeatD\n    put `` into VizHeatE\n    put `` into VizHeatF\n    put `` into VizHeatG\n    put `` into VizFlowCallD\n    put `` into VizFlowJumpD\n    put `` into VizFlowReturnD\n    put `` into VizRulesD\n!! @hash 55620f7b\n!! @verified 466d9a4e\n!!!\n!! The frame the picture is drawn in is the run's own extent, not the file's.\n!!\n!! The rules cover exactly the lines the recording names — an arrival, or either end of a transfer — and the axis is scaled to the steps it took, so a picture of a run that touched nine lines shows nine.\n    ! ---- pass one: the frame the picture is drawn in ----\n    ! **Nothing below depends on the window, so it is built once per picture and not once per gesture.** It\n    ! reads the recording and nothing else — the extent the axis is scaled to, the rule set, and the size one\n    ! mark is drawn at, which is a judgement off the span. It used to run above the test for a new recording,\n    ! so every wheel notch re-read the whole trace *and* paid the `wait` this loop breathes with, which alone\n    ! is worth about a second and a half on a big one. The condition is the same one the fit is decided by,\n    ! read here because the fit comes later and cannot be asked yet; the two cannot disagree, since both read\n    ! `VizTrace` and `VizSource`.\n    put 0 into VizPictureNew\n    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace put 1 into VizPictureNew\n    if VizSource is not VizLastSource put 1 into VizPictureNew\n    ! **The range is part of the key, and this is one of the two places it has to be.** A clip does\n    ! not change the recording's text, so without this test the guard would let a clipped draw\n    ! through on the previous picture's measurements — its extent, its rule set, its busiest line.\n    ! The same test is made at the fit below; the two decide the same thing and must not disagree.\n    if VizSetTag is not VizLastSetTag put 1 into VizPictureNew\n    if VizPictureNew is 0 go to VizFrameDone\n    ! A leading and trailing space, so that every entry added is padded on both sides and ` 15 ` can\n    ! never match inside ` 115 `. Cleared with the pass that fills it rather than with the paths above,\n    ! because a gesture no longer runs the pass and an empty set is a picture with no rules.\n    put ` ` into VizRuledLines\n    put 0 into VizBreathe\n    put 0 into VizMaxSteps\n    put 0 into VizMaxCount\n    put json of `{}` into VizRunCounts\n    put 0 into VizIndex\n    while VizEvents has element VizIndex\n    begin\n        ! Give the browser a turn every hundred marks. Without this the page's thread is held for the\n        ! whole pass — tens of seconds on a big recording — and the browser offers to stop the script\n        ! instead of painting the frame that is already built.\n        add 1 to VizBreathe\n        if VizBreathe is greater than 100\n        begin\n            put 0 into VizBreathe\n            wait 1 millis\n        end\n        put element VizIndex of VizEvents into VizEvent\n        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory\n        put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs\n        if VizCategory is `window`\n        begin\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizMaxSteps\n            add 0 to VizMaxSteps\n        end\n        if VizCategory is `anchor`\n        begin\n            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps\n            add 0 to VizSteps\n            if VizSteps is greater than VizMaxSteps put VizSteps into VizMaxSteps\n            ! How often this line is reached in the whole recording, kept for the ramp: the busiest\n            ! line is what the bands are scaled against, so it has to be known before any mark is\n            ! coloured. See the mark loop.\n            put property VizLine of VizRunCounts into VizCount\n            if VizCount is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizCount\n            add 1 to VizCount\n            set property VizLine of VizRunCounts to VizCount\n            if VizCount is greater than VizMaxCount put VizCount into VizMaxCount\n            gosub to VizRuleAdd\n        end\n        if VizCategory is `transfer`\n        begin\n            put property `from_line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            gosub to VizRuleAdd\n            put property `to_line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            gosub to VizRuleAdd\n        end\n        add 1 to VizIndex\n    end\n\n    if VizMaxSteps is less than 1 put 1 into VizMaxSteps\n    ! **The run's width in steps, which is not `VizMaxSteps` once a clip is on.** `VizMaxSteps` is the\n    ! run's *last* step, wherever the picture starts; the fit, the clamp, the bars and the handle drag\n    ! all want the width — how much run there is to move a window over — and with a clip that is the\n    ! clip's own span. Unclipped `VizMinSteps` is 0, so this is `VizMaxSteps` and every one of those\n    ! four sites reads exactly what it always did.\n    take VizMinSteps from VizMaxSteps giving VizSpanSteps\n    if VizSpanSteps is less than 1 put 1 into VizSpanSteps\n    ! **The clip's own axis: the working set's extent, always.** It used to be the recording's own steps\n    ! while there was no clip and to stand still once there was one, which is why a cut left the markers\n    ! where they had been chosen — they were placed on the recording's scale while the picture above them\n    ! was the range. Measured here, over the set the picture is of, as part of the walk pass one has just\n    ! made, so the measurement is free: measuring it separately cost 37 seconds on a 435KB recording, which\n    ! is what walking 2,300 records at 15ms a record costs.\n    put VizMinSteps into VizRunFrom\n    put VizMaxSteps into VizRunTo\n    ! **The recording's own last step, remembered while nothing has been cut.** A cut to the *outside* has\n    ! to name the far end of the run, and this is the one place that measures it: pass one over the whole\n    ! recording, which is what the set is until the first cut. Kept rather than measured again, because\n    ! measuring it separately is a walk of every record — 37 seconds on a 435KB trace.\n    if VizKeepN is 0 put VizMaxSteps into VizAllTo\n    ! **And the selection goes home whenever the applied range changes**, which is the reader's expectation:\n    ! the bar says where the selection sits in what is on screen, and a cut has just changed what that is.\n    ! Keyed on the range rather than reset every draw, or a pan — which redraws — would throw away a\n    ! selection the reader had just made.\n    if VizSetTag is not VizSelKey\n    begin\n        put VizRunFrom into VizSelFrom\n        put VizRunTo into VizSelTo\n        put VizSetTag into VizSelKey\n    end\n!! @hash 6e051bfd\n!! @verified 92278436\n!!!\n!! A mark has to stay legible at the pane's scale, or it is not a mark.\n!!\n!! Its size is the one number here that is a judgement rather than a measurement, and it has a history — see the note at the assignment for the clamp that made every redraw draw a different size.\n    ! ---- the size of a mark ----\n    ! One size for every mark, and it is set *here* because this is the first point at which the span\n    ! is known. It used to live in the per-draw block at the top of this pass, which is before pass\n    ! one has measured anything, so it divided VizPlotH by the span of the *previous* draw — and by\n    ! nothing at all on the first draw, where an empty span divides to Infinity and clamps down to\n    ! the ceiling of 14. So a first draw drew 14-unit dots and every redraw drew 3-unit ones, which\n    ! is what \"returning to Graph reduces the markers to tiny dots\" was.\n    !\n    ! The row-proportional rule it replaces also only ever applied between 41 and 193 lines: shorter\n    ! runs were clamped up to 14 anyway, and on a long run a row is a couple of units, so the floor\n    ! of 3 was a pinprick by construction. That clamp — not the cap — was the mistake, and a mark has\n    ! to stay legible at the pane's scale to be a mark at all. When this view takes the host panel's\n    ! size rather than asserting a 1000x700 frame, this is the number that should come from it.\n    put 14 into VizDotSize\nVizFrameDone:\n!! @hash d35b38f7\n!! @verified 74df2991\n!!!\n!! The window is held as two ranges in the trace's own units, never as a scale factor.\n!!\n!! A factor would have to be relative to a fit that changes with the recording, and an absolute one ends up pinned at one end — the prototype's own lesson, where the same control read 51.6 px a step for one window and 0.7 for another. Multiplying before dividing keeps that arithmetic exact, as it does for the marks.\n!! The window doubles as the reset. Both gestures clamp at the edges of the run, so zooming out returns to the whole picture and panning stops at its ends; there is no reset button to find. The window is fitted once per *recording* and kept across visits, so the pane comes back as it was left; opening a different run is what re-fits it, and `VizReset` is there for a host that wants to force one.\n!!\n!! The zoom stops where the text is drawn at its own size. A source line is eighteen units tall, so a window of fewer than `VizGlyphRows` rows would only blow the text up, and there is nothing more to see. Applied before the clamp to the run, so a recording shorter than that many lines simply has no vertical zoom — the whole run is already legible and there is nothing to zoom into.\n    ! ---- the window on the run ----\n    ! Fitted to the whole run the first time that recording is drawn and left alone after that:\n    ! a redraw is what a gesture causes, and re-fitting there would undo the gesture that asked for\n    ! it. The recording is the key rather than the visit, because leaving the pane and coming back to\n    ! the *same* run should find the window exactly as it was — coming back is how a reader works, and\n    ! a visit that resets is a visit that has to be redone.\n    !\n    ! The comparison is over the whole recording, and it is the honest one: a recording is a file\n    ! beside the script, so editing the script leaves it alone and the window still means what it\n    ! meant, while re-running the script writes a new one and the window is fitted again.\n    put 0 into VizNewRun\n    if VizTrace is not VizLastTrace put 1 into VizNewRun\n    ! **The range joins the key here too, and this is the second of the two places.** A clip changes\n    ! what the picture is *of*, so the window has to be fitted afresh or the fit would stay fitted to\n    ! the part that has just been cut away: the extent, `VizFitXW` — which is what the status line\n    ! measures the zoom against — and the bars would all still describe the whole run. Both keys are\n    ! advanced at this one point, where a picture is decided, so they cannot drift apart.\n    if VizSetTag is not VizLastSetTag put 1 into VizNewRun\n    put VizTrace into VizLastTrace\n    put VizSetTag into VizLastSetTag\n    ! The picture first, because the fit below is over the extent it measured.\n    if VizNewRun is 1 go to VizPictureFresh\n    if VizSource is not VizLastSource go to VizPictureFresh\n    go to VizPictureDone\nVizPictureFresh:\n    gosub to VizSourceMeasure\n    put VizSource into VizLastSource\n    ! **A new picture can mean a new extent, so the window is fitted again.** The extent is the *file's*\n    ! line count, and editing the script changes it — so a window that was fitted to the old file can\n    ! be left off the end of the new one. The recording is not the only input that can move the ground\n    ! under the window, and this is the one that does it.\n    gosub to VizReset\nVizPictureDone:\n    if VizNewRun is 1 gosub to VizReset\n    if VizViewFit is 1 go to VizWindow\n    ! The run starts at `VizMinSteps` — 0 unclipped, the clip's own start when clipped — so a clip\n    ! fits the picture to the range the reader chose and not to the whole recording they cut it from.\n    put VizMinSteps into VizViewX0\n    put VizSpanSteps into VizViewXW\n    put VizMinLine into VizViewY0\n    put VizSpanLines into VizViewYH\n    ! The fit is kept, because it is what the status line measures the zoom *against*: the two window\n    ! sizes on their own say what is in view but not how far in it is, and \"how far in\" is the part a\n    ! reader can recognise. It is the only thing here that a second copy is right for — recomputing it\n    ! would mean asking `VizClamp` what it allowed, which is not a question it answers.\n    put VizSpanSteps into VizFitXW\n    put VizSpanLines into VizFitYH\n    put 1 into VizViewFit\nVizWindow:\n    gosub to VizClamp\n\n    ! ---- the window on the run, in the pane's own units ----\n    ! Computed here, at the top of the draw, because two things need it and only one of them is the\n    ! pane: the pane's `viewBox` is this, and so is the factor that turns a line's length in characters\n    ! into a place in the frame — which is what the left end of every rule is made of. It used to be\n    ! computed inside `VizSourcePicture`, which is the last thing a draw does, so a rule reading it\n    ! would have been reading the draw before: the trap this file has already paid for twice, in the\n    ! size of a mark and in a value measured before the pass that measures it.\n    !\n    ! The window's `y` is in the *document's* own units so that a row of text lands on the line the axis\n    ! names, and its width is what the frame's aspect allows at that row height — there is no wider one.\n    take VizMinLine from VizViewY0 giving VizWindowY\n    multiply VizWindowY by 18\n    multiply VizViewYH by 18 giving VizWindowH\n    multiply VizWindowH by VizPlotW giving VizWindowW\n    divide VizWindowW by VizPlotH\n!! @hash 0e27806e\n!! @verified 91fd04ef\n!!!\n!! The source is drawn behind the heat, once, and shown through a window.\n!!\n!! An element is a declared symbol, so there cannot be one `svgtext` per source line — but one `<image>` carries the whole run, and its `href` is an SVG *document* the view builds itself out of `json split` and `replace`. That is why it is a document rather than a canvas raster: the language can already build one, so nothing new had to be invented to reach a canvas it has no words for, and it stays vector, so the text does not go soft past the scale it was rendered at.\n    ! ---- the source behind the heat ----\n    ! Built when either of its two inputs has changed, and not otherwise. A new script is new text to\n    ! draw; a new recording is a new range of lines to draw it for. Both are compared as whole strings,\n    ! which is what they are — and it is the same reasoning as the window's, on the other input: the\n    ! thing that decides is the *content*, not the visit. Drawing on every draw would be the redraw\n    ! Graham asked to be rid of, and drawing only on a new recording would leave an edited script\n    ! showing its old text.\n\n    ! No scale factors are kept, and that is deliberate. AllSpeak's arithmetic is integer-first and\n    ! division truncates (`divide 10 by 3` is 3), so a fraction here would be lost: a 626-line span\n    ! against a 580-unit pane gives 0, and the picture would collapse onto a single line. Multiplying\n    ! *before* dividing keeps the intermediate large and the result exact to a unit — the documented\n    ! scaled-integer pattern, applied where it matters most.\n!! @hash d71d28a1\n!! @verified d71d28a1\n!!!\n!! One path per kind of mark.\n!!\n!! An AllSpeak element is a declared symbol, so a script cannot create N elements for N marks. What it can do is grow one path's `d` attribute by concatenation and let the browser draw the whole layer from it, so the marks cost string building rather than DOM nodes. A trace of 9 arrivals and 10 transfers is two elements. Per-mark colour would need one path per colour band, which is what the heat ramp will use.\n!! And the heat is relative, not absolute. Each mark's colour is its line's *running* count against the busiest line in the recording, so every line starts cold and the top of the ramp means the hottest line in *this* run. The editor used to send per-line counts for this and the view no longer reads them: they arrive keyed to a neighbouring line, so a mark's colour was its neighbour's heat. See the note in TODO.md.\n    ! ---- pass two: the marks, at those scales ----\n    ! How many times each line has been reached *in this draw*, which is what the ramp is drawn from.\n    ! It is thrown away and started again on every draw because a redraw is a recount: the window may\n    ! have moved, but the run has not, so the counts have to come out the same.\n    put json of `{}` into VizSeen\n    put 0 into VizIndex\n    while VizEvents has element VizIndex\n    begin\n        ! Give the browser a turn every hundred marks. Without this the page's thread is held for the\n        ! whole pass — tens of seconds on a big recording — and the browser offers to stop the script\n        ! instead of painting the frame that is already built.\n        add 1 to VizBreathe\n        if VizBreathe is greater than 100\n        begin\n            put 0 into VizBreathe\n            wait 1 millis\n        end\n        put element VizIndex of VizEvents into VizEvent\n        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory\n        put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs\n        if VizCategory is `anchor`\n        begin\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps\n            add 0 to VizSteps\n            take VizViewX0 from VizSteps giving VizX\n            multiply VizX by VizPlotW\n            divide VizX by VizViewXW\n            add VizMargin to VizX\n            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            ! The line's own y, which is the middle of the row of text it names — `VizPlaceY` is the one\n            ! place that mapping lives, because this, the rules and the axis labels have to agree.\n            put VizLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            put VizTickPos into VizY\n            ! A mark outside the window is left out rather than drawn and clipped. There is no clip\n            ! rectangle to draw into — the plugin has no element for one — and a mark outside the\n            ! frame paints over the axis and the key, so leaving it out is the honest version. The\n            ! tests are strict, which by itself lets a mark *on* an edge through: 940 is the right\n            ! edge, and `940 is greater than 940` is false, so a mark on the last step is kept.\n            ! **How hot the line had become *by this mark*, in the run's own scale.** The count is\n            ! the view's own and it is a *running* one: how many times this line has been reached so\n            ! far. The band is that count against the *busiest line in the recording*, so the ramp is\n            ! **relative, not absolute** — every line starts cold, and the hottest line in the run is\n            ! what the top of the range means. That is what makes the picture work as a first\n            ! impression: a run whose busiest line is reached six times and one reached six thousand\n            ! both use the whole ramp, and neither is a flat colour.\n            !\n            ! Two earlier versions of this were wrong, and both are worth remembering. The first\n            ! shaded by the *clock* — `band x steps / maxSteps` — which reaches the top colour only at\n            ! the very last step of a run, where no mark sits, so a picture drawn that way shows blue,\n            ! violet and magenta and never red; Graham read the missing red off a screenshot before I\n            ! did. The second counted in a fixed ladder — one visit, two to three, four to seven,\n            ! eight or more — which is *absolute*: on a busy run every mark is at the top of the ramp\n            ! and the picture is a flat wash.\n            !\n            ! Counting here rather than reading the editor's `line_counts` also removes the one input\n            ! that could disagree with the marks: those counts arrive keyed to a different line from\n            ! the anchors that place the marks, by one or two, so a mark's colour was its\n            ! *neighbour's* heat. See the note in TODO.md.\n            !\n            ! `multiply` before `divide` because the runtime truncates every division and a\n            ! proportion computed the other way rounds to nothing on a big run.\n            put property VizLine of VizSeen into VizBand\n            if VizBand is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizBand\n            add 1 to VizBand\n            set property VizLine of VizSeen to VizBand\n            multiply VizBand by 3 giving VizCount\n            divide VizCount by VizMaxCount\n            if VizCount is greater than 3 put 3 into VizCount\n            put VizCount into VizBand\n\n            put 0 into VizOutside\n            if VizX is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizX is greater than VizPlotRight put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizY is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizY is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizOutside is 0\n            begin\n                if VizBand is 0 put VizHeatD cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatD\n                if VizBand is 1 put VizHeatE cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatE\n                if VizBand is 2 put VizHeatF cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatF\n                if VizBand is 3 put VizHeatG cat `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `h0.01` into VizHeatG\n            end\n        end\n        if VizCategory is `transfer`\n        begin\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps\n            add 0 to VizSteps\n            take VizViewX0 from VizSteps giving VizX\n            multiply VizX by VizPlotW\n            divide VizX by VizViewXW\n            add VizMargin to VizX\n            put property `from_line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            put VizLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            put VizTickPos into VizY\n            put property `to_line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            put VizLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            put VizTickPos into VizY2\n            ! One x decides a transfer, because a transfer is a vertical segment: its two ends differ\n            ! only in their line, so if the x is outside the window the whole segment is.\n            !\n            ! **And it is drawn whenever it *crosses* the window — in full, at its true ends.** Two earlier\n            ! answers to this were wrong in the same way. Clamping each end to the frame put an end on the\n            ! border rather than on a line, which reads as a fault; leaving the whole segment out then lost\n            ! the fact itself, because a transfer from off-screen is the one thing a picture of a *sliced*\n            ! run can only say by showing a line leave the frame. Graham's correction: *\"the panel\n            ! displaying all of each line and leaving the existing mask to hide what goes outside.\"* So the\n            ! ends stay where the lines are and the plot's nested viewport cuts them off, which is the same\n            ! mechanism the source picture is shown through. A transfer wholly above or wholly below the\n            ! window is still left out — it is not in the picture at all.\n            put 0 into VizOutside\n            if VizX is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizX is greater than VizPlotRight put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizY is less than VizMargin and VizY2 is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizY is greater than VizPlotBottom and VizY2 is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizOutside is 0\n            begin\n                ! The head is a chevron drawn in the path data, not a marker element — the plugin has\n                ! none, and a stroked chevron needs no fill, so it joins the segment's own stroke.\n                !\n                ! It goes at the *destination* end, `to_line`, with its wings behind the tip rather\n                ! than in front of it — and which side is behind depends on which way the segment\n                ! runs, so the wings are placed nine units back from the tip in the direction it came\n                ! from. No head is drawn when both ends are the same line: a head with no direction\n                ! would be claiming one.\n                put property `kind` of VizArgs into VizKind\n                take 5 from VizX giving VizHeadL\n                add 5 to VizX giving VizHeadR\n                if VizY2 is greater than VizY take 9 from VizY2 giving VizHeadY\n                if VizY2 is less than VizY add 9 to VizY2 giving VizHeadY\n                put `M` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY cat `L` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY2 into VizSegment\n                if VizY2 is not VizY put VizSegment cat `M` cat VizHeadL cat ` ` cat VizHeadY cat `L` cat VizX cat ` ` cat VizY2 cat `L` cat VizHeadR cat ` ` cat VizHeadY into VizSegment\n                ! One path per kind, because the colour is the kind. `branch` — the compiler's own\n                ! jumps — is left off rather than drawn faintly: scaffolding is not a program jump,\n                ! and drawing it would put the `if` and `while` machinery into the flow.\n                if VizKind is `call` put VizFlowCallD cat VizSegment into VizFlowCallD\n                if VizKind is `jump` put VizFlowJumpD cat VizSegment into VizFlowJumpD\n                if VizKind is `return` put VizFlowReturnD cat VizSegment into VizFlowReturnD\n            end\n        end\n        add 1 to VizIndex\n    end\n!! @hash 52ac5048\n!! @verified 52ac5048\n!!!\n!! The status line exists to be quoted.\n!!\n!! It carries the numbers raw rather than in words, because a reader copying a line into a bug report needs it exact and the picture needs it short.\n    ! ---- the status line ----\n    ! Percentages rather than the window sizes alone, because that is the reading a person recognises,\n    ! and the window as well, because a percentage names a size but not a position — two windows of the\n    ! same size over different parts of the run are the same number and different pictures. Multiplying\n    ! before dividing keeps the intermediate large: the runtime truncates every division, and a ratio\n    ! computed the other way rounds a 125% zoom to 1.\n    ! **The clip is stated, and it has to be** — but on its own row now, up on the bar, rather than leading\n    ! this line: once a clip is on, the `visit N of T` the sidebar reads counts the arrivals *in the range*\n    ! and the ramp's reddest mark is the range's busiest line, so a picture that did not say it was clipped\n    ! would be a picture that lies about both. It ran into the flow key's words when it led this line, which\n    ! is what moved it. See `VizClipEdge`.\n    !\n    ! **And it starts from empty.** This line is the accumulator, so dropping it as \"the clip's reset\" took\n    ! the reset away with it and the status line grew by a copy of itself on every draw — which is what the\n    ! harness caught, as every check that reads the window off this line.\n    put `` into VizStatusText\n    multiply 100 by VizFitXW giving VizStatusZoom\n    divide VizStatusZoom by VizViewXW\n    put VizStatusText cat `zoom x ` cat VizStatusZoom cat `% y ` into VizStatusText\n    multiply 100 by VizFitYH giving VizStatusZoom\n    divide VizStatusZoom by VizViewYH\n    put VizStatusText cat VizStatusZoom cat `%   ` into VizStatusText\n    put VizViewX0 into VizStatusEnd\n    add VizViewXW to VizStatusEnd\n    take 1 from VizStatusEnd\n    put VizStatusText cat `steps ` cat VizViewX0 cat `-` cat VizStatusEnd cat `, lines ` into VizStatusText\n    put VizViewY0 into VizStatusEnd\n    add VizViewYH to VizStatusEnd\n    take 1 from VizStatusEnd\n    set the text of VizStatus to VizStatusText cat VizViewY0 cat `-` cat VizStatusEnd\n    ! On the flow key's own row, at the left. It used to sit a row below, and the caption — which\n    ! printed the run's line range and step count, both of which are already in this line — sat\n    ! opposite the keys. Graham's reading: the caption repeats what is visible elsewhere, so the\n    ! status takes its row and the foot of the canvas loses one line.\n    move VizStatus to 60 711\n    ! **One line below it, and the pair is the whole of the foot.** The status says what the window is\n    ! showing; this says what the mark under the pointer is, which is the one thing a row of dots cannot\n    ! say for itself — every dot on a row names the same line and the same doc block, so the visit is\n    ! what tells them apart. Set here rather than on a press because a press leaves the picture alone:\n    ! the row is fixed for the life of the drawing, and `VizSelect` only changes the words.\n    move VizHitStatus to 60 728\n!! @hash 010002b4\n!! @verified 52b8170a\n!!!\n!! The axis is the picture's yardstick, and it is the same grid as the text.\n!!\n!! Its labels are placed with the mapping the marks use, so a label sits on the line it names at any zoom; a tick whose line falls outside the window is left blank rather than dragged to the edge, because the pool is four labels and a label that lies about where it sits is worse than an absent one.\n    ! ---- the axis ----\n    ! Four ticks a side, spaced by a third of the *window* rather than of the whole run, and placed\n    ! with the same mapping the marks use, so a label sits on the line it names at any zoom. A tick\n    ! whose line falls outside the window is left with no text rather than dragged to the edge: the\n    ! pool is four labels, and a label that lies about where it sits is worse than an absent one.\n    divide VizViewYH by 3 giving VizTickStep\n    if VizTickStep is less than 1 put 1 into VizTickStep\n    divide VizViewXW by 3 giving VizXStep\n    if VizXStep is less than 1 put 1 into VizXStep\n\n    put VizViewY0 into VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceY\n    move VizYLabel0 to 52 VizTickPos\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizYLabel0 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel0 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceY\n    move VizYLabel1 to 52 VizTickPos\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizYLabel1 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel1 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceY\n    move VizYLabel2 to 52 VizTickPos\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizYLabel2 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel2 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizTickStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceY\n    move VizYLabel3 to 52 VizTickPos\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizYLabel3 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotBottom set the text of VizYLabel3 to VizTickValue\n\n    ! **The step-axis labels sit one row lower than they did**, at 670 rather than 664: the steps\n    ! scrollbar runs along the foot of the frame and the labels' ascenders would otherwise touch it.\n    ! 670 still leaves the caption below them at 690 and the heat key beside it at 679 alone.\n    put VizViewX0 into VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    move VizXLabel0 to VizTickPos 670\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizXLabel0 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel0 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizXStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    move VizXLabel1 to VizTickPos 670\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizXLabel1 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel1 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizXStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    move VizXLabel2 to VizTickPos 670\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizXLabel2 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel2 to VizTickValue\n\n    add VizXStep to VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    move VizXLabel3 to VizTickPos 670\n    ! Cleared here rather than at the top of the draw, so that the blank and the value land in the\n    ! same uninterrupted run: a label set to nothing and left there is a label that flickers.\n    set the text of VizXLabel3 to ``\n    if VizTickPos is not less than VizMargin\n        and VizTickPos is not greater than VizPlotRight set the text of VizXLabel3 to VizTickValue\n!! @hash fafcd75b\n!! @verified fafcd75b\n!!!\n!! A rule is a leader, and the flow is drawn in full and masked.\n!!\n!! Each rule runs from one em past the last character of the line it names to the right-hand edge of the frame, so it points at its line instead of crossing it — the indentation and the first word are the part the eye reads first, and they are the part a rule drawn from the frame's left edge used to run straight through. And a transfer is drawn at its true ends whenever it *crosses* the window, the part outside the frame being cut off by the plot — a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, the same mechanism the source picture is shown through. **Two earlier answers to this were wrong in the same way:** clamping each end to the frame put an end on a border rather than on a line, which reads as a fault; leaving the whole segment out then lost the fact itself, because a line leaving the frame is the only way a picture of a *sliced* run can say that control came from somewhere off-screen.\n    ! ---- the rules ----\n    ! A hairline at every line the recording *names* — an arrival, or either end of a transfer — and at\n    ! no other line. The set is built as the recording is read, and not from the window's per-line\n    ! counts, which cover every line the run executed: on a recording that walks a chain of labels the\n    ! counts carry more than twice as many lines as anything happened on, and a rule for each of those\n    ! was a grid drawn over lines with nothing on them. Graham's \"too many of them, and too dark\".\n    !\n    ! It exists for the zoomed-out view, where a mark is a dot in mid-space and the text behind it is a\n    ! texture: the rule is what says which line the dot belongs to, and it spares a reader the choice\n    ! between zooming in to find out and hovering to ask the sidebar.\n    put `` into VizRulesD\n    put VizMinLine into VizRuleLine\n    add VizSpanLines to VizMinLine giving VizRuleEnd\n    while VizRuleLine is less than VizRuleEnd\n    begin\n        put ` ` cat VizRuleLine cat ` ` into VizRuledLine\n        if VizRuledLines includes VizRuledLine\n        begin\n            ! The line's own y, by the same `VizPlaceY` the marks and the labels use — so a rule runs\n            ! through the middle of the text it names rather than above it.\n            put VizRuleLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            put VizTickPos into VizRuleY\n            ! Only the rules in view. The loop runs over the whole run and the window is a slice of it,\n            ! so a line outside the window maps to a y outside the frame — and a rule drawn there\n            ! would be a line across the axis or the caption. The test is the marks' own test, and\n            ! inclusive for the same reason: a line on the very edge is on the edge, not outside it.\n            put 0 into VizOutside\n            if VizRuleY is less than VizMargin put 1 into VizOutside\n            if VizRuleY is greater than VizPlotBottom put 1 into VizOutside\n            ! **The rule is a leader, not a line through the code.** It begins one em past the last\n            ! character of the line it names and runs to the right-hand edge of the frame, so it points\n            ! at its line rather than crossing it. What it spares is the indentation and the first word —\n            ! the part that says what the line is, and the part the eye reads first.\n            !\n            ! The line's length is a count of *characters*, so it becomes a width by the same 8 units a\n            ! column that the picture is measured in, and then by the factor the picture is drawn at: the\n            ! pane holds `VizWindowW` of the document's units across `VizPlotW` of the frame. One em is\n            ! the text's own font size — `VizGlyphEm`, not a number here, because the gap cleared has to\n            ! be the size of the letters cleared or it is not a gap.\n            !\n            ! A row the picture does not draw has no characters to clear — a doc block is blank, see\n            ! `VizSourceMeasure` — so its rule begins at the left edge and is the longest kind there is.\n            ! The test is that one's, for the same reason: the left end is measured from what is drawn,\n            ! not from what the file says.\n            put `` into VizSourceLine\n            take 1 from VizRuleLine giving VizSourceAt\n            if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine\n            if VizSourceLine starts with `!!` put `` into VizSourceLine\n            multiply the length of VizSourceLine by 8 giving VizRuleX\n            add VizGlyphEm to VizRuleX\n            multiply VizRuleX by VizPlotW\n            divide VizRuleX by VizWindowW\n            add VizMargin to VizRuleX\n            ! **And when the line's own text reaches the frame's edge, the leader has nowhere to be.**\n            ! The text is cut off there at that zoom — the price of the anchoring, above — so a rule\n            ! beginning off the frame would leave its mark with no line to sit on, which is the one thing\n            ! a rule is for. Those lines keep the rule they had, from the frame's left edge: they are the\n            ! one case where crossing the text costs nothing, because what is crossed is already gone.\n            if VizRuleX is greater than VizPlotRight put VizMargin into VizRuleX\n            if VizRuleX is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizRuleX\n            ! To the frame's right-hand edge, which is as far as the drawing goes — unless the line is a\n            ! label, whose name is drawn at that edge: then the rule stops one em short of the name, so the\n            ! two read as a signpost beside the line rather than as one long line with a word on the end.\n            ! The space the name and the gap need is measured in the same document units the left end was.\n            take VizRuleX from VizPlotRight giving VizRuleWidth\n            gosub to VizLabelCheck\n            if VizIsLabel is 1\n            begin\n                multiply VizLabelLen by 8 giving VizLabelSpace\n                add VizGlyphEm to VizLabelSpace\n                multiply VizLabelSpace by VizPlotW\n                divide VizLabelSpace by VizWindowW\n                take VizLabelSpace from VizPlotRight giving VizLabelSpace\n                take VizRuleX from VizLabelSpace giving VizRuleWidth\n            end\n            if VizRuleWidth is less than 1 put 1 into VizRuleWidth\n            if VizOutside is 0 put VizRulesD cat `M` cat VizRuleX cat ` ` cat VizRuleY cat `h` cat VizRuleWidth into VizRulesD\n        end\n        add 1 to VizRuleLine\n    end\n!! @hash 2b7d71cf\n!! @verified 2b7d71cf\n!!!\n!! The source is anchored to the left edge of the pane, and it is the `viewBox`'s `x` that says so.\n!!\n!! The window's `y` moves over the document as the lines move and its height is the vertical zoom, but its `x` is always zero: what the recording does cannot drag the source sideways. The left-hand end of a line is the part that says what the line is — the indentation and the first word — so losing sight of it is losing the reading, and the window's `x` used to be derived from the horizontal window, which is exactly what took it away whenever the run was panned along. The price is the tail of a line wider than the frame, which is not reachable on screen at all now: the frame shows about a hundred and ten columns at the legible floor, so most lines fit and a few do not.\n    ! ---- the window on the source's picture ----\n    ! The document itself is built once, in `VizSourceMeasure`, and only when the source changes: it\n    ! holds *every* line of the file, so the rows line up with the axis and a line the run never\n    ! reached still has its row. What is rebuilt on a redraw is the window alone — the pane's `viewBox`\n    ! and the `href` naming the document.\n    gosub to VizSourcePicture\n    ! And the labels, which are rebuilt on every draw because they follow the window.\n    gosub to VizLabelNames\n    ! And the scrollbars, which are the same window read a second way.\n    gosub to VizBars\n    ! And the clip bar, which is the recording read a second way: the two markers stand where the range\n    ! is, and at the recording's ends when there is none.\n    gosub to VizClipBar\n\n    set attribute `d` of VizHeat0 to VizHeatD\n    set attribute `d` of VizHeat1 to VizHeatE\n    set attribute `d` of VizHeat2 to VizHeatF\n    set attribute `d` of VizHeat3 to VizHeatG\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat0 to VizDotSize\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat1 to VizDotSize\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat2 to VizDotSize\n    set attribute `stroke-width` of VizHeat3 to VizDotSize\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat0 to `round`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat1 to `round`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat2 to `round`\n    set attribute `stroke-linecap` of VizHeat3 to `round`\n    set attribute `d` of VizFlowCall to VizFlowCallD\n    set attribute `d` of VizFlowJump to VizFlowJumpD\n    set attribute `d` of VizFlowReturn to VizFlowReturnD\n    set attribute `d` of VizRules to VizRulesD\n    subtract DrawStarted from now giving DrawMillis\n    put 0 into VizDrawing\n    ! Whatever asked while this was running. Once, not in a loop: a request made *during* the draw\n    ! below is served by that draw's own end, and that is what makes a chain of them settle.\n    if VizPending is 1\n    begin\n        put 0 into VizPending\n        gosub to Draw\n    end\n    return\n!! @hash cca01dd3\n!! @verified ca7a095f\n!!!\n!! The gestures are four entry points, and this module registers them.\n!!\n!! `VizGrab` on pick, `VizPan` on drag, `VizWheel` on wheel and `VizReset` when a run is opened afresh. They live here rather than with the editor because the window is the view's own state and the events arrive many times a second — the handlers are registered at the top of this file, on the pane, and never travel as messages.\n!   ---- the gestures ----\n!   The host registers three events and calls in here: `VizGrab` on pick, `VizPan` on drag, and\n!   `VizWheel` on wheel. They live with the view rather than with the host because they are the\n!   view's own state; a host that wants the picture to move has only to wire them up. There are\n!   four entry points in all — those three and `VizReset` — and that is the whole of the interface.\n!\n!   Graham's scheme, from Kdenlive and Audacity: the wheel scrolls, shift-wheel zooms the lines,\n!   control-wheel the steps, and a drag pans. **The drag is the way the run is moved**, and the one\n!   gesture that asks for no modifier — Graham's own correction, having found the modifier pair for the\n!   scroll-across unnatural to reach for. That pair is *parked*, see `VizWheel`. The modifier therefore\n!   decides which axis a zoom applies to and the direction does not, which is why the wheel handler asks\n!   for `the wheel shift` rather than reading the sign of the amount.\nVizWheel:\n    ! Which gesture a notch is, is the modifiers' business, not the wheel's. Shift zooms the lines,\n    ! control zooms the steps, and with neither the wheel scrolls down the run, which is what it does\n    ! in every other application. Control is the horizontal modifier here against the usual pull of\n    ! the browser's own page zoom, which this takes over: the listener is registered on the pane and\n    ! calls `preventDefault`, so the page never sees it, and the pane is where the gesture is being\n    ! aimed.\n    !\n    ! **Both modifiers together used to scroll the steps, and that is parked.** It was a normalization\n    ! rather than a fourth gesture — two zooms at once is what the plain arithmetic gave, and the pair\n    ! was taken out here instead so the one test below still sorted a notch into a scroll or a zoom. It\n    ! worked, and Graham did not want it: *\"I've placed over-emphasis on the use of modifier keys … for\n    ! panning I found myself wanting to mouse click/drag as this seems to be the natural instinct.\"* The\n    ! drag does that job, so the binding is gone and the machinery is left standing: `VizScrollAcross` and\n    ! `VizScroll`'s second axis are still here, and **restoring the gesture is the three lines that set\n    ! the flags** — clear the two zooms and put 1 into `VizScrollAcross`. Nothing else refers to them.\n    !\n    ! **The gesture is never what waits.** This used to return at once when a draw was running, so a\n    ! slow draw swallowed every notch meanwhile — the reader had to repeat themselves, and at a deep\n    ! zoom on a big recording that is what \"the wheel has stopped working\" feels like. It now always\n    ! applies, and `VizRequest` is what remembers that a draw is owed.\n    put the wheel amount into VizWheelAmount\n    add 0 to VizWheelAmount\n    if VizWheelAmount is 0 return\n    put 0 into VizZoomAcross\n    put 0 into VizZoomDown\n    put 0 into VizScrollAcross\n    if the wheel shift is 1 put 1 into VizZoomDown\n    if the wheel control is 1 put 1 into VizZoomAcross\n    ! **Both modifiers together is ignored, which is not the same as deleting the test.** With no special\n    ! case the pair sets both flags and `VizZoom` applies both axes — the accidental two-at-once zoom that\n    ! the plain arithmetic gave before this file had any gesture for the pair. Graham asked for the pair to\n    ! go, so it goes: a notch with both modifiers down does nothing at all.\n    if VizZoomAcross is 1 and VizZoomDown is 1 return\n    put VizZoomAcross into VizMove\n    add VizZoomDown to VizMove\n    if VizMove is 0 gosub to VizScroll\n    if VizMove is not 0 gosub to VizZoom\n    return\n\nVizGrab:\n    ! Where the window stands when the drag begins, and **which control the drag is holding**: the\n    ! picture itself (0), the lines bar (1) or the steps bar (2). It is decided here because the move\n    ! cannot tell where the press was, and once only because a drag that changed its mind half way\n    ! would be two gestures in one.\n    !\n    ! The window is kept rather than accumulated: a pan measured step by step from the last event\n    ! drifts, because the runtime's division truncates and every step throws its remainder away.\n    ! Measured from the grab instead, the picture is where the pointer says it is, however many events\n    ! there have been.\n    put the pick position into VizGrabPos\n    put property `x` of VizGrabPos into VizGrabX\n    put property `y` of VizGrabPos into VizGrabY\n    put VizViewX0 into VizGrabFromX\n    put VizViewY0 into VizGrabFromY\n    put 0 into VizSlide\n    gosub to VizHandleAt\n    ! **A press on a control acts at once, and nothing else happens.** That is the settled interaction: a\n    ! drag only selects and a press is what commits, so the two are different gestures with different\n    ! consequences rather than one gesture with a side effect. `VizGrabbed` stays 0, so no drag follows a\n    ! control press and the drop that ends it finds nothing to release.\n    !\n    ! 5 is the window, 6 is the outside, 7 is the whole recording — and the first two are the same call,\n    ! `VizCut`, with `VizKeepTail` saying which. That is what keeps three controls from being three\n    ! answers to one question.\n    if VizSlide is 5 put 1 into VizKeepTail\n    if VizSlide is 6 put 2 into VizKeepTail\n    if VizSlide is 5 or VizSlide is 6 gosub to VizCut\n    if VizSlide is 7 gosub to VizUnclip\n    if VizSlide is 5 or VizSlide is 6 or VizSlide is 7 return\n    ! A press on the picture is also a *selection*. The bars are tested first because a press on one of\n    ! those is a grab rather than a pointing at a mark, which is why this sits behind the test above\n    ! rather than beside it — and a press that names no mark leaves the last selection alone.\n    if VizSlide is 0 gosub to VizSelect\n    put 1 into VizGrabbed\n    return\n\nVizRelease:\n    ! **A drag commits nothing, and that is the settled interaction.** It used to apply the clip on the\n    ! drop, which made the gesture that *selects* also the gesture that *acts* — so a range could not be\n    ! adjusted without paying for a cut, and a reader who had finished choosing could not change their mind\n    ! for free. The controls above the bar commit now; the markers are the reader's to move, and moving\n    ! them costs no drawing at all.\n    put 0 into VizGrabbed\n    return\n\nVizPan:\n    ! Move the window so the picture follows the pointer. Two scales sit between a pointer and a\n    ! window: the panel's pixels against the drawing's viewBox units, which differ because the\n    ! browser fits the drawing to the panel; and those units against the window, which differ\n    ! because the window is a slice of the run. Both are divisions, and the runtime truncates, so\n    ! each is multiplied out first — the same scaled-integer rule the marks use.\n    if VizGrabbed is 0 return\n    gosub to VizNote\n    put the drag position into VizDragPos\n    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX\n    put property `y` of VizDragPos into VizDragY\n    take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDx\n    take VizGrabY from VizDragY giving VizDy\n    gosub to VizPixelScale\n    multiply VizDx by 1000\n    divide VizDx by VizPixelsK\n    multiply VizDy by 1000\n    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK\n    multiply VizDx by VizViewXW\n    divide VizDx by VizPlotW\n    multiply VizDy by VizViewYH\n    divide VizDy by VizPlotH\n    take VizDx from VizGrabFromX giving VizViewX0\n    take VizDy from VizGrabFromY giving VizViewY0\n    gosub to VizClamp\n    gosub to VizRedraw\n    return\n\nVizPixelScale:\n    ! How long a viewBox unit is, in *thousandths of a pixel of the panel*. The drawing keeps its own\n    ! 1000x700 frame and the browser fits it to the panel with `meet`, so the scale is the smaller of\n    ! the two ratios. The thousandths are not decoration: the ratio is well under one, the runtime's\n    ! division truncates, and without them it would come out as zero and a drag would move nothing.\n    !\n    ! The panel is read from `VizHost` rather than from the canvas, and that is a limitation worth\n    ! naming: the canvas is an `svg`, and `svg` is the one element type the plugin registers without\n    ! the `dom` extra, so `the width of` will not compile for it. The host's box is the canvas's box\n    ! in practice, since the canvas is created to fill it.\n    ! **And the canvas is 740 tall, not 700.** The constant here was written for the canvas the view\n    ! started with; the extra forty units went in with the flow key and this line did not follow, so\n    ! every drag and every wheel moved the window 5.7% short of what the pointer did.\n    !\n    ! `VizPixelsW` and `VizPixelsH` are the panel's own two sides and are left *untouched*, because the\n    ! scrollbar's hit test needs them to work out how much of the panel the fitted canvas does not fill;\n    ! the height scaled to a thousand-wide canvas is a value in its own right and gets its own name,\n    ! which is a thing worth being strict about — the first version of the hit test read the scaled\n    ! number as though it were the height and put every hit 350 pixels out. See `VizHandleAt`.\n    put the width of VizHost into VizPixelsW\n    put the height of VizHost into VizPixelsH\n    multiply VizPixelsH by 1000 giving VizPaneH\n    divide VizPaneH by 740\n    put VizPaneH into VizPixelsK\n    if VizPixelsW is less than VizPixelsK put VizPixelsW into VizPixelsK\n    if VizPixelsK is less than 1 put 1 into VizPixelsK\n    return\n\nVizBars:\n    ! **The two bars, drawn from the window, and why the picture needs them.** Past a hundred per cent a\n    ! reader can see *where* they are — the axis says which lines are on screen — but not *how much* of\n    ! the run is on screen, and those are different questions. The vertical bar is the lines axis, the\n    ! horizontal the steps axis; each handle is the window's share of its axis, its length the fraction\n    ! in view — which is why the fit fills the bar — and its offset how far into the run the window has\n    ! gone.\n    !\n    ! That offset is scaled to the part of the bar the handle can *move* along rather than to the whole\n    ! bar, and the difference is the one thing worth stating: a handle of half the bar can travel half\n    ! the bar, so the ends of the run put it at the ends of the bar instead of three quarters of the way\n    ! along. Every scrollbar does this, and the reason is plain — the handle is a picture of the window,\n    ! and the window can be at an end of the run.\n    !\n    ! The lines bar needs no minimum: the legible floor holds the window to a tenth of even a short\n    ! file, so the handle cannot vanish. The steps axis can be zoomed to four steps out of thousands,\n    ! and a handle a quarter of a unit long is neither visible nor hittable, so ten units — the bar's\n    ! own thickness — is the floor. Where the true fraction is below it the handle is very slightly\n    ! longer than the truth it reports, which is what a real scrollbar does too.\n    put VizPlotH into VizBarLen\n    put VizSpanLines into VizBarExtent\n    put VizViewYH into VizBarSpan\n    put VizViewY0 into VizBarStart\n    put VizMinLine into VizBarOrigin\n    gosub to VizBarFit\n    put VizSlidePos into VizHandleVPos\n    put VizSlideSize into VizHandleVSize\n    add VizMargin to VizSlidePos\n    set attribute `y` of VizHandleV to VizSlidePos\n    set attribute `height` of VizHandleV to VizSlideSize\n\n    put VizPlotW into VizBarLen\n    ! The steps axis runs from `VizMinSteps` to `VizMaxSteps` — 0 and the run's last step when there\n    ! is no clip, the clip's own range when there is one. The origin is what the handle's position is\n    ! measured from, so the pair has to move together or a clipped bar would put the handle at the\n    ! wrong place along it.\n    put VizSpanSteps into VizBarExtent\n    put VizViewXW into VizBarSpan\n    put VizViewX0 into VizBarStart\n    put VizMinSteps into VizBarOrigin\n    gosub to VizBarFit\n    put VizSlidePos into VizHandleHPos\n    put VizSlideSize into VizHandleHSize\n    add VizMargin to VizSlidePos\n    set attribute `x` of VizHandleH to VizSlidePos\n    set attribute `width` of VizHandleH to VizSlideSize\n    return\n\nVizBarFit:\n    ! One bar, from five numbers about its axis: the bar's length, the size of the whole thing, the size\n    ! of the part in view, where that part starts, and where the whole thing starts. Two axes and one\n    ! rule, which is the whole point of writing it down once rather than twice.\n    !\n    ! reads VizBarLen, VizBarExtent, VizBarSpan, VizBarStart, VizBarOrigin\n    ! leaves VizSlideSize, VizSlidePos, VizSlideTravel, VizSlideRoom\n    multiply VizBarSpan by VizBarLen giving VizSlideSize\n    divide VizSlideSize by VizBarExtent\n    if VizSlideSize is less than 10 put 10 into VizSlideSize\n    if VizSlideSize is greater than VizBarLen put VizBarLen into VizSlideSize\n    take VizSlideSize from VizBarLen giving VizSlideTravel\n    take VizBarSpan from VizBarExtent giving VizSlideRoom\n    put 0 into VizSlidePos\n    ! At the fit there is no travel and nothing to divide by. That is not a fallback: it is the case\n    ! where the handle fills its bar and cannot be moved, so zero is the answer.\n    if VizSlideTravel is greater than 0\n    begin\n        take VizBarOrigin from VizBarStart giving VizSlidePos\n        multiply VizSlidePos by VizSlideTravel\n        divide VizSlidePos by VizSlideRoom\n    end\n    return\n\nVizDrag:\n    ! Which of the three ways to move a drag is, decided once by the press that began it: a pan of the\n    ! picture, or one of the two handles. A symbol rather than a handler body so that the gesture is one\n    ! entry point like the others — `VizGrab`, `VizWheel`, `VizReset` — and so a test can drive the real\n    ! path instead of the pair it would otherwise have to guess at.\n    if VizSlide is 0 gosub to VizPan\n    if VizSlide is 1 gosub to VizSlideTo\n    if VizSlide is 2 gosub to VizSlideTo\n    if VizSlide is 3 gosub to VizClipMove\n    if VizSlide is 4 gosub to VizClipMove\n    return\n\nVizHandleAt:\n    ! **Which control the pointer went down on — and why this is a hit test rather than a handler.** The\n    ! SVG plugin's shapes are not browser elements and the runtime's `on pick` accepts only those, so a\n    ! `rect` cannot be given a handler of its own: the pane's pick is the only one there is, and it has\n    ! to work out where it landed. The answer has to be in the *drawing's* units — the units the handles\n    ! are drawn in — and the pane's own box is not that, for the reason below.\n    !\n    ! The two boxes sit in different margins, so they cannot overlap and the order of the tests cannot\n    ! matter. Each is the drawn handle grown by four units: ten units is a thin thing to grab with a\n    ! mouse, and every scrollbar a reader has used lets them miss it slightly. The *troughs* are\n    ! deliberately not hit — a press there is a pan of the picture, which is the gesture the pane already\n    ! had, and a jump-to-page would be a second way to move that dragging back cannot undo.\n    !\n    ! So: from the pane's corner, less the letterbox, at the fitted scale. The letterbox is what the pan\n    ! does not care about and this does — the browser fits the canvas inside the pane and centres the\n    ! difference, so the canvas's top-left corner is *not* the pane's, and every unit of that offset is a\n    ! unit a hit test would be wrong by. It is half of what the canvas leaves over on each side, which\n    ! the pane's two sides and the scale between them give.\n    put the left of VizHost into VizPtrX\n    put the top of VizHost into VizPtrY\n    gosub to VizPixelScale\n    take VizPtrX from VizGrabX giving VizPtrX\n    take VizPtrY from VizGrabY giving VizPtrY\n    take VizPixelsK from VizPixelsW giving VizPtrA\n    divide VizPtrA by 2\n    take VizPtrA from VizPtrX\n    multiply VizPixelsK by 740 giving VizPtrA\n    divide VizPtrA by 1000\n    take VizPtrA from VizPixelsH giving VizPtrA\n    divide VizPtrA by 2\n    take VizPtrA from VizPtrY\n    multiply VizPtrX by 1000\n    divide VizPtrX by VizPixelsK\n    multiply VizPtrY by 1000\n    divide VizPtrY by VizPixelsK\n    if VizPtrX is greater than 940 and VizPtrX is less than 958\n    begin\n        take VizHandleVPos from VizPtrY giving VizPtrA\n        add 4 to VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is not less than 0\n        begin\n            take VizHandleVSize from VizPtrA\n            if VizPtrA is less than 5 put 1 into VizSlide\n        end\n    end\n    if VizPtrY is greater than 642 and VizPtrY is less than 660\n    begin\n        take VizHandleHPos from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        add 4 to VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is not less than 0\n        begin\n            take VizHandleHSize from VizPtrA\n            if VizPtrA is less than 5 put 2 into VizSlide\n        end\n    end\n    ! **The controls, tested before the bar's own strip.** They sit *in* that strip, so a press meant for\n    ! `keep` must not fall through to a marker standing under it. Each is its drawn box exactly: a control\n    ! is small and its box is a number, so there is no forgiveness to add and nothing to guess at — which\n    ! is the whole reason the box is a variable rather than a constant written twice.\n    take VizCtlY from VizPtrY giving VizPtrA\n    if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizCtlH\n    begin\n        take VizKeepX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizKeepW put 5 into VizSlide\n        take VizOuterX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizOuterW put 6 into VizSlide\n        take VizResetX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is greater than 0 and VizPtrA is less than VizResetW put 7 into VizSlide\n    end\n    ! **The clip bar's two markers, in the strip above the plot.** A band of its own, so this test cannot\n    ! be confused with anything else on the canvas — the plot begins at 60 and nothing else is drawn above\n    ! it — and each marker is the drawn one grown by four units, the same forgiveness the handles get. The\n    ! band between them is deliberately *not* hit: a press there is a pan of the picture, which is the\n    ! gesture the strip would otherwise take away.\n    if VizPtrY is greater than 24 and VizPtrY is less than 60\n    begin\n        take VizClipInX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is greater than -5 and VizPtrA is less than 5\n        begin\n            put 3 into VizSlide\n            put VizClipInX into VizGrabMarker\n        end\n        take VizClipOutX from VizPtrX giving VizPtrA\n        if VizPtrA is greater than -5 and VizPtrA is less than 5\n        begin\n            put 4 into VizSlide\n            put VizClipOutX into VizGrabMarker\n        end\n    end\n    return\n\nVizMarkAt:\n    ! **Which mark the pointer went down on, if any — and why this is a walk rather than a hit box.**\n    ! Every mark of a kind is one path, which is the same constraint that makes a layer cost string\n    ! building rather than DOM nodes, so there is nothing for the DOM to hit and the answer has to be\n    ! arithmetic — exactly as it is for the bars above. The walk is over the recording's *arrivals*\n    ! rather than over what was drawn, and it runs once per press: a pass of a few hundred integer\n    ! operations is the cheapest thing in this file, and it is paid only when somebody clicks.\n    !\n    ! The comparison is in the picture's own units, which is what `VizPlaceX` and `VizPlaceY` produce.\n    ! **Reads `VizPtrX`/`VizPtrY`, the pointer converted into those units** — `VizHandleAt` has already done that work by the time this runs, and converting a second time would be a second copy of the letterbox arithmetic, which is the one thing here that has already been got wrong once. Both mappings are *called* rather than written out again, for the reason they exist: a mark and a rule that disagreed about where a line is was a real fault, found by measurement.\n    !\n    ! The reach is the dot's own radius plus a few units. A mark is fourteen units across, which is a\n    ! hard thing to hit exactly, and every scrollbar a reader has used forgives a near miss. The\n    ! *nearest* mark wins rather than the first found: two arrivals a line apart are a row apart, so a\n    ! press between them is closer to one of them, and that is the one the reader is pointing at.\n    !\n    ! A mark whose line is off the window is skipped by the same reasoning the draw uses: it is not in\n    ! the picture, so it cannot be pointed at.\n    put 0 into VizHit\n    put json of `{}` into VizHitValues\n    put 1000000 into VizHitDist\n    divide VizDotSize by 2 giving VizHitReach\n    add 4 to VizHitReach\n    put 0 into VizHitI\n    ! **The running count of arrivals on each line, counted here as the walk goes.** This is the number the\n    ! dot's colour is drawn from — the draw counts the same thing in the same order over the same events —\n    ! and it is the one thing that tells two dots on a row apart, since the line and the doc block they name\n    ! are identical. Counting it here rather than reading the draw's own table keeps the two halves of a\n    ! report (this arrival's number, and the line's total) from the same pass, so they can never disagree.\n    put json of `{}` into VizHitSeen\n    put 0 into VizHitVisit\n    put 0 into VizHitTotal\n    while VizEvents has element VizHitI\n    begin\n        put element VizHitI of VizEvents into VizEvent\n        if property `cat` of VizEvent is `anchor`\n        begin\n            put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps\n            add 0 to VizSteps\n            put VizSteps into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceX\n            put VizTickPos into VizHitX\n            put property `line` of VizArgs into VizLine\n            add 0 to VizLine\n            ! This arrival's number on its line, which is the count *including* it: the first dot on a row\n            ! is visit 1. The guard mirrors the draw's, for the same reason — an absent property reads as\n            ! nothing at all rather than as zero, and one arithmetic step away from that is a number that\n            ! is not a count.\n            put property VizLine of VizHitSeen into VizHitCount\n            if VizHitCount is not greater than 0 put 0 into VizHitCount\n            add 1 to VizHitCount\n            set property VizLine of VizHitSeen to VizHitCount\n            put VizLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            put VizTickPos into VizHitY\n            ! Outside the frame is outside the picture, and the margin is where the frame begins.\n            if VizHitX is not less than VizMargin and VizHitX is not greater than VizPlotRight\n            begin\n                if VizHitY is not less than VizMargin and VizHitY is not greater than VizPlotBottom\n                begin\n                    take VizPtrX from VizHitX giving VizHitDx\n                    take VizPtrY from VizHitY giving VizHitDy\n                    ! Absolute without a function: the language has no `abs`, and the axis clamp\n                    ! spells the same test out.\n                    if VizHitDx is less than 0 multiply VizHitDx by -1\n                    if VizHitDy is less than 0 multiply VizHitDy by -1\n                    if VizHitDx is not greater than VizHitReach and VizHitDy is not greater than VizHitReach\n                    begin\n                        ! Squared, so no square root is needed and the comparison is exact — the\n                        ! scaled-integer rule this file uses everywhere a proportion is wanted.\n                        multiply VizHitDx by VizHitDx giving VizHitNear\n                        multiply VizHitDy by VizHitDy giving VizHitTmp\n                        add VizHitTmp to VizHitNear\n                        if VizHitNear is less than VizHitDist\n                        begin\n                            put VizHitNear into VizHitDist\n                            put VizLine into VizHit\n                            put VizHitCount into VizHitVisit\n                            ! **`@show N, Total` arrives with the arrival it belongs to**, so the values\n                            ! travel with the mark and the sidebar never has to find the trace again. They are\n                            ! taken here, where the winning arrival is known, rather than off the drawn dots —\n                            ! a dot is not where the values live, and the walk already has the event.\n                            put property `values` of VizArgs into VizHitValues\n                        end\n                    end\n                end\n            end\n        end\n        add 1 to VizHitI\n    end\n    return\n\nVizSelect:\n    ! **The pane reports and does not decide.** Which doc block holds a line is the editor's model and\n    ! the sidebar's to show, so one small dict goes back and nothing here changes on screen — the panel\n    ! beside the picture *is* the feedback. It travels on a press rather than on a move, for the same\n    ! reason the gestures are registered here: a report per pointer move would put a message round trip\n    ! inside every drag.\n    !\n    ! A press that names no mark reports nothing rather than reporting \"nothing found\". The selection\n    ! the reader made a moment ago is still what they are looking at, and a pan that began on empty\n    ! picture would otherwise clear the panel on its way past.\n    gosub to VizMarkAt\n    if VizHit is 0 return\n    ! The line's own total, read out of the table the walk filled: a reader told \"visit 3\" has no way to\n    ! know whether that is all of them or the third of forty, and the answer is already in hand.\n    put property VizHit of VizHitSeen into VizHitTotal\n    ! **And said here, where both numbers are in hand, rather than sent to be said elsewhere.** The\n    ! wording is the panel's own — the subject first, then the figures, in plain words rather than\n    ! symbols, because what a reader copies out of a status line should still read as a sentence. The\n    ! total can be missing (a report from a pane that did not count) and \"visit 3 of 0\" would be worse\n    ! than saying less.\n    put `line ` cat VizHit cat `   visit ` cat VizHitVisit into VizHitText\n    if VizHitTotal is greater than 0 put VizHitText cat ` of ` cat VizHitTotal into VizHitText\n    set the text of VizHitStatus to VizHitText\n    put `{}` into VizMark\n    set property `kind` of VizMark to `mark`\n    set property `line` of VizMark to VizHit\n    set property `visit` of VizMark to VizHitVisit\n    set property `total` of VizMark to VizHitTotal\n    ! Only when the script said something worth watching: an empty dict would tell the sidebar to print\n    ! nothing, which is the same as saying nothing at all, and one message shape is easier to read.\n    if VizHitValues is not empty set property `values` of VizMark to VizHitValues\n    send VizMark to parent\n    return\n\nVizSlideTo:\n    ! **Holding a handle, which is not a pan.** Holding the picture moves the window one unit per unit of\n    ! pointer travel, so the drawing stays under the finger. Holding a *handle* moves the whole run past\n    ! the window instead, so the handle follows the pointer — the two differ by the ratio of the run to\n    ! the window, which is exactly what the handle's length already says. That ratio is why the familiar\n    ! mapping is the right one rather than decoration: a drag that moved one line per line of handle\n    ! would take a hundred drags to cross a hundred-line file.\n    !\n    ! The travel is read from what is *drawn*, so a drag can never disagree with the bar in front of the\n    ! reader; the window it moves from is the one `VizGrab` kept.\n    if VizGrabbed is 0 return\n    if VizSlide is 0 return\n    gosub to VizNote\n    put the drag position into VizDragPos\n    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX\n    put property `y` of VizDragPos into VizDragY\n    if VizSlide is 1\n    begin\n        take VizGrabY from VizDragY giving VizDy\n        take VizHandleVSize from VizPlotH giving VizSlideTravel\n        take VizViewYH from VizSpanLines giving VizSlideRoom\n    end\n    if VizSlide is 2\n    begin\n        take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDy\n        take VizHandleHSize from VizPlotW giving VizSlideTravel\n        take VizViewXW from VizSpanSteps giving VizSlideRoom\n    end\n    ! At the fit the handle fills its bar: no travel, no room, nothing to move. This is what makes a\n    ! hundred per cent immovable rather than merely hard to move.\n    if VizSlideTravel is less than 1 return\n    if VizSlideRoom is less than 1 return\n    gosub to VizPixelScale\n    multiply VizDy by 1000\n    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK\n    multiply VizDy by VizSlideRoom\n    divide VizDy by VizSlideTravel\n    if VizSlide is 1\n    begin\n        put VizGrabFromY into VizViewY0\n        add VizDy to VizViewY0\n    end\n    if VizSlide is 2\n    begin\n        put VizGrabFromX into VizViewX0\n        add VizDy to VizViewX0\n    end\n    gosub to VizClamp\n    gosub to VizRedraw\n    return\n\nVizScroll:\n    ! The wheel alone scrolls the run, and both modifiers scroll it *across*: either way the step is\n    ! a quarter of the window on the axis being scrolled rather than a number of lines or steps, so\n    ! it feels the same at every zoom — a fixed step is a nudge on a long run and a leap on a short\n    ! one, which is the prototype's lesson in its smallest form. The two axes are one subroutine\n    ! because the axis is the only thing that differs between them, and `VizScrollAcross` — set by\n    ! `VizWheel` — is what says which.\n    !\n    ! **The sign is the whole of the direction, and it went missing.** This read the amount but never\n    ! asked whether it was positive, so every notch walked the same way down the file — Graham's \"the\n    ! page scrolls up whichever way I roll\". The zoom always had its sign (`VizZoom` tests it); the\n    ! scroll did not.\n    gosub to VizNote\n    divide VizViewYH by 4 giving VizMove\n    if VizScrollAcross is 1 divide VizViewXW by 4 giving VizMove\n    if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove\n    if VizWheelAmount is less than 0 take VizMove from 0 giving VizMove\n    if VizScrollAcross is 1 add VizMove to VizViewX0\n    if VizScrollAcross is not 1 add VizMove to VizViewY0\n    gosub to VizClamp\n    gosub to VizRedraw\n    return\n\nVizZoom:\n    ! A notch in or out, on whichever axes `VizWheel` marked. Two steps rather than one, and they are\n    ! exact inverses of each other: in shrinks a window by a fifth and out grows it by a quarter, and\n    ! four fifths times five quarters is one, so a notch in and a notch out land back where they\n    ! started. A single step could not do that here, because the runtime truncates every division.\n    !\n    ! The two axes are independent because the questions are: the steps are *when* and the lines are\n    ! *where*, and how far apart two arrivals are in time says nothing about how far apart the lines\n    ! they land on are. An axis that is not marked is left exactly as it was.\n    gosub to VizNote\n    if VizWheelAmount is greater than 0 go to VizZoomOut\n    if VizZoomAcross is 1\n    begin\n        divide VizViewXW by 5 giving VizMove\n        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove\n        take VizMove from VizViewXW\n    end\n    if VizZoomDown is 1\n    begin\n        divide VizViewYH by 5 giving VizMove\n        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove\n        take VizMove from VizViewYH\n    end\n    go to VizZoomSettled\nVizZoomOut:\n    if VizZoomAcross is 1\n    begin\n        divide VizViewXW by 4 giving VizMove\n        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove\n        add VizMove to VizViewXW\n    end\n    if VizZoomDown is 1\n    begin\n        divide VizViewYH by 4 giving VizMove\n        if VizMove is less than 1 put 1 into VizMove\n        add VizMove to VizViewYH\n    end\nVizZoomSettled:\n    ! The *size* is clamped before the origin is moved to follow it, and that order is the whole of\n    ! this. `VizClamp` has the last word on how small a window may get, so a centring computed from\n    ! the size before the clamp slides the window every time a notch is refused: at the legible floor\n    ! each notch shortened the window by a fifth, the clamp put it back, and the origin kept the\n    ! centring's share of a change that never happened — which is \"the wheel scrolls up one line\".\n    gosub to VizClamp\n    ! Now hold the middle still, using the size the clamp actually allowed.\n    take VizViewXW from VizWasXW giving VizMove\n    divide VizMove by 2\n    add VizMove to VizViewX0\n    take VizViewYH from VizWasYH giving VizMove\n    divide VizMove by 2\n    add VizMove to VizViewY0\n    gosub to VizClamp\n    gosub to VizRedraw\n    return\n\nVizNote:\n    ! What the window was before a gesture, so the gesture can tell whether it changed anything. Kept\n    ! here rather than re-derived, because `VizClamp` writes the window in place and the old value is\n    ! gone by the time the answer is wanted.\n    put VizViewX0 into VizWasX0\n    put VizViewXW into VizWasXW\n    put VizViewY0 into VizWasY0\n    put VizViewYH into VizWasYH\n    return\n\nVizRequest:\n    ! Ask for a draw without ever losing the asking. Everything that wants the picture rebuilt comes\n    ! through here — a gesture that moved the window, a new run handed over by the editor — so a\n    ! request arriving while a draw is running is remembered rather than dropped, and `Draw` serves it\n    ! on the way out. Drawing then reads the window as it stands *at that moment*, so a burst of\n    ! notches during one draw costs one draw more and lands on the latest of them.\n    if VizDrawing is 1\n    begin\n        put 1 into VizPending\n        return\n    end\n    gosub to Draw\n    return\n\nVizRedraw:\n    ! A draw only if the window actually moved. A refused gesture — a zoom at its limit, a pan against\n    ! the edge of the run — would otherwise redraw the whole picture to produce the picture that is\n    ! already on screen, which is wasted work and a visible flash on a slow machine.\n    put 0 into VizMoved\n    if VizViewX0 is not VizWasX0 put 1 into VizMoved\n    if VizViewXW is not VizWasXW put 1 into VizMoved\n    if VizViewY0 is not VizWasY0 put 1 into VizMoved\n    if VizViewYH is not VizWasYH put 1 into VizMoved\n    if VizMoved is 1 gosub to VizRequest\n    return\n\nVizClamp:\n    ! Keep the window inside the run, and never empty. The upper clamps are the outer limit of both\n    ! gestures — the whole picture is as far as either can go — and they double as the way back to\n    ! it, so the pane needs no reset button. The lower ones matter only on a tiny run: a window of\n    ! under four units maps to under four pixels, and every division above would then round away.\n    ! No zoom past the size the text is written at — see `VizGlyphRows`. It is applied *before* the\n    ! clamp to the run, so a recording shorter than that many lines simply has no vertical zoom in it,\n    ! which is right: the whole run is already legible and there is nothing to zoom into.\n    if VizViewYH is less than VizGlyphRows put VizGlyphRows into VizViewYH\n    ! The steps axis is bounded by the *run* the picture is of, which is `VizMinSteps` to `VizMaxSteps`\n    ! — 0 to the last step with no clip, the clip's own range with one. `VizSpanSteps` is that width,\n    ! and the lower clamp is where the run now begins, so both gestures stop at the clip's edges\n    ! exactly as they stop at the recording's when there is no clip.\n    if VizViewXW is greater than VizSpanSteps put VizSpanSteps into VizViewXW\n    if VizViewYH is greater than VizSpanLines put VizSpanLines into VizViewYH\n    if VizViewXW is less than 4 put 4 into VizViewXW\n    if VizViewYH is less than 4 put 4 into VizViewYH\n    if VizViewX0 is less than VizMinSteps put VizMinSteps into VizViewX0\n    if VizViewY0 is less than VizMinLine put VizMinLine into VizViewY0\n    put VizViewX0 into VizViewTemp\n    add VizViewXW to VizViewTemp\n    if VizViewTemp is greater than VizMaxSteps\n    begin\n        take VizViewXW from VizMaxSteps giving VizViewX0\n    end\n    add VizSpanLines to VizMinLine giving VizViewTemp\n    put VizViewY0 into VizMove\n    add VizViewYH to VizMove\n    if VizMove is greater than VizViewTemp\n    begin\n        take VizViewYH from VizViewTemp giving VizViewY0\n    end\n    return\n\nVizReset:\n    ! Fit the window to the run again on the next draw. The view calls this itself when the recording\n    ! changes — see the comparison at the fit in `Draw`, which is where \"a different run\" is decided —\n    ! and it stays an entry point because a host may have its own reason to start again. It is no\n    ! longer what a visit does: leaving the pane and coming back keeps the window, which is what the\n    ! reader wants and what a reset on entry destroyed.\n    put 0 into VizViewFit\n    return\n\nVizRuleAdd:\n    ! Note a line the recording names, once. The rules are drawn for *these* lines and not for every\n    ! line the run executed, which is what the window's counts carry: on a recording that walks a\n    ! chain of labels the counts cover more than twice as many lines as carry anything, and a grid\n    ! that dense stops being a guide. A line qualifies if an arrival landed on it or a transfer began\n    ! or ended on it — which is exactly the set of lines the picture has something to say about.\n    !\n    ! The set is a padded string and the test is `includes`, because the language has one of those and\n    ! no set type. The dedupe is not an optimisation: a rule is a line drawn right across the frame,\n    ! so a line visited two hundred times would contribute two hundred identical rules to the same\n    ! path attribute — invisible, and a waste that grows with the recording.\n    put ` ` cat VizLine cat ` ` into VizRuledLine\n    if VizRuledLines includes VizRuledLine return\n    put VizRuledLines cat VizRuledLine into VizRuledLines\n    return\n\nVizSourceMeasure:\n!! @hash 0e1bde0c\n!! @verified dc725895\n!!!\n!! And a doc block is not drawn in it.\n!!\n!! Every line of the file keeps its row, and a line belonging to a doc block — the `!!` prose, the `@hash`/`@verified` pair, the `!!!` terminator — is drawn as an empty row. The picture is of the *program*, and on a file written to this convention the prose is longer than the code by a wide margin and buries the flow; a bare `!` comment is a note to the line beside it rather than a block, so it stays. Blanked rather than skipped, because the rows are what the axis, the rules and the marks line up with: the pane's numbering still matches the editor's, and that is what makes the two views comparable at all.\n!! **The trap this had to dodge:** numbers read out of the trace stay *text*, and text compares lexically — `9` is not less than `10` — so the range-finding below converts with `add 0 to …` before it compares anything. Without that the picture is silently wrong, which is the failure mode the project's own notes warn about.\n    ! ---- reading the source, and drawing it once ----\n    ! `json split` with no `on` splits on the newline, and it is the one to use: it leaves a JSON array\n    ! behind, which `element N of` can read. The plain `split` does not — it stores value objects, and\n    ! `element N of` tries to `JSON.parse` each one and reports \"Can't parse JSON\".\n    !\n    ! Every row of the file is drawn here, once, at the size the text is written at, and nothing about\n    ! it changes afterwards: a zoom or a pan moves the *window* rather than redrawing the picture. That\n    ! is the whole point of the pane, and it is also why the width is measured over every line — the\n    ! picture's proportions are fixed by its longest line, and the window decides how much of it is in\n    ! view.\n    json split VizSource giving VizSourceLines\n    put the json count of VizSourceLines into VizSourceCount\n    ! The em of the text, in the document's own units — the size the rows are written at, and therefore\n    ! the width of the letters a rule has to clear before it begins. Kept as a value rather than written\n    ! into the `<text>` element below as a number, because the rules' arithmetic is a second reader of\n    ! it: two spellings of one measurement is how a gap drifts.\n    put 13 into VizGlyphEm\n    ! **The picture is the whole file, and the recording is a window over it.** Drawing only the lines\n    ! the run touched made the pane a picture of the run with no way to see it in context, and it left\n    ! the window unable to travel past the last line that happened to execute — a scroll that stops\n    ! dead for no visible reason, which is what Graham hit. So the extent here is 1..the file's own\n    ! line count, every line is drawn, and the marks, the rules and the heat are what say which part\n    ! ran. That is the more informative picture as well as the more navigable one: a stretch with no\n    ! marks at all now reads as a stretch that did not run, which is a fact about the program.\n    put 1 into VizMinLine\n    put VizSourceCount into VizMaxLine\n    take VizMinLine from VizMaxLine giving VizSpanLines\n    add 1 to VizSpanLines\n    put 0 into VizGlyphMax\n    put VizMinLine into VizSourceIndex\n    while VizSourceIndex is not greater than VizMaxLine\n    begin\n        take 1 from VizSourceIndex giving VizSourceAt\n        if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount\n        begin\n            put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine\n            if the length of VizSourceLine is greater than VizGlyphMax put the length of VizSourceLine into VizGlyphMax\n        end\n        add 1 to VizSourceIndex\n    end\n    if VizGlyphMax is less than 1 put 1 into VizGlyphMax\n    multiply VizGlyphMax by 8 giving VizGlyphWidth\n    multiply VizSpanLines by 18 giving VizGlyphHeight\n\n    ! **Every row of the file is drawn, and each carries its own absolute `y`.** It used to be one\n    ! `<text>` whose rows were placed by `dy=\"18\"` and accumulated — the cheaper document, and the one\n    ! that came apart: a `<tspan>` with no content does not advance the text position, so a row that\n    ! draws nothing collapses and every row below it is drawn too high. See the note where the rows are\n    ! built; the drift is the number of empty rows above a line, which is why blanking the doc blocks\n    ! made an invisible fault visible. `y=\"-4\"` on the element keeps the first row's descenders off the\n    ! document's edge for a reader of the source; the rows themselves name their own place.\n    put `<text x=\"0\" y=\"-4\" xml:space=\"preserve\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"` cat VizGlyphEm cat `\" fill=\"#5b6472\">` into VizGlyphBody\n    ! **The redaction is built here, in the same pass, and that is not an economy — it is the design.**\n    ! The bars are a function of the *source*, not of the window, so they belong with the picture that is\n    ! built once per run rather than with the marks and rules that are rebuilt on every draw. A zoom or a\n    ! pan therefore costs nothing for them, and the `d` string is written once. Had they been drawn in the\n    ! frame's units instead they would have had to be re-laid-out on every zoom — which is where the cost\n    ! Graham expected would have come from, and why it does not arise.\n    put `` into VizRedactD\n    put VizMinLine into VizSourceIndex\n    while VizSourceIndex is not greater than VizMaxLine\n    begin\n        put `` into VizSourceLine\n        take 1 from VizSourceIndex giving VizSourceAt\n        if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount\n        begin\n            put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine\n        end\n        ! **A doc block is drawn as an empty row.** The picture is of the *program*, and a doc block is\n        ! the part of the file that is not the program: on a file written to this convention the prose\n        ! is longer than the code by a wide margin, and it buries the flow. Everything the convention\n        ! adds to a file is under `!!` — the prose, the `@hash`/`@verified` pair and the `!!!` terminator\n        ! — so one test covers the lot. A bare `!` comment is left alone: it is a note to the line beside\n        ! it rather than a block, and the reader of the picture is reading the code.\n        !\n        ! **Blanked rather than skipped, and that is the whole of the care taken here.** Every row is\n        ! drawn, and the rows are what the axis, the rules and the marks line up with — so a blanked line\n        ! keeps its number and the pane's numbering still matches the editor's. Skipping it would\n        ! renumber the file from its first doc block onwards, which is the one thing this must not do.\n        if VizSourceLine starts with `!!` put `` into VizSourceLine\n        ! **This row's bar, and it is measured off the text rather than chosen.** Its width is the line's\n        ! own length at the document's eight units a column — the same measure the rules use for the code\n        ! they lead away from, so the two agree by construction about where a line ends. Its `y` is the\n        ! *top* of the row and its height is the whole eighteen units, which is what puts the leading above\n        ! and below the glyphs inside the bar as well as the glyphs themselves.\n        !\n        ! A row that draws nothing gets no bar, and gets none rather than a zero-width one: the blanked\n        ! doc blocks are most of a file written to this convention, and a zero-width rectangle per one of\n        ! them would be several hundred pieces of path data saying nothing at all.\n        if VizSourceLine is not empty\n        begin\n            multiply the length of VizSourceLine by 8 giving VizRedactW\n            take VizMinLine from VizSourceIndex giving VizRedactY\n            multiply VizRedactY by 18\n            put VizRedactD cat `M0 ` cat VizRedactY cat `h` cat VizRedactW cat `v18h-` cat VizRedactW cat `z` into VizRedactD\n        end\n        ! A line the run names but the file does not have is left as an empty row rather than skipped:\n        ! the rows are what lines up with the axis, so a missing one has to keep its place.\n        !\n        ! Three entities, and the ampersand first — escaping it after the others would escape the `&`\n        ! of the escapes just written.\n        replace `&` with `&amp;` in VizSourceLine\n        replace `<` with `&lt;` in VizSourceLine\n        replace `>` with `&gt;` in VizSourceLine\n        ! **Each row is placed at its own absolute `y`, and that is not a detail.** The rows used to be\n        ! positioned by `dy=\"18\"` alone — the offset accumulating as the text was laid out, so a whole\n        ! file could be one element with no per-line coordinate computed at all. That reads as the\n        ! cheaper picture, and it is wrong: a `<tspan>` with **no content does not advance the text\n        ! position**, so every row that draws nothing collapses and every row below it is drawn that much\n        ! too high. With fourteen blank lines in a file of six hundred the drift was under a row and\n        ! invisible; blanking the doc blocks added a hundred and seventy-eight more, and the picture came\n        ! apart — Graham's *\"the whole graph is still misaligned with the text\"*, and it was neither: the\n        ! text was moved, by exactly the number of empty rows above each line.\n        !\n        ! So the saving is gone and the coordinate is computed. It is the one thing a renderer cannot\n        ! skip: an empty row is still given its `y`, and the row *after* it names its own place and does\n        ! not care what the empty one did. `14` is where the first row's baseline sits inside its\n        ! eighteen-unit row — four units down for the descenders, as the element's own `y` used to say,\n        ! plus the row it is in.\n        take VizMinLine from VizSourceIndex giving VizRowY\n        multiply VizRowY by 18\n        add 14 to VizRowY\n        put VizGlyphBody cat `<tspan x=\"0\" y=\"` cat VizRowY cat `\">` cat VizSourceLine cat `</tspan>` into VizGlyphBody\n        add 1 to VizSourceIndex\n    end\n    put VizGlyphBody cat `</text>` into VizGlyphBody\n    ! The bars go up in one attribute, which is the same economy the marks live under: an AllSpeak element\n    ! is a declared symbol, so N bars are one path rather than N elements.\n    set attribute `d` of VizRedact to VizRedactD\n\n    ! Encoded once, here, because the picture is never redrawn: a data URL has two characters an SVG\n    ! document cannot spell for it, and `%` goes first or the `#` replacement would have its own `%`\n    ! turned into `%25`. The header written per draw has neither, so re-encoding it is not needed.\n    replace `%` with `%25` in VizGlyphBody\n    replace `#` with `%23` in VizGlyphBody\n\n    set attribute `width` of VizGlyphs to VizGlyphWidth\n    set attribute `height` of VizGlyphs to VizGlyphHeight\n    return\n\nVizLabelNames:\n!! @hash da070898\n!! @verified da070898\n!!!\n!! Every label is named at the right-hand edge, and the rules make room for it.\n!!\n!! A label is a line at the left margin ending in a colon — the pane is given the script as text and nothing else, and the recording carries no label names, so `VizLabelCheck` is where a label is recognised — and its name, without the colon, is drawn level with its own line in a strong indigo the picture uses nowhere else. The rule for such a line stops one em short of the name, so the two read as a signpost beside the line rather than as one long line with a word on the end. It began as a diagnostic, at Graham's suggestion, to test whether the drawing and the text agreed about where a line is; it settled that, then caught a fault no check could see, and he kept it because a column of names down the edge is a way to navigate a long file — so it stays, and it stays honest about its origin.\n    ! ---- every label, named at the right-hand end of its own line ----\n    ! **This began as a diagnostic and stays because it earns its place.** Graham asked for it to test the\n    ! alignment — \"render the name of that label onto the right-hand end of its line, using the same size\n    ! font\" — and it did that job twice over: it showed that the drawing's mapping was right, and later\n    ! that the picture's *rows* were being misplaced. He then kept it on his own judgement: it collides\n    ! with nothing, and a column of label names down the right-hand edge is a way to navigate a long file.\n    !\n    ! It is *folded into the drawing* rather than drawn beside it: each name is put where `VizPlaceY` says\n    ! its line is, in the same font at the same size as the source, in a strong indigo the picture uses\n    ! nowhere else. So it is also a standing check on the mapping — if the names ever stop sitting level\n    ! with the lines they name, the mapping has moved again — which is worth knowing given that the last\n    ! fault of that kind cost a morning.\n    !\n    ! **The numbers are what put it on the line rather than near it.** The size is the source's thirteen\n    ! units scaled by the same factor the picture is — `VizPlotW` across `VizWindowW` — and rounded rather\n    ! than truncated, because a fifth of a font size shows where a fifth of a unit does not. The drop is\n    ! where a source row's baseline sits: five document units below the middle of the row, a row being\n    ! eighteen tall with the baseline fourteen below its top. Both are derived from the same numbers the\n    ! picture is drawn from.\n    !\n    ! **And it draws nothing when the copy would be too small to read**, clearing the layer rather than\n    ! leaving it: below four units the picture's own text is a smudge, and a name that cannot be compared\n    ! with the line it names is noise. Clearing matters more than the threshold — an annotation left behind\n    ! by a zoom is a stale artefact, and a stale artefact in a picture is a wrong answer.\n    multiply 13 by VizPlotW giving VizLabelSize\n    divide VizWindowW by 2 giving VizLabelDrop\n    add VizLabelDrop to VizLabelSize\n    divide VizLabelSize by VizWindowW\n    if VizLabelSize is less than 1 put 1 into VizLabelSize\n    multiply 5 by VizPlotW giving VizLabelDrop\n    divide VizLabelDrop by VizWindowW\n    if VizLabelSize is less than 4\n    begin\n        set attribute `href` of VizLabelText to `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1000\" height=\"740\"></svg>`\n        return\n    end\n\n    put `<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1000\" height=\"740\">` into VizLabelBody\n    put VizViewY0 into VizLabelLine\n    put VizViewY0 into VizLabelEnd\n    add VizViewYH to VizLabelEnd\n    while VizLabelLine is less than VizLabelEnd\n    begin\n        take 1 from VizLabelLine giving VizSourceAt\n        put `` into VizSourceLine\n        if VizSourceAt is less than VizSourceCount put element VizSourceAt of VizSourceLines into VizSourceLine\n        if VizSourceLine starts with `!!` put `` into VizSourceLine\n        gosub to VizLabelCheck\n        if VizIsLabel is 1\n        begin\n            ! The name without its colon: it is a signpost, and the colon belongs to the line of code the\n            ! pane does not draw. `left` of the line is the language's own way to say that, and it is done\n            ! before the escaping below so the length is the character count and not a byte count.\n            put left VizLabelLen of VizSourceLine into VizSourceLine\n            ! The entities first, ampersand first, as the picture does — and the `%`/`#` pass at the end\n            ! covers the colour written into the text element below.\n            replace `&` with `&amp;` in VizSourceLine\n            replace `<` with `&lt;` in VizSourceLine\n            replace `>` with `&gt;` in VizSourceLine\n            put VizLabelLine into VizTickValue\n            gosub to VizPlaceY\n            add VizLabelDrop to VizTickPos giving VizLabelY\n            put VizLabelBody\n                cat `<text x=\"` cat VizPlotRight cat `\" y=\"` cat VizLabelY cat `\" font-family=\"monospace\" font-size=\"` cat VizLabelSize\n                cat `\" fill=\"#4338ca\" text-anchor=\"end\">` cat VizSourceLine cat `</text>`\n                into VizLabelBody\n        end\n        add 1 to VizLabelLine\n    end\n    put VizLabelBody cat `</svg>` into VizLabelBody\n    replace `%` with `%25` in VizLabelBody\n    replace `#` with `%23` in VizLabelBody\n    set attribute `href` of VizLabelText to `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,` cat VizLabelBody\n    return\n\nVizLabelCheck:\n    ! **Is this line a label, and how long is its name?** A label is a whole line at the left margin whose\n    ! last character is a colon — the language has no other shape for one, the pane is given the script as\n    ! *text* and nothing else, and the recording carries no label names (its anchors are the `viz` markers,\n    ! named `viz-start@206` and the like). So this is where a label is recognised.\n    !\n    ! **One routine because two things ask.** The rules stop short of a label so that the name at the\n    ! frame's edge reads as a signpost rather than as the end of the line, and the names themselves are\n    ! drawn from the same answer — so the whole meaning of \"a label\" is here, and there is no second copy\n    ! of it to drift. A doc block is blanked by both callers before they come here, which takes care of the\n    ! `!!` lines; a bare `!` comment is excluded by hand because a comment can end in a colon too. What is\n    ! left is a label in every case, and the cost of a false positive is a stray name at the frame's edge.\n    !\n    ! `VizSourceLine` holds the line on the way in; `VizIsLabel` is 0 or 1 on the way out and `VizLabelLen`\n    ! is the length of the name — the line without its colon.\n    put 0 into VizIsLabel\n    put 0 into VizLabelLen\n    if VizSourceLine is `` return\n    if VizSourceLine ends with `:` go to VizLabelShape\n    return\nVizLabelShape:\n    if VizSourceLine starts with ` ` return\n    if VizSourceLine starts with `!` return\n    put 1 into VizIsLabel\n    put the length of VizSourceLine into VizLabelLen\n    take 1 from VizLabelLen giving VizLabelLen\n    return\n\nVizSourcePicture:\n!! @hash 2d741f7c\n!! @verified 2d741f7c\n!!!\n!! The picture never changes; the window over it does.\n!!\n!! `VizPane` is a nested `<svg>` the size of the frame, and its `viewBox` is the whole of the zoom and the pan — one attribute per draw, and the picture itself is drawn once when the run is opened. That is what keeps the text the shape it was written in: a window whose aspect followed the zoom instead is what stretched the letters as you zoomed in. The pane's size being the frame is also the clipping, which is the one thing a picture cannot do for itself — an `<image>` either scales its content to its own box or is clipped by the outer viewport.\n    ! ---- the window on the picture ----\n    ! The pane's `viewBox`, which is the whole of the zoom and the pan. The three values are the draw's\n    ! own, computed at its top — see `VizWindow` for why they are not worked out here, where they are\n    ! wanted. In the document's units its aspect is the frame's, which is what gives the text the\n    ! proportions it was written at (a window whose shape followed the zoom is what stretched the\n    ! letters), and its height is one row per line in view, so a row lands on the line the axis names.\n    !\n    ! **Its `x` is always zero, so the source stays anchored to the left edge of the pane.** A line\n    ! longer than the window shows goes off the right-hand edge — and there it stays, because the\n    ! left-hand end of a line is the part that says what the line is. That offset used to be derived\n    ! from the horizontal window, so panning along the run dragged the source sideways and took the\n    ! indentation and the first word out of view with it. The tail of a line wider than the frame is\n    ! what that costs, and the frame is not narrow: it shows about a hundred and ten columns at the\n    ! legible floor, which is more than most lines and less than a few.\n    set attribute `viewBox` of VizPane to `0 ` cat VizWindowY cat ` ` cat VizWindowW cat ` ` cat VizWindowH\n    ! **The redaction's width is pinned here, and one attribute is the whole of it.** Its bars are stored at\n    ! the width the text would be at its own size, so that a bar is as wide as its line *is* when a reader can\n    ! read it; the document is then shrunk to fit whatever is in view, and without this it would shrink the\n    ! bars with it. The `transform` widens them by exactly what the `viewBox` above narrows the document by,\n    ! so the two cancel and a bar occupies the same width at every zoom — which is the shape Graham asked to\n    ! be kept: *\"so that at all zoom levels the bars still occupy the same width\"*. Their **height** is left\n    ! alone, so it still follows the row, and that is the \"zooms with it, but only in Y\".\n    !\n    ! **And it is why there is nothing to defer.** The obvious way to keep a width while the zoom changes is\n    ! to rebuild the bars at each zoom, which is the load Graham expected; a `transform` says the same thing\n    ! in one number, so a notch costs one attribute and no string at all. The path itself is still built once\n    ! per run, with the text.\n    !\n    ! The factor is thousandths, written as a decimal by string arithmetic, because the language has integers\n    ! and an SVG number wants a point. **It is never allowed below one, and that is where the reference comes\n    ! from**: at the deepest zoom the document is drawn at its own size, the factor is one, and the bars are\n    ! exactly as wide as the text they redact — Graham's \"the length of its text at maximum zoom\". Every other\n    ! zoom is within a tenth of a per cent of that, which is under a unit on a two-hundred-unit bar, and the\n    ! harness pins the width across forty-seven phases at a 0.75% spread.\n    multiply VizWindowH by 1000 giving VizRedactScale\n    divide VizRedactScale by VizPlotH\n    if VizRedactScale is less than 1000 put 1000 into VizRedactScale\n    ! Through a string, because `the length of` a *number* is undefined in this runtime — the same trap the\n    ! source's measure dodges by counting characters rather than digits.\n    !\n    ! **Built in steps rather than one expression, and that is not a style choice.**\n    ! `left 1 of X cat '.' cat right 3 of X` reads as the three parts it looks like, and the runtime reads it as\n    ! `left 1 of (X cat '.' cat right 3 of X)`: `cat`'s right-hand side is the whole rest of the expression,\n    ! so the first part takes the leading digit of everything that follows. It compiles, it runs, and the\n    ! factor came out as `8` where `8.068` was meant — which the harness caught as a width that still moved\n    ! with the zoom, and is exactly the kind of fault a check earns its keep on.\n    put `` cat VizRedactScale into VizRedactText\n    put the length of VizRedactText into VizRedactLength\n    take 3 from VizRedactLength\n    put left VizRedactLength of VizRedactText into VizRedactFactor\n    put VizRedactFactor cat `.` into VizRedactFactor\n    put right 3 of VizRedactText into VizRedactText\n    put VizRedactFactor cat VizRedactText into VizRedactFactor\n    set attribute `transform` of VizRedact to `scale(` cat VizRedactFactor cat ` 1)`\n    set attribute `href` of VizGlyphs to `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"` cat VizGlyphWidth cat `\" height=\"` cat VizGlyphHeight cat `\">` cat VizGlyphBody cat `</svg>`\n    return\n!! @hash 78dcffaf\n!! @verified 78dcffaf\n!!!\n!! The mappings: the one place a line's y and a step's x are worked out.\n!!\n!! `VizPlaceY` turns a line number into the y of the row it occupies, and `VizPlaceX` turns a step into the x of its column. Every mark, every rule and every axis label goes through one of them, which is why they are here together and why the other code calls them rather than doing the arithmetic: when the axis and the marks disagreed about where a line was, the fault was one of these two and the picture said so at a glance.\n    ! ---- the mappings ----\nVizPlaceY:\n    ! Where a line number lands in the frame. **This is the one place that mapping lives**, because the\n    ! marks, the rules and the axis labels all have to agree about it — and they did not: the mapping\n    ! was written out at each site, and every one of them put a line at the *top* of the row of text it\n    ! names.\n    !\n    ! **A row is eighteen document units tall and its glyphs are centred in it, so the line's own y is\n    ! half a row lower.** With the mapping at the row's top, every rule and every dot sat just under the\n    ! text of the line *above* the one it belonged to — which reads as a vertical misalignment and makes\n    ! a rule that begins one em past its line's code look like it begins in the middle of the line above.\n    ! Graham found it by eye and measurement confirmed it: of seventeen rules in a screenshot, every one\n    ! sat between a fifth and a half of a row above its own glyphs.\n    !\n    ! The half row is added *before* the division, which is half of `VizPlotH` and exactly half a row\n    ! once the divisor is applied — the same scaled-integer pattern the marks use, and no fraction to\n    ! lose.\n    take VizViewY0 from VizTickValue giving VizTickPos\n    multiply VizTickPos by VizPlotH\n    add VizRowHalf to VizTickPos\n    divide VizTickPos by VizViewYH\n    add VizMargin to VizTickPos\n    return\n\nVizPlaceX:\n    take VizViewX0 from VizTickValue giving VizTickPos\n    multiply VizTickPos by VizPlotW\n    divide VizTickPos by VizViewXW\n    add VizMargin to VizTickPos\n    return\n!! @hash 3de4d772\n!! @verified 3de4d772\n!!!\n\n!! Keep the picture to a range of steps, and give the whole recording back.\n!!\n!! A recording of a big run is slow to draw and, past a certain size, only useful as an overview: the reader wants one region of it, and everything outside that region is what costs the time. The clip is that, after the fact — nothing is rewritten, so the recording stays the run's own evidence and the same file can be read whole again.\n!!\n!! **The window is the selector.** The two numbers are taken from `VizViewX0` and its width, which are in the trace's own steps — the unit an IN/OUT bar would mark — so the range is chosen with gestures already in use: shift-wheel zooms the lines, control-wheel the steps, and a drag pans. There is nothing else to learn and no second control to find.\n!!\n!! **Nothing is stored but the range.** The recording is re-read from `VizTrace` on every draw, so `VizUnclip` is two assignments and the whole of the run is back on the next draw. That also means a clip does not survive leaving the pane — the same deal the window itself makes, and the honest one: a recording is a file, and a range over it is a way of looking, not a property of the run.\n!!\n!! **And the controls are two boxes above the bar.** A pane element cannot take a click — `svg` is the one element type the plugin registers without the `dom` extra — so a button cannot live inside the pane, and the pane's own idiom, an arithmetic hit test on the pointer, is what every one of its gestures already uses. For this job the bar is the better control for a reason that is measured rather than argued: **the zoom is the expensive gesture on a big recording**, each notch a whole drawing, so marking a range costs one drawing where reaching the same range by wheel and pan takes five or six.\n!!\n!! **A drag only selects; a press is what commits.** The two markers are the reader's, and moving one costs no drawing at all — but letting go applies nothing, which is what lets a range be adjusted as many times as it takes without paying for a cut each time. `<> keep` makes the window between the markers the run, and `reset` gives the whole recording back. Each is a box drawn from the same numbers the hit test matches, so the press lands on the rectangle that was drawn rather than on a coordinate written somewhere else.\n!!\n!! **The bar's axis is the working set's own extent**, so a cut brings both markers home to the ends of what is now on screen — the picture above them is a new total range, and the bar says where the selection sits within it. The guides and the washes are placed in the *picture's* mapping instead, so they are drawn at any zoom and with a clip on, which is what a reader pointing at the picture is looking with. `VizClip` — the window as the range — remains, and puts the markers on the window's ends so that a range chosen with the wheel shows on the bar too.\n    ! ---- the clip ----\nVizClip:\n    ! **Keep the window** — the host's own way in, and the same operation as the `in` control. The range is\n    ! the window exactly as it stands: `VizViewX0` is the first step in view and the window's width is how\n    ! many of them there are, so the last one is one short of their sum, the convention the status line\n    ! prints. The selection is moved onto it too, so the markers show a range chosen with the wheel and\n    ! pressing `keep` straight afterwards is a no-op rather than a cut to somewhere else.\n    put VizViewX0 into VizSelFrom\n    put VizViewX0 into VizSelTo\n    add VizViewXW to VizSelTo\n    take 1 from VizSelTo\n    put 1 into VizKeepTail\n    gosub to VizCut\n    return\n\nVizUnclip:\n    ! **The whole recording back** — `reset`, and the only operation that widens. Nothing is un-done\n    ! record by record, because nothing was ever rewritten: the empty list means no narrowing, and the next\n    ! draw's filter keeps every record again.\n    put 0 into VizKeepTail\n    gosub to VizCut\n    return\n\nVizCut:\n    ! **`add 0 to` the four numbers before any arithmetic on them.** A step read out of a parsed recording is\n    ! *text*, and text compares lexically — an unconverted `10` is less than `9` — so the ranges this makes\n    ! would cut the wrong records: measured in isolation before the filter was written, `10 is less than 9`\n    ! is 1 without the conversion and 0 with it. This is where the selection, the remembered recording end\n    ! and the range arithmetic all meet, so it is where the conversion belongs.\n    add 0 to VizSelFrom\n    add 0 to VizSelTo\n    add 0 to VizAllTo\n    ! **The one operation the controls share: rewrite the list to what the reader chose to keep.**\n    ! `VizKeepTail` says which part of the window that is — 0 the whole recording, 1 the window itself,\n    ! 2 the two parts outside it — and the selection (`VizSelFrom`/`VizSelTo`) is the window the markers\n    ! make. Keeping the inside intersects each live range with the window; keeping the outside subtracts\n    ! it, and a subtraction can split one range into two, which is why the list is a list and why the new\n    ! one is built in the spare pair before it replaces the live one.\n    !\n    ! **Nothing is rewritten but the list.** The recording is re-read on every draw, so a cut is a handful\n    ! of numbers and the next draw's filter, and `reset` is the empty list. That also means a cut does not\n    ! survive leaving the pane — the same deal the window itself makes, and the honest one: a recording is\n    ! a file, and a set of ranges over it is a way of looking, not a property of the run.\n    if VizKeepTail is 0 go to VizCutWhole\n    ! The list the cut replaces, so `undo` can put it back, and the tag, which is what makes a cut\n    ! *visible* to the two places that decide a picture.\n    put VizKeepTail into VizKeepSet\n    gosub to VizHistPush\n    put 0 into VizKeepN2\n    put 0 into VizKeepI\n    ! With nothing kept yet, the window *is* the answer — no range to intersect.\n    if VizKeepN is 0 and VizKeepTail is 1\n    begin\n        put VizSelFrom into VizRangeFrom\n        put VizSelTo into VizRangeTo\n        gosub to VizCutAdd\n    end\n    ! And with nothing kept yet, the outside is the two ends of the recording. `0` is where the fit starts,\n    ! and `VizAllTo` is the recording's own last step, remembered in pass one while the list is still empty.\n    if VizKeepN is 0 and VizKeepTail is 2\n    begin\n        put 0 into VizRangeFrom\n        take 1 from VizSelFrom giving VizRangeTo\n        if VizRangeTo is not less than 0 gosub to VizCutAdd\n        add 1 to VizSelTo giving VizRangeFrom\n        put VizAllTo into VizRangeTo\n        if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd\n    end\n    ! Otherwise every live range is rewritten.\n    while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN\n    begin\n        index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI\n        put VizKeepFrom into VizKeepA\n        index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI\n        put VizKeepTo into VizKeepB\n        if VizKeepTail is 1\n        begin\n            if VizSelFrom is greater than VizKeepA put VizSelFrom into VizKeepA\n            if VizSelTo is less than VizKeepB put VizSelTo into VizKeepB\n            if VizKeepA is not greater than VizKeepB\n            begin\n                put VizKeepA into VizRangeFrom\n                put VizKeepB into VizRangeTo\n                gosub to VizCutAdd\n            end\n        end\n        if VizKeepTail is 2\n        begin\n            ! What is left of this range on each side of the window.\n            if VizKeepA is less than VizSelFrom\n            begin\n                put VizKeepA into VizRangeFrom\n                put VizSelFrom into VizRangeTo\n                take 1 from VizRangeTo\n                if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd\n            end\n            if VizKeepB is greater than VizSelTo\n            begin\n                put VizSelTo into VizRangeFrom\n                add 1 to VizRangeFrom\n                put VizKeepB into VizRangeTo\n                if VizRangeFrom is not greater than VizRangeTo gosub to VizCutAdd\n            end\n        end\n        add 1 to VizKeepI\n    end\n    ! **A cut that would keep nothing is refused, and the reader is told why.** Subtracting a selection from\n    ! a list it already covers leaves nothing, and an empty list means \"the whole recording\" — so the pane\n    ! would *widen* at a press, which is the one thing this control must never do. The history entry the\n    ! cut had already saved goes back, and the notice says what happened rather than leaving a reader to\n    ! work out which button was ignored. It is the notice and not the status line for the reason the\n    ! design gives: an explanation of a control belongs where the controls are.\n    if VizKeepN2 is 0\n    begin\n        take 1 from VizHistN\n        put VizHistN into VizHistAt\n        set the text of VizClipNotice to `nothing left to keep — the whole recording stands`\n        return\n    end\n    gosub to VizCutSwap\n    add 1 to VizSetTag\n    gosub to VizRequest\n    return\n\nVizCutWhole:\n    ! The whole recording, which is the empty list — both the starting state and what `reset` restores.\n    put 0 into VizKeepN\n    put 0 into VizKeepSet\n    add 1 to VizSetTag\n    gosub to VizRequest\n    return\n\nVizCutAdd:\n    ! One range into the list being built. Ranges go in in order — a live list is ordered and the two\n    ! halves of a split are added left first — so the built list is ordered too, which is what lets the\n    ! axis take its ends from the first and last entries and lets the filter's walk stay a walk.\n    !\n    ! **`VizKeepCap` is a ceiling and not a promise.** At it, a cut that would split a range further\n    ! simply keeps less of it than it should — sixteen outer cuts, on one recording, without resetting.\n    ! Said rather than hidden: an overrun here would be a wrong picture with no sign that anything was\n    ! dropped.\n    if VizKeepN2 is not less than VizKeepCap return\n    index VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepN2\n    put VizRangeFrom into VizKeepFrom2\n    index VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepN2\n    put VizRangeTo into VizKeepTo2\n    add 1 to VizKeepN2\n    return\n\nVizCutSwap:\n    ! The built list becomes the live one. A loop rather than a swap, because a swap would need a third\n    ! pair of holders, and either way this is a handful of numbers.\n    put VizKeepN2 into VizKeepN\n    put 0 into VizKeepI\n    while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN\n    begin\n        index VizKeepFrom2 to VizKeepI\n        put VizKeepFrom2 into VizRangeFrom\n        index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI\n        put VizRangeFrom into VizKeepFrom\n        index VizKeepTo2 to VizKeepI\n        put VizKeepTo2 into VizRangeTo\n        index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI\n        put VizRangeTo into VizKeepTo\n        add 1 to VizKeepI\n    end\n    return\n\nVizHistPush:\n    ! **The list a cut is about to replace, as one string** — `0-20`, or `0-4;12-20`. The whole history in\n    ! a handful of bytes is what makes an undo worth having: a saved *set* would be a copy of the\n    ! recording, while this is a few numbers however big the run is, so keeping thirty of them costs\n    ! nothing. An empty list is the recording unreduced, which is what undoing past the first cut restores.\n    put `` into VizHistText\n    put 0 into VizHistI\n    while VizHistI is less than VizKeepN\n    begin\n        if VizHistText is not empty put VizHistText cat `;` into VizHistText\n        index VizKeepFrom to VizHistI\n        put VizKeepFrom into VizHistPiece\n        put VizHistText cat VizHistPiece into VizHistText\n        put VizHistText cat `-` into VizHistText\n        index VizKeepTo to VizHistI\n        put VizKeepTo into VizHistPiece\n        put VizHistText cat VizHistPiece into VizHistText\n        add 1 to VizHistI\n    end\n    index VizHist to VizHistN\n    put VizHistText into VizHist\n    add 1 to VizHistN\n    put VizHistN into VizHistAt\n    return\n\nVizHistLoad:\n    ! The text back into the list. Empty text is the recording unreduced — undo, past the first cut, is the\n    ! whole run again. `split` fills a slot per piece, so the walk is `index` and `has element`, the same\n    ! shape the editor's own buffer walk uses.\n    put 0 into VizKeepN2\n    if VizHistText is empty go to VizCutSwap\n    split VizHistText by `;` into VizHistParts\n    put 0 into VizHistI\n    while VizHistParts has element VizHistI\n    begin\n        index VizHistParts to VizHistI\n        put VizHistParts into VizHistPiece\n        split VizHistPiece by `-` into VizHistPair\n        index VizHistPair to 0\n        put VizHistPair into VizRangeFrom\n        index VizHistPair to 1\n        put VizHistPair into VizRangeTo\n        ! **And back to numbers.** A list read out of the history is *text*, and the next cut's arithmetic on\n        ! it would compare lexically — `10 is less than 9` — so an undo followed by a cut would keep the\n        ! wrong records. The same conversion the filter makes per record, made here for the same reason.\n        add 0 to VizRangeFrom\n        add 0 to VizRangeTo\n        gosub to VizCutAdd\n        add 1 to VizHistI\n    end\n    go to VizCutSwap\n\nVizUndo:\n    ! **Back to the list the last cut replaced**, and it is instant because it is a parse and a draw rather\n    ! than a re-filter of the recording: the tag is bumped so the next draw rebuilds the set from the\n    ! restored list, which is the same path a cut takes and is why an undo cannot drift from cutting.\n    if VizHistAt is less than 1 return\n    take 1 from VizHistAt\n    index VizHist to VizHistAt\n    put VizHist into VizHistText\n    gosub to VizHistLoad\n    add 1 to VizSetTag\n    gosub to VizRequest\n    return\n\nVizKeepRun:\n    ! **Keep the records the list covers, and count what was kept and seen.** Called from `Draw` before\n    ! anything reads the events, so what it leaves in `VizEvents` is what the whole draw — the extent, the\n    ! rule set, the busiest line over the ramp and the hit test's `visit N of T` — is built from. That is\n    ! what makes the notice a fact rather than a label.\n    !\n    ! **An empty list means no narrowing**, which is what makes `reset` a state rather than an operation:\n    ! there is nothing to remember about the whole recording, and every record is kept.\n    !\n    ! **`add 0 to` before a comparison is not tidiness.** A number that arrived as text compares *lexically*,\n    ! so an unconverted `10` is less than `9` and the filter would keep the wrong records — measured in\n    ! isolation before this was written: `10 is less than 9` is 1 without the conversion and 0 with it. A\n    ! step comes out of a parsed recording, so it is text until something makes it a number.\n    !\n    ! Only `anchor` and `transfer` records are counted or kept. They are the two kinds the picture is made\n    ! of; the metadata entries in a Chrome trace name the process and the thread and are read by nothing\n    ! here. The `window` records are deliberately dropped with the rest: their `steps` is how long a window\n    ! ran rather than a step anything is *at*, so keeping one would stretch the extent back out past the\n    ! range the reader chose.\n    json set VizKept to array\n    put 0 into VizClipTotal\n    put 0 into VizKeepCount\n    put 0 into VizIndex\n    while VizEvents has element VizIndex\n    begin\n        put element VizIndex of VizEvents into VizEvent\n        put property `cat` of VizEvent into VizCategory\n        put 0 into VizClipIs\n        if VizCategory is `anchor` put 1 into VizClipIs\n        if VizCategory is `transfer` put 1 into VizClipIs\n        if VizClipIs is 1\n        begin\n            add 1 to VizClipTotal\n            put property `args` of VizEvent into VizArgs\n            put property `steps` of VizArgs into VizSteps\n            add 0 to VizSteps\n            put 0 into VizKeepKept\n            if VizKeepN is 0 put 1 into VizKeepKept\n            put 0 into VizKeepI\n            while VizKeepI is less than VizKeepN\n            begin\n                index VizKeepFrom to VizKeepI\n                put VizKeepFrom into VizKeepA\n                index VizKeepTo to VizKeepI\n                put VizKeepTo into VizKeepB\n                if VizSteps is not less than VizKeepA and VizSteps is not greater than VizKeepB put 1 into VizKeepKept\n                add 1 to VizKeepI\n            end\n            if VizKeepKept is 1\n            begin\n                json add VizEvent to VizKept\n                ! The picture's own first step is the first *kept* record's, not the list's first range's:\n                ! a range can hold no records at all, and a picture that began in the blank between two\n                ! ranges would move the axis and the bars for nothing.\n                if VizKeepCount is 0 and VizKeepN is not 0 put VizSteps into VizMinSteps\n                add 1 to VizKeepCount\n            end\n        end\n        add 1 to VizIndex\n    end\n    put VizKeepCount into VizClipKept\n    put VizKept into VizEvents\n    ! **And the tag it was built for, so that only a *change* of list rebuilds it.** The walk this makes\n    ! is the expensive one — it reads every record, and reading a record costs about 15ms on a 435KB\n    ! recording, because the runtime re-parses the whole array per access — so it is a cost to pay once\n    ! when the reader cuts and never again while that list stands.\n    put VizSetTag into VizKeptKey\n    return\n\nVizClipBar:\n    ! **Where the two markers stand, and the band between them.** Read from the clip's ends, or from\n    ! the recording's ends when there is no clip, so clearing a clip puts them back without a second\n    ! place knowing how. The band is the range drawn as a shape, which is the reading a reader wants\n    ! off the bar: not *where* each end is, but how much of the run is in.\n    !\n    ! The markers are drawn centred on the step they name — they are four units wide — and both are\n    ! clamped to the bar, because the floor `VizClamp` puts under a window can push the window's own\n    ! range a step outside a very short run.\n    ! The markers are the **selection**, which is the reader's and not the clip's. It goes home to the\n    ! ends of the working set whenever the applied range changes — see the axis in pass one — so a cut\n    ! brings both markers in to the ends of what is now the run, which is what the picture above them has\n    ! just become.\n    !\n    ! Both are clamped to the bar, because the floor `VizClamp` puts under a window can push the window's\n    ! own range a step outside a very short run.\n    put VizSelFrom into VizClipStep\n    gosub to VizClipToPos\n    if VizClipPos is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizClipPos\n    if VizClipPos is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizClipPos\n    put VizClipPos into VizClipInX\n    put VizSelTo into VizClipStep\n    gosub to VizClipToPos\n    if VizClipPos is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizClipPos\n    if VizClipPos is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizClipPos\n    put VizClipPos into VizClipOutX\n    gosub to VizClipEdge\n    return\n\nVizClipEdge:\n    ! **Everything the chosen range looks like, from the two markers' places.** One place, called by the\n    ! draw (so the bar and its guides stand where the clip is) and by the drag (so a reader moving a marker\n    ! sees them follow without a drawing). Two copies of this would be two answers to \"where is the range\",\n    ! and the drag's would be the one nobody checked.\n    !\n    ! reads `VizClipInX`/`VizClipOutX`, the two step positions in the frame's own x.\n    !\n    ! **The markers and the band first, in the bar's own units.** They are the bar's furniture and belong\n    ! to the axis it is drawn on; the guides and the washes below belong to the picture and are placed\n    ! with its mapping.\n    take 2 from VizClipInX giving VizMove\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipMarkIn to VizMove\n    take 2 from VizClipOutX giving VizMove\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipMarkOut to VizMove\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipBand to VizClipInX\n    take VizClipInX from VizClipOutX giving VizMove\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipBand to VizMove\n    ! **The guides and the washes are placed in the *picture's* mapping, not the bar's.** They say where\n    ! the selection falls in the plot, so the plot's own step-to-x is the only mapping that can be right —\n    ! and it is right at any zoom: zoom past an end and that end's guide leaves the frame, which is what a\n    ! reader expects, rather than the pair standing aside as they used to. `VizPlaceX` is that mapping,\n    ! the same one the marks and the rules use, so a guide cannot disagree with a line.\n    put VizSelFrom into VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    put VizTickPos into VizClipInXW\n    put VizSelTo into VizTickValue\n    gosub to VizPlaceX\n    put VizTickPos into VizClipOutXW\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to VizClipInXW\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to VizClipOutXW\n    ! An end the window has gone past is off the plot, and a plumb line drawn there would be a mark at the\n    ! wall — so it is parked outside the frame, where it draws nothing.\n    if VizClipInXW is less than VizMargin set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to -10\n    if VizClipInXW is greater than VizPlotRight set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideIn to -10\n    if VizClipOutXW is less than VizMargin set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to -10\n    if VizClipOutXW is greater than VizPlotRight set attribute `x` of VizClipGuideOut to -10\n    ! And the washes: what a `keep` would take away, on each side of the selection, clamped to the plot so\n    ! that neither width can come out negative — a negative width is not a wide wash, it is an error.\n    put VizClipInXW into VizMove\n    if VizMove is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizMove\n    if VizMove is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizMove\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipMaskIn to VizMargin\n    take VizMargin from VizMove giving VizClipWash\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipMaskIn to VizClipWash\n    put VizClipOutXW into VizMove\n    if VizMove is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizMove\n    if VizMove is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizMove\n    set attribute `x` of VizClipMaskOut to VizMove\n    take VizMove from VizPlotRight giving VizClipWash\n    set attribute `width` of VizClipMaskOut to VizClipWash\n    ! And the notice, in the recording's own numbers. The counts come from the filter, so they are only\n    ! known once a cut has been applied — which is why a window being dragged is named without them, in\n    ! `VizClipMove`. **What the last cut kept is said in words**, because that is the one thing a reader\n    ! cannot read off the picture: `the window` and `the outside` are different sets of records, and the\n    ! marks alone do not say which was asked for.\n    set the text of VizClipNotice to ``\n    if VizKeepN is 0 return\n    set the text of VizClipNotice to `kept the window: ` cat VizClipKept cat ` of ` cat VizClipTotal cat ` records`\n    if VizKeepSet is 2 set the text of VizClipNotice to `kept the outside: ` cat VizClipKept cat ` of ` cat VizClipTotal cat ` records`\n    return\n\nVizClipToPos:\n    ! **One step, in the frame's own x** — the clip bar's half of the mapping the axis, the marks and\n    ! the rules all share. The scaled-integer shape is the file's everywhere: the span is multiplied\n    ! out before it is divided, because the runtime truncates every division and a proportion of a big\n    ! run rounds to nothing the other way round.\n    !\n    ! reads `VizClipStep`, leaves `VizClipPos`, and `VizClipSpan` beside them because the inverse needs\n    ! the same width.\n    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipSpan\n    if VizClipSpan is less than 1 put 1 into VizClipSpan\n    take VizRunFrom from VizClipStep giving VizClipPos\n    multiply VizClipPos by VizPlotW\n    divide VizClipPos by VizClipSpan\n    add VizMargin to VizClipPos\n    return\n\nVizClipToStep:\n    ! And the inverse, for a marker being dragged: an x read as a step. It truncates, so a round trip\n    ! can land a step low — which is why `VizClipMove` snaps a marker at the end of the bar rather than\n    ! trusting the arithmetic not to leave it a step short.\n    !\n    ! reads `VizClipPos`, leaves `VizClipStep`.\n    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipSpan\n    if VizClipSpan is less than 1 put 1 into VizClipSpan\n    take VizMargin from VizClipPos giving VizClipStep\n    multiply VizClipStep by VizClipSpan\n    divide VizClipStep by VizPlotW\n    add VizRunFrom to VizClipStep\n    return\n\nVizClipMove:\n    ! **A marker being dragged, and the picture deliberately not redrawn.** The marker and the band are\n    ! attributes, so moving them costs nothing; the clip is applied by `VizRelease` on the drop. See the\n    ! section's own note for why that ordering is the whole point of the bar rather than an omission.\n    !\n    ! The partner's step is read *first*, because both markers have to be interpreted the same way\n    ! whether or not a clip is already on — the other end is a drawn marker either way.\n    if VizGrabbed is 0 return\n    ! The partner's step, straight from the selection rather than read back from where it is drawn: both\n    ! ends are ours, and reading one back through the mapping would be a second copy of the arithmetic the\n    ! snap below already does.\n    put VizSelFrom into VizClipOther\n    if VizSlide is 3 put VizSelTo into VizClipOther\n    ! The dragged marker's own x, from the pointer: the grab was measured in the same units and the\n    ! delta scaled, which is the arithmetic the scrollbar handle drag uses and for the same reason —\n    ! a position accumulated event by event drifts, because every division throws its remainder away.\n    put the drag position into VizDragPos\n    put property `x` of VizDragPos into VizDragX\n    take VizGrabX from VizDragX giving VizDy\n    gosub to VizPixelScale\n    multiply VizDy by 1000\n    divide VizDy by VizPixelsK\n    add VizGrabMarker to VizDy\n    if VizDy is less than VizMargin put VizMargin into VizDy\n    if VizDy is greater than VizPlotRight put VizPlotRight into VizDy\n    put VizDy into VizClipPos\n    gosub to VizClipToStep\n    ! **Inside the recording, and not onto its partner** — and the floor between them has to yield to\n    ! the run. On a three-step recording a four-step floor fights the run's own bounds, and the two\n    ! clamps then disagreed and the later one lost: that is how a marker was drawn past the end of the\n    ! bar the first time this was tried. The run's bounds are therefore applied *last*, after the floor.\n    take VizRunFrom from VizRunTo giving VizClipMin\n    if VizClipMin is greater than 4 put 4 into VizClipMin\n    if VizSlide is 3\n    begin\n        take VizClipMin from VizClipOther giving VizClipPos\n        if VizClipStep is greater than VizClipPos put VizClipPos into VizClipStep\n    end\n    if VizSlide is 4\n    begin\n        add VizClipMin to VizClipOther giving VizClipPos\n        if VizClipStep is less than VizClipPos put VizClipPos into VizClipStep\n    end\n    if VizClipStep is less than VizRunFrom put VizRunFrom into VizClipStep\n    if VizClipStep is greater than VizRunTo put VizRunTo into VizClipStep\n    ! **And a snap at the ends, within one floor's width.** A pointer cannot land exactly on the last step:\n    ! the drag's x round-trips through pixels and truncates, so a marker dragged hard against the end\n    ! stopped a step or two short, and a selection that was not quite the run.\n    ! so \"drag it home to clear the clip\" did not work, measured on the 441-step trace while it happened\n    ! to work on the 24-step one. Every scrollbar snaps at its ends for the same reason: the end is a place\n    ! the reader means to reach, and it should not be a place they have to hit exactly.\n    take VizClipMin from VizRunTo giving VizClipPos\n    if VizClipStep is greater than VizClipPos put VizRunTo into VizClipStep\n    add VizClipMin to VizRunFrom giving VizClipPos\n    if VizClipStep is less than VizClipPos put VizRunFrom into VizClipStep\n    if VizSlide is 3 put VizClipStep into VizSelFrom\n    if VizSlide is 4 put VizClipStep into VizSelTo\n    gosub to VizClipToPos\n    if VizSlide is 3 put VizClipPos into VizClipInX\n    if VizSlide is 4 put VizClipPos into VizClipOutX\n    ! One call draws the whole appearance, the same one a drawing uses — see `VizClipEdge`.\n    gosub to VizClipEdge\n    ! **And the window being selected, named as it moves.** It says *select*, not *clip*, because that is\n    ! what a drag now does: the counts belong to a clip some control has applied, and these words are the\n    ! reader's only feedback while dragging. `VizClipEdge` has just cleared the notice, which is why this\n    ! comes after it.\n    if VizSlide is 3 set the text of VizClipNotice to `select steps ` cat VizClipStep cat `-` cat VizClipOther\n    if VizSlide is 4 set the text of VizClipNotice to `select steps ` cat VizClipOther cat `-` cat VizClipStep\n    return\n\n!! @hash 7b5d51ae\n!!!\n","asedit-side.allspeak":"!! asedit-side.allspeak — the editor's Graph sidebar, as a companion module.\n!!\n!! The sidebar is the reading aid beside the picture: a tabbed panel down the right-hand edge of the Graph pane, whose first tab shows the doc block that the line under a clicked mark belongs to. It is a module for the same reason the drawing is — the editor needs none of it until somebody opens a run — and it is a *separate* module from the drawing for a reason worth stating: the two are about different things, a picture and a reading, they attach to different elements, and either can grow without the other's file growing with it. Each is given its own box by `asedit.json`, so neither lays the other out.\n!!\n!! What it owns, and what it does not. Everything inside its panel: the tab strip, the body, the heading, the prose and the hint that stands in for them before anything is clicked. **It does not find the block.** Which doc block holds a line is a question about the script's structure, and the editor already answers it for Blocks mode; a second answer here would be a second parser to keep in step. So the editor sends the block — its heading and its prose — and this module is a pane. That is also why nothing is read from disk here: the run, the path, and every block all arrive by message.\n!!\n!! What crosses. One dict in, and it is one of two kinds, so the message says which: a `run` carries the script's path and whether the pane is still on screen, and a `block` carries the line that was clicked, whether any block holds it, and the heading and prose to show if one does. Nothing goes back — this module has no gestures and nothing to report — and it never needs to know that a picture exists.\n!   -- the module, and the panel it owns --\n\n    script ASEditorSide\n!! @hash 62b44485\n!! @verified 62b44485\n!!!\n!! What the module holds from the editor's page, and the state the boundary is kept in.\n!!\n!! The panel is attached **by id** — `asedit.json` names it, the same way the editor names every element it owns — so the module has a handle on the page without the editor creating anything for it. Everything the panel is made of is built inside that one element, so the page names one box for the sidebar and no more.\n!   -- the page, by id --\n    div SideHost\n!   -- the panel's own elements, built once --\n    div SideTabs            ! the strip along the top\n    div SideTab             ! a pool: one element per tab, created at its index\n    div SideBody            ! everything under the strip\n    span SideHead           ! what the body is showing, as a line\n    div SideProse           ! and the prose itself, in the reader's serif\n    div SideHint            ! what stands in for both before a mark is clicked\n    div SideStatus          ! the strip along the foot: what the last clicked mark was\n    variable SideBuilt      ! 1 once the elements exist, because a message can arrive twice\n!   -- the tabs --\n    variable SideTabName    ! array: the label on each tab\n    variable SideTabCount   ! how many there are\n    variable SideTabSel     ! which is up\n    variable SideTabI       ! scratch: index over them\n    variable SideTabLabel   ! scratch: one tab's label, for the tooltip and the row\n!   -- the run, and the block shown for a click --\n    variable SideRun        ! the dict the editor sent, as it arrived\n    variable SideKind       ! `run` or `block`\n    variable SidePath       ! the script or its recording, named for the reader\n    variable SideLine       ! the line the clicked mark named\n    variable SideVisit      ! which arrival on that line the clicked mark was\n    variable SideStatusText ! scratch: the status line, assembled before it is put up\n    variable SideValues     ! what the clicked arrival captured: the names the script asked to watch\n    variable SideValueKeys  ! scratch: those names, in the script's own order\n    variable SideValueText  ! scratch: them and their values, as one run of text\n    variable SideValueI     ! scratch: the walk over them\n    variable SideValueName  ! scratch: one of those names\n    variable SideFound      ! 1 if a doc block holds that line\n    variable SideTitle      ! the block's heading, numbered as Blocks mode numbers it\n    variable SideProseText  ! its prose\n    variable SideTmp        ! general scratch\n\n    attach SideHost to `se-graph-side`\n    on message go to SideMessage\n    release parent\n    stop\n!! @hash 0dfeb1aa\n!! @verified b6b4b753\n!!!\n!! Building the panel, once, and the tabs as the one pool of elements they are.\n!!\n!! **An AllSpeak element is a declared symbol, so a tab is a symbol rather than something grown at run time** — the same constraint the drawing's marks live under. The pool is `SideTab`, sized here to the number of tabs and created one element at a time at its own index, which is the shape Blocks mode's table of contents already uses; **so a tab is a name in `SideTabName` and a branch in `SideRender`**, and adding the next one is those two things and nothing else.\n!!\n!! The strip is built before the body so that it sits above it, and the three body elements are created once and emptied rather than replaced: a panel that rebuilt its own children on every click would flicker, and a hidden panel that keeps them is the cheaper thing to show again.\nSideBuild:\n    if SideBuilt is 1 return\n    put 1 into SideBuilt\n\n    create SideTabs in SideHost\n    set the style of SideTabs to `display:flex;gap:2px;padding:4px 8px 0 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;flex-shrink:0`\n\n    ! **The body holds no padding of its own, because the two text components carry their own margins.**\n    ! That is Graham's arrangement and it is the better one: the synopsis and the body are two things a\n    ! reader reads in two different ways, and giving each its own inset is what keeps them from being\n    ! pinned to the panel's edges at one width and lost in it at another. A single padding on the body\n    ! would look identical today and would have to be unpicked the moment one of them wanted a wider\n    ! gutter than the other.\n    create SideBody in SideHost\n    set the style of SideBody to `flex:1;overflow-y:auto;box-sizing:border-box`\n    create SideHead in SideBody\n    set the style of SideHead to `display:block;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#8a8a80;margin:14px 16px 6px 16px`\n    create SideProse in SideBody\n    ! **The prose is the same reading as Blocks gives it** — Georgia on the pane's own paper — because a\n    ! doc block a reader has met in Blocks should not meet them differently here. The colour is set rather\n    ! than inherited: the panel is light now, but a component that relied on that would break the day the\n    ! panel's colour changed, and light-grey-on-light is the failure nobody notices until it ships.\n    !\n    ! `pre-wrap` rather than running the text together, because the doc-block convention writes one\n    ! paragraph to a line and those lines are the paragraphs: re-flowing them here would throw the shape\n    ! away, which is the one thing the convention is for.\n    set the style of SideProse to `font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;white-space:pre-wrap;color:#222;margin:0 16px 16px 16px`\n    create SideHint in SideBody\n    set the style of SideHint to `color:#8a8a80;font-size:12px;font-style:italic;margin:14px 16px`\n\n    ! **The status bar, along the foot of the panel.** It is the one place a reader can be told something\n    ! about the *mark* rather than about the block the mark's line belongs to — and along a single row of\n    ! dots the block is the same for every one of them, so the block is exactly what cannot tell them\n    ! apart. Created after the body so it sits below it, and given a top border rather than a second\n    ! background: the panel is one surface, and a reader should see the bar as the panel's own foot, not as\n    ! a third thing wedged into it.\n    create SideStatus in SideHost\n    set the style of SideStatus to `border-top:1px solid #ddd;padding:6px 16px;font-size:12px;color:#666;flex-shrink:0;min-height:18px`\n\n    ! The names first, then the strip built from them, so the strip and this list cannot disagree\n    ! about how many tabs there are.\n    put 1 into SideTabCount\n    set the elements of SideTabName to SideTabCount\n    index SideTabName to 0\n    put `Doc block` into SideTabName\n\n    put 0 into SideTabSel\n    set the elements of SideTab to SideTabCount\n    put 0 into SideTabI\n    while SideTabI is less than SideTabCount\n    begin\n        index SideTab to SideTabI\n        create SideTab in SideTabs\n        index SideTabName to SideTabI\n        put SideTabName into SideTabLabel\n        set the content of SideTab to SideTabLabel\n        set attribute `title` of SideTab to `Show the ` cat SideTabLabel cat ` for the line a mark names`\n        on click SideTab go to SideTabClick\n        add 1 to SideTabI\n    end\n    gosub to SideTabsDraw\n    return\n!! @hash 8ee3c368\n!! @verified 8ee3c368\n!!!\n!! The tab strip's one piece of state, which is which tab is up.\n!!\n!! The selected tab is the only one that differs, and it is redrawn from `SideTabSel` rather than remembered on the element: the same rule the TOC rows follow, so a tab cannot be lit and not be the one being shown.\nSideTabsDraw:\n    put 0 into SideTabI\n    while SideTabI is less than SideTabCount\n    begin\n        index SideTab to SideTabI\n        put `padding:4px 10px;font-size:11px;border-radius:3px 3px 0 0;cursor:pointer;background:` into SideTmp\n        if SideTabI is SideTabSel put SideTmp cat `#1e88e5;color:white` into SideTmp\n        else put SideTmp cat `#e4e2da;color:#555` into SideTmp\n        set the style of SideTab to SideTmp\n        add 1 to SideTabI\n    end\n    return\n\nSideTabClick:\n    put the index of SideTab into SideTabSel\n    gosub to SideTabsDraw\n    gosub to SideRender\n    stop\n!! @hash e76c1079\n!! @verified e76c1079\n!!!\n!! What the body shows, which is one of three things: a block, a line in no block, or nothing yet.\n!!\n!! **The middle case is a real answer rather than an error.** The lines before the first doc block and the code between two of them are outside the convention by design, and a reader who clicks a mark there should be told that rather than shown the last block somebody looked at — which is what a panel that only ever replaced its contents on a hit would do.\n!!\n!! The hint is what the panel says whenever there is nothing to show, and it is the only thing in this module that tells a reader what the panel is for.\nSideRender:\n    ! The status bar first, and unconditionally: it is about the mark, not about the block, so it says its\n    ! piece whether or not the line turned out to be in one.\n    gosub to SideStatusDraw\n    if SideKind is not `block` go to SideRenderHint\n    if SideFound is 0 go to SideRenderOutside\n    set the content of SideHead to SideTitle\n    set the content of SideProse to SideProseText\n    set the content of SideHint to ``\n    return\n\nSideRenderOutside:\n    set the content of SideHead to `Line ` cat SideLine\n    set the content of SideProse to ``\n    set the content of SideHint to `This line is outside any doc block.`\n    return\n\nSideRenderHint:\n    set the content of SideHead to ``\n    set the content of SideProse to ``\n    set the content of SideHint to `Click a mark in the picture to see the doc block its line belongs to.`\n    return\n!! @hash b04946e6\n!! @verified b04946e6\n!!!\n!! The status bar: what the script asked to have watched, at the mark it was watching.\n!!\n!! **It is the values and not the mark, and that is a division made on 2026-10-05.** It used to carry the mark's own figures too — \"line 254 visit 23 of 44\" — and Graham moved them out: they are read while pointing at a dot, and on a big screen the foot of this panel is the other side of the room from the picture, so the pane says them itself, one line under its own status line, where the eye already is. What is left here is the other half of the same message: the numbers the script asked to have shown by writing `@show`, which belong beside the block because they are about the work the line did rather than about which dot was clicked.\n!!\n!! **The figures still decide whether the bar is drawn at all.** A press that named no mark leaves an empty bar, and so does a mark whose script asked to watch nothing — the panel is not the place for a line that says only that there is nothing to say.\nSideStatusDraw:\n    if SideVisit is less than 1\n    begin\n        set the content of SideStatus to ``\n        return\n    end\n    put `` into SideStatusText\n    ! **And the values, which is what a script's `@show` was for.** This is where the question that made\n    ! somebody write the attribute is answered: the bar is read at the same moment as the picture, so the\n    ! number and the run it belongs to arrive together without a second place to look.\n    !\n    ! **In name order**, which is what `the json keys of` gives and the reason to use it here: two visits\n    ! compared across a run read the same way round, and a reader scanning a column of status lines is\n    ! comparing them. The script's own order (`@show N, Total`) would put the names in whatever order each\n    ! line happened to be written in, which is a difference nobody asked to see.\n    if SideValues is not empty\n    begin\n        put the json keys of SideValues into SideValueKeys\n        put `` into SideValueText\n        put 0 into SideValueI\n        ! **`the json count of` and `element N of`, because these keys are one json list and one value.** A\n        ! variable is a holder with slots: `index X to N` picks a slot, and a *json list* is a single value\n        ! occupying a single slot — so `index Keys to 0` with `put Keys into Name` hands back the whole list\n        ! (correctly), and the elements of that list are reached with `element N of`. `split` is the other way\n        ! round: it fills a slot per line, which is why this editor's buffer walk uses `index` and `has\n        ! element` on it. Both are index-based and neither replaces the other; using the wrong one prints a\n        ! list where a value belongs.\n        while SideValueI is less than the json count of SideValueKeys\n        begin\n            put element SideValueI of SideValueKeys into SideValueName\n            if SideValueText is not empty put SideValueText cat `  ` into SideValueText\n            put SideValueText cat SideValueName cat `=` cat property SideValueName of SideValues into SideValueText\n            add 1 to SideValueI\n        end\n        if SideStatusText is not empty put SideStatusText cat `   ` into SideStatusText\n        put SideStatusText cat SideValueText into SideStatusText\n    end\n    set the content of SideStatus to SideStatusText\n    return\n!! @hash b74412d4\n!! @verified 591e92f4\n!!!\n!! The boundary: one message in, and the module parks until the next one.\n!!\n!! `release parent` in the plumbing above is what lets the editor's `run` return, and this is what carries on from there: the panel is built on the first message rather than at load, because a module that is loaded and never opened should cost nothing but its attach.\n!!\n!! The two kinds are told apart by the message's own `kind` rather than by which of its properties are present, so a field can be added to either without the other being read wrongly.\nSideMessage:\n    put the message into SideRun\n    put property `kind` of SideRun into SideKind\n    gosub to SideBuild\n    if SideKind is `block` go to SideBlockArrived\n    if property `open` of SideRun is 0 go to SideClosed\n    put property `path` of SideRun into SidePath\n    ! A new run clears whatever the last click left up, because the block it showed belonged to another\n    ! script — the picture has been replaced, and so has everything the sidebar was saying about it. The\n    ! mark's figures go with it: they counted a line in a run that is no longer on screen.\n    put 0 into SideVisit\n    put json of `{}` into SideValues\n    gosub to SideRender\n    stop\n\nSideBlockArrived:\n    put property `line` of SideRun into SideLine\n    put property `visit` of SideRun into SideVisit\n    ! The values the script asked to have watched, if it asked for any. Passed through, not interpreted:\n    ! what `@show Total` means was settled by the recorder that read it.\n    put property `values` of SideRun into SideValues\n    put property `found` of SideRun into SideFound\n    put property `title` of SideRun into SideTitle\n    put property `prose` of SideRun into SideProseText\n    ! Said here as well as in the editor, so that the two halves of the relay can be told apart when only\n    ! one of them is working: this line means the panel was reached, and its absence means the message\n    ! never crossed. It names its own subject so a copied line still makes sense on its own.\n    log `sidebar: showing line ` cat SideLine cat ` visit ` cat SideVisit cat ` found=` cat SideFound cat ` title=` cat SideTitle\n    gosub to SideRender\n    stop\n\n!   The pane is off screen. Nothing is said and nothing is cleared: the panel is hidden with the rest\n!   of the Graph area, so it will be showing the same block when the pane comes back, which is what a\n!   reader returning to the same run expects.\nSideClosed:\n    stop\n!! @hash 5f3ab6e1\n!! @verified cd2f39fb\n!!!\n"};
